ssion and investigation of crimes.", "madsrdf:useFor": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026204"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008025530"}]}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2011-05-05T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-02T13:38:11"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Crime films"}}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb5", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb6"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doctor Mabuse films"}}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb6", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doctor Mabuse films"}}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 65-69 (Crime film; Crime drama; Criminal film: crime films are one of the most important genres of the cinema. Crime is, however, a vast subject and crime film too broad a label embracing many subgenres ... Some of these subgenres have developed ... into full genres ... others are in the process of doing so ... Caper film, Conspiracy film, Expos\u00e9 film, Film noir, Gangster film, Giallo, Lawyer film, Motorcycle movie, Mystery film, Police film, Prison film, Private detective film, Psychopathic thriller, Social consciousness film, Spy film, Thriller, Vice film, Vigilante film, Whodunit, Juvenile delinquent films)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Lopez, D. Films by genre, 1993:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Bonnie and Clyde, 1967.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 1-17 (The crime film is a stronger genre ... than the criminal subgenres ... not only because its scope is by definition broader than theirs, but because ... the problem that defines it as a genre places the film noir and the gangster film in a more sharply illuminated context by showing that each of those is a part of a coherent larger project ... Crime films present as their defining subject a crime culture that depends on normalizing the unspeakable, a place where crime is both shockingly disruptive and completely normal ... the genre of crime films includes all films that focus on any of the three parties to a crime--criminal, victim, avenger, while exploring the parties links to the other two)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Leitch, T. Crime films, 2002:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 39 (Crime films and programs: use for fictional genre films and programs which focus on the commission and investigation of crime. NT: Caper films and programs, Detective films and programs, Film noir, Gangster films and programs, Legal films and programs, Mysteries, Police films and programs, Prison films and programs)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Yee, M.M. Moving image materials, 1988:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Crime: fictional work depicting a narrative descent into the underworld of syndicates, criminals, and their lawless activities, such as the drug trade, or bootlegging during the Prohibition era. The plot turns on such questions as how a criminal will be caught or who stole the object of value ... Use more specific types of the crime formula when possible, such as Caper, Film Noir, Gangster, Mystery, Police, or Thriller, if one of those aspects is the primary situation or character. For true crime, use Reality-based)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Moving image genre-form guide online, February 11, 2008:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Criminal films"}}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Criminal films"}}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doctor Mabuse films"}}, {"@id": "_:nfc3f967e311d427bb69fc67771f3c1edb17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doctor Mabuse films"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026177"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026178", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCultural television programs", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2011026178", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab1"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cultural television programs"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026673"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026189"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab5"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab6"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab7"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab9"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:useFor": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2007025574"}}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2011-05-05T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-23T16:15:48"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cultural television programs"}}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. ix (arts programming) p. v (dance, opera and musical theater, performing arts, music, drama, folk art, artists, media art)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The arts on television, 1976-1990 : fifteen years of cultural programming, 1991:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Moving image genre-form guide online, November 15, 2007", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 365 (cultural television programming; elite or high culture generally associated with great works of art or literature. Program content tends to focus on performances whether they be theatrical, dance, or musical)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "TV genres, 1985:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(hdg.: Cultural television programs)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, May 30, 2008", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Arts television programs"}}, {"@id": "_:n86b92c372f864bfca81a242d52d7a92ab10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Arts television programs"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026178"}
