"2019-04-09T11:07:24"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/nanbg"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Video installations (Art)"}}, {"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a collection of music videos, short films, video installations, and commercials)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: The work of director Chris Cunningham [VR] 2003", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "disc 2 (special features include: \"Listen to Britain,\" a 2001 video-installation piece by English artist Victor Burgin inspired by the film of the same name with an introduction by Burgin (7 min.))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "A Canterbury tale [VR] 2006:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(video installations)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford art online, Feb. 20, 2009", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(media installation art; video installations; screen-reliant installation art)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Mondloch, K. Screens, 2010", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Video installation (Video installation is a contemporary art method that combines video technology with installation art. It is an art form that utilizes all aspects of its surrounding environment as a vehicle of affecting the audience) Video art (Video art came into existence during the 1960s and 1970s, is still widely practiced and has given rise to the widespread use of video installations)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Mar. 19, 2009:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Taves, B. The moving image genre-form guide online, Mar. 20, 2009", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Screen-reliant installations (Art)"}}, {"@id": "_:n4d22824d56df4a1db908e44588bd2300b12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Screen-reliant installations (Art)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026722"}
