f:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The Rose Parade is broadcast live in the US beginning at 8:00 a.m. PST)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Pasadena Tournament of Roses WWW site, viewed Jan. 31, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb9ece050dee049a8b6e952bb69a69616b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 00570550: 111th annual Tournament of Roses Parade.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb9ece050dee049a8b6e952bb69a69616b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb9ece050dee049a8b6e952bb69a69616b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Parades, Televised"}}, {"@id": "_:nb9ece050dee049a8b6e952bb69a69616b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Parades, Televised"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2012025006"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2012025007", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aUpside-down maps", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2012025007", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b1"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Upside-down maps"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026387"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b5"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b6"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b7"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b8"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b9"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b10"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b11"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b12"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b13"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b16"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b19"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Maps in which the orientation has been reversed and south appears at the top."}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2012-02-13T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-07T16:12:53"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Upside-down maps"}}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "home page (south-up maps) Whats up? South! world map (south-at-the-top map)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "ODT Maps, Inc. website, Oct. 10, 2011:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(upsidedown maps; south up maps; McArthur's Universal Corrective Map of the World has a fabulous history, made by an Australian who was tormented for coming from the \"bottom of the world\". It was the first modern south-up map, published in 1979)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The upsidedown map page, via WWW, Oct. 10, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "2002628168: Magellan Geographix (Firm). Upside down map of California, c1997.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(2,390 results for \"reversed maps\"; 5 results for \"south-at-the-top maps\"; 2,360 results for \"south-up maps\"; 40,200 results for \"upside-down maps\"; 590 results for \"upsidedown maps\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Google search, Oct. 10, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Oriented with north to the bottom)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Hema Maps (Firm). Upside down world map, c2004", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Oriented with north to the bottom)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "94682150: MacArthur, S. McArthur's universal corrective map of the world, c1979", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Reversed map (A reversed map, also known as an Upside-Down map or South-Up map, is a map where south is up, north is down, east is left and west is right. Thus the Southern Hemisphere at the top of the map instead of the bottom. These maps are just as accurate as traditionally oriented maps, because the position of North at the top of maps is arbitrary. Such maps have been made in several cultures and time periods. The convention that North is at the top (and East at the right) on most modern maps was established by the astronomer Ptolemy and was adopted by other cartographers. In modern times, reversed maps are made as a learning device or to illustrate Northern Hemisphere bias. Different from simply turning a north-up map upside down, a reversed map has the text oriented to be read with south up.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Oct. 10, 2011:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "97682945: Imprint Graphic Deisgn. Upside down world map, c1996.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "South-up maps"}}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "South-up maps"}}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Upsidedown maps"}}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Upsidedown maps"}}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Reversed maps"}}, {"@id": "_:n6cd364e0d1e942c7aafef0a49cadf788b20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Reversed maps"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2012025007"}
