 "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Arthurian romances: Used for fictional works about King Arthur and his Knights of the Round Table.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Worldcat.org/genres, October 30, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n98c4cb5d21b4441b8612be47f6935a75b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Arthurian literature: a large body of writings in various languages in the 12 and 13th centuries and thereafter, recounting legends of King Arthur, his sword Excalibur, his queen Guinevere, and his various knights at the court of Camelot. The historical Arthur, if he existed, seems to have been some kind of chieftain in 6th century Wales. These legends have been adapted and retold in various forms and languages over the centuries. See also Matter of Britain) Matter of Britain (The legends of King Arthur and his Knights of the Round Table, which form the subject-matter for a number of medieval romances--usually known as Arthurian romances. These are often distinguished from the matter of France (i.e. legends of Charlemagne and his knights) or the matter of Rome (classical Roman legends or myths))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Baldick, C. Oxford dictionary of literary terms, c2008", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n98c4cb5d21b4441b8612be47f6935a75b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n98c4cb5d21b4441b8612be47f6935a75b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Matter of Britain (Arthurian romances)"}}, {"@id": "_:n98c4cb5d21b4441b8612be47f6935a75b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Matter of Britain (Arthurian romances)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026227"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026228", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aAtellanae", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026228", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab1"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Atellanae"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026335"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab5"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab6"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab8"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab11"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab14"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab17"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Farcical, bawdy pantomimes from Ancient Rome in which stock characters were represented by masks."}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T17:40:05"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Atellanae"}}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Atellanae)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 21, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Atellan fables, see fabula; fabula: The Latin fabulae were forms of drama among which we may distinguish the following: (a) Fabula Atellana, so called after the Oscan town of Atella. A kind of southern Italian farce popular in Rome until the period of Augustus (63 BC-AD 14). They were bawdy pantomimes with stock characters who were represented by masks)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cuddon, J.A. A dictionary of literary terms and literary theory, 1998", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(fabula: In Latin literature, fabula (plural -lae) is the general name for various kinds of play, of which the most significant genres are fabula Atellana or Atellan farce, and fabula palliata or Roman new comedy)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Baldick, C. The Oxford dict. of literary terms, 2008", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Fabulae Atellanae"}}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Fabulae Atellanae"}}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Fables, Atellan"}}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Fables, Atellan"}}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Atellan fables"}}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Atellan fables"}}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Atellan farces"}}, {"@id": "_:ndd60e67cbfd64f7187d645e46a67feeab18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Atellan farces"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026228"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026229", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aAutobiographical comics", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026229", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b1"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Autobiographical comics"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026047"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026244"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b5"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b6"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b7"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b8"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b9"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b10"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b11"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b12"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b13"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b16"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b19"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2018-03-13T13:23:02"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Autobiographical comics"}}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 4 of cover (graphic memoir)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Lee, Stan. Amazing, fantastic, incredible : a marvelous memoir, 2015:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "t.p. (autobio comics by Melanie Gillman)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Gillman, Melanie. Nonbinary, 2015:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(autobiographical comics: 33,800 hits; autobiographic comics: 2,780 hits (with \"did you mean autobiographical comics); comics autobiographies: 944 hits)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Google, March 6, 2015", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Nonfiction comics of note, continued (Autobiographical comics; comics autobiographies)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "English 333--Comics & graphic novels blog, viewed Dec. 11, 2012:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Autobiographical comic books, strips, etc. BT Biographical comic books, strips, etc.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 21, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Autobiographical comics (Autobiographical comics (often referred to in the comics field as simply autobio) are autobiography in the form of comic books or comic strips. The form first became popular in the underground comics movement and has since become more widespread; graphic memoir)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Dec. 28, 2015:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Graphic memoirs are comics or sequential art that tell an autobiographical or semi-autobiographical story; a sub-genre of graphic novels and comics in general; most graphic memoirs do not cover the same wide scope a print memoir would. Typically what they lack in breath, the make up for in depth)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Genre guide: graphic memoirs, via YALSA's The hub blog, Feb. 16, 2015, viewed on Dec. 28, 2015", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(comics autobiographies have changed the field of contemporary narrative)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Graphic women : life narrative and contemporary comics, 2011, via Upcoming events & things to do website, viewed on Dec. 11, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Autobiographic comics"}}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Autobiographic comics"}}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Autobio comics"}}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Autobio comics"}}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Graphic memoirs"}}, {"@id": "_:nce57357fc2304223a5e3c3569da5ac14b20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Graphic memoirs"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026229"}
