{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026248", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aBlack humor", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026248", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb1"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Black humor"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026110"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026415"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb5"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb6"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb8"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb11"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb14"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb17"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb20"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb23"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Literary works that derive humor from suffering or the absurdity of existence in a bitter, ironic, morbid, or grimly satiric way."}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2016-02-04T15:14:54"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Black humor"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(black comedy. A kind of drama (or, by extension, a non-dramatic work) in which disturbing or sinister subjects like death, disease, or warfare are treated with bitter amusement, usually in a manner calculated to offend and shock. Prominent in the theatre of the absurd, black comedy is also a feature of Joe Orton's Loot (1965). A similar black humour is strongly evident in modern American fiction from Nathaniel West's A Cool Million (1934) to Joseph Heller's Catch-22 (1961) and Kurt Vonnegut's Slaughterhouse-Five (1969))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Baldick, C. The Oxford dictionary of literary terms, 2008", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Black humor (Literature). Use for works characterized by a desperate, sardonic humor intended to induce laughter as the appropriate response to the apparent meaninglessness and absurdity of existence. UF Black comedy (Literature); Dark comedy (Literature); Dark humor (Literature); Gallows humor)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "GSAFD, 2000", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(black comedy. Black comedy is a form of drama which displays a marked disillusionment and cynicism. It shows human beings without convictions and with little hope, regulated by fate or fortune or incomprehensible powers. In fact, human beings in an 'absurd' predicament. At its darkest such comedy is pervaded by a kind of sour despair: we can't do anything so we may as well laugh. The wit is mordant and the humour sardonic. In other forms of literature 'black comedy' and 'black humour' (e.g. the 'sick joke') have become more and more noticeable in the 20th c. It has been remarked that such comedy is particularly prominent in the so-called 'literature of the absurd')"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cuddon, J.A. A dictionary of literary terms and literary theory, 1998", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comedies, Black"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comedies, Black"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Gallows humor"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Gallows humor"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dark humor"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dark humor"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dark comedies"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dark comedies"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comedies, Dark"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comedies, Dark"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb23", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb24"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Black comedies"}}, {"@id": "_:nf7258765d05340e296a02d9ae1200d5eb24", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Black comedies"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026248"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026249", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aBlank verse", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026249", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb1"}, {"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Blank verse"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026481"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb5"}, {"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Poems having regular meter but no rhyme."}, {"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T14:58:47"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Blank verse"}}, {"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Blank verse)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 21, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nec91086f6d0d487a826c602c37d5d52fb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Blank verse: Unrhymed but otherwise regular verse, usually iambic pentameter. The form, generally accepted as the best adapted to dramatic verse in English, is commonly used for long poems whether dramatic, philosophic, or narrative)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Harmon, W. A handbook to literature, c2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026249"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026250", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aBlues poetry", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026250", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b1"}, {"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Blues poetry"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026481"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b5"}, {"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Poetry stemming from the musical tradition of the blues that generally portray struggle, despair, helplessness and loss."}, {"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T14:58:59"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Blues poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Poetic Form: Blues Poem--one of the most popular forms of American poetry, the blues poem stems from the African American oral tradition and the musical tradition of the blues. A blues poem typically takes on themes such as struggle, despair, and sex. It often (but not necessarily) follows a form, in which a statement is made in the first line, a variation is given in the second line, and an ironic alternative is declared in the third line)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Poets.org, viewed Dec. 26, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4fdd8eb583ff4a4e97bdae17c94e7cb1b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Blues Poem. Blues poetry came from the musical form known as \"the blues.\" There are two types of blues poems. The first type has no particular form, but has the content typical of the blues. The second type of blues poem is one that has blues content and the structure of the old blues songs. Blues songs are about hopelessness, grief, and loss.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Teachers & Writers handbook of poetic forms, c2000", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026250"}
