50"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026063"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026069"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026004"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026083"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb5"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb6"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb7"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb8"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb9"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb11"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb14"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb17"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb20"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb23"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb26"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb29"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb32"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb35"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb38"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb41"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb44"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Narrative works that employ sequential art, and often prose, to tell a story. For humorous images that use wit and satire to comment on contemporary events, social habits, politics, or other subjects, see [Cartoons (Humor).]"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2021-09-10T10:13:03"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comics (Graphic works)"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 118 (comic strip: sequence of cartoons that relates a narrative story. The key here is that drawings are in a specific sequence that tells a story; comic book: sequences of comic strips, often relating a single story, bound as a magazine. Although a comic book contains collections of comic strips, this does not constitute a graphic novel. A comic book is properly categorized as a serial; graphic novel: a book presenting a fictional story told in a comic-strip format; expresses a single story through comic art; for practical purposes, it is common to refer to an original graphic novel with chapters and a bound collection with episodes as graphic novels)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Miller, S. Developing and promoting graphic novel collections, c2005:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(comic noun 2. a periodical containing comic strips, intended chiefly for children; (comics) North American comic strips; comix: comic books and comic strips, especially ones written for adults or of an underground or alternative nature. Origin: alteration of comics; comic strip: a sequence of drawings in boxes that tell an amusing story, typically printed in a newspaper or magazine)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford dictionaries website, Dec. 6, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Encyclopedia of American comics, 1990.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 17 (Comic book: used to describe both the medium itself and the periodical form. As a result, all graphic novels are comic books, but not all comic books are graphic novels. Comic: Used interchangeably with \"comic book.\" Graphic novel: Used to describe the specific format of a comic book that has greater production values and longer narrative. Can also describe the trade paperback format)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Lyga, A.A.W. Graphic novels in your media center, 2004:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Carrier, D. The aesthetics of comics, 2000.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comic book (A comic book or comicbook, also called comic paper or comic magazine (often shortened to simply comic or comics) is a magazine made up of \"comics\"--narrative artwork in the form of separate panels that represent individual scenes, often accompanied by dialog (usually in word balloons, emblematic of the comic book art form) as well as including brief descriptive prose.) Comics (A comic, often known collectively as comics, is a hybrid medium in which illustrations are strongly blended with other types of communicative representations, usually written words (see comics vocabulary), in order to convey information or narrative, thus seeking synergy by using both visual and verbal (or otherwise communicative) elements in interaction; comic strips; comic books) Comic strip (A comic strip is a sequence of drawings arranged in interrelated panels to display brief humor or form a narrative, often serialized, with text in balloons and captions. Traditionally, throughout the 20th century and into the 21st, these were published in newspapers, with horizontal strips printed in black-and-white in daily newspapers, while Sunday newspapers offered longer sequences in special color comics sections; cartoonist Will Eisner has suggested that 'sequential art' would be a better name)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Dec. 5, 2012:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Papers, Comic"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Papers, Comic"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Serial picture books"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Serial picture books"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comix"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comix"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comic papers"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comic papers"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb23", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb24"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Art, Sequential"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb24", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Art, Sequential"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb26", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb27"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comic books, strips, etc."}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb27", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comic books, strips, etc."}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb29", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb30"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comic strips"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb30", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comic strips"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb32", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb33"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comicbooks"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb33", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comicbooks"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb35", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb36"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Funnies (Comics)"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb36", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Funnies (Comics)"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb38", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb39"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sequential art"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb39", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sequential art"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb41", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb42"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Magazines, Comic"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb42", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Magazines, Comic"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb44", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb45"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comic magazines"}}, {"@id": "_:n7509550d06774e77b6ecc5ff0873e3dbb45", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Comic magazines"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026266"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026267", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCommedia dell'arte", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026267", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b1"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia dell'arte"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026264"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b5"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b6"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b7"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b8"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b9"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b11"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b14"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b17"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b20"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b23"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b26"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b29"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b32"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b35"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b38"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Scenarios, scripts, and lazzi of comedies popular in the 16th-18th centuries that were improvised by professional actors within a conventional framework of masked stock characters and rehearsed comic routines."}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T12:55:39"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia dell'arte"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(commedia dell'arte. The Italian term for 'professional comedy', a form of improvised comic performance popular between the 16th and 18th centuries in Italy, France, and elsewhere in Europe, acted in masks by travelling companies of professional actors each of whom specialized in a stock character)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Baldick, C. The Oxford dict. of literary terms, 2008", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "glossary (Commedia dell'arte: Form of comic theater, originating in Italy in the sixteenth century, in which dialogue was improvised around a loose scenario involving a set of stock characters, each with a distinctive costume and a traditional name.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wilson, E. The theater experience, c2004, via McGraw-Hill Higher Education online learning center, Nov. 14, 2012:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Gordon, M. Lazzi : the comic routines of the Commedia dell'Arte, 1983.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(commedia dell'arte, the name usually given to the popular Italian improvised comedy first recorded in 1545, which flourished from the 16th to the early 18th centuries. Other names for it: commedia a soggetto; all'improvviso; dei zanni; dei maschere; all'italiana. Dell'arte, the only phrase to survive in general use, means roughly 'of the profession', the actors being trained professionals. To distinguish it from the popular theatre, the written Italian drama of this time was known as commedia erudita. Actors made up their speeches as they went along; comic servants provided most of the humour; most of the actors wore masks; acted in accordance with a scenario or pre-arranged synopsis)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford companion to the theatre, 1983", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. v (at no time was Commedia dell'Arte totally spontaneous. The improvisation was laced with memorised speeches, rhymed couplets, witty sallies, exit lines, and above all, the rehearsed comic business we call Lazzi) p. 209 (a Lazzo might be said to have been a piece of rehearsed comic business inserted into the improvised drama, usually at a moment when the audience's attention was flagging. Here, I use the term in a broader sense to mean any bit of stage business, from a single action or word, to a whole scene, which is devised and rehearsed beforehand and inserted into an improvised or scripted production, and usually called for by the dictates of the scenario.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grantham, B. Commedia plays : scenarios scripts lazzi, 2006:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "The commedia dell'arte in Naples : a bilingual edition of the 176 Casamarciano scenarios, 2001.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia dei zanni"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia dei zanni"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia a soggetto"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia a soggetto"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia all'improviso"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia all'improviso"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Arte, Commedia dell'"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Arte, Commedia dell'"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b23", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b24"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia dei maschere"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b24", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia dei maschere"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b26", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b27"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Soggetto, Commedia a"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b27", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Soggetto, Commedia a"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b29", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b30"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia all'italiana"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b30", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commedia all'italiana"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b32", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b33"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Zanni, Commedia dei"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b33", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Zanni, Commedia dei"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b35", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b36"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Italiana, Commedia all'"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b36", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Italiana, Commedia all'"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b38", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b39"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Maschere, Commedia dei"}}, {"@id": "_:nf9f430c2188546018432d59d1d397366b39", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Maschere, Commedia dei"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026267"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026268", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCommendatory poetry", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026268", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb1"}, {"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commendatory poetry"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb3"}]}, "madsrdf:exampleNote": "Note under [Laudatory poetry]", "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026460"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb5"}, {"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Poems that generally appear in the preliminaries of books and praise the author and/or the work. For poems that praise a person, place, event, or thing see [Laudatory poetry.]"}, {"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-01T11:15:42"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commendatory poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Commendatory poems. Use for poems, generally appearing in the preliminaries of books, written in praise of the author and the work. UF Commendatory verse. BT Poems)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Genre terms : a thesaurus for use in rare book and special collections cataloging, via WWW, Aug. 12, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5b52ca8d50f5456c96846aef54dfca7eb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 1 (Commendatory poems, in which friends and well-wishers testify to the merits of favored authors, are a familiar feature of the preliminary leaves of English books during the two centuries that span the Renaissance. Seldom of intrinsic value as poetry, these verses are commonly searched for evidence on conditions of publication or for biographical clues on the contributor or his subject.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Williams, F.B. Commendatory verses : the rise of the art of puffing, in Studies in bibliography, v. 19 (1966), viewed online via JSTOR, Jan. 8, 2013:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026268"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026269", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aComputer poetry", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026269", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b1"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer poetry"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026326"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026018"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b5"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b6"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b7"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b8"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b9"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b10"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b11"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b12"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b13"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b16"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b19"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b22"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b25"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b28"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b31"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Poems generated or manipulated by computer algorithm and arranged as a sequence of words, signs, or symbols."}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-18T07:01:48"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Computer poetry: Is generated by computer algorithm, arranged as a sequence of words, or signs and symbols according to a programming code)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Notes on digital poetry from Literature in a wired world, Engl278W, spring 2011, University of Maryland, viewed Jan. 8, 2013", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Computational poetry: A term used by theorists to describe poems generated by instructions that can be formalized as procedural or algorithmic sequences. Whether executed by hand, in print, or on a computer, the defining features of such poems include a program that precedes and determines the poem, a relation between form and content that distinguishes the poem from concepts of poetry that are either organic or formalist, and a definition of the authorial function that differentiates the poem from conventional romantic notions about poetry)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Princeton encyclopedia of poetry and poetics, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "t.p. (poems by code-davinci-002)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "I am code : an artificial intelligence speaks, 2023:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(AI-generated poetry)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://aitidings.com/artificial-intelligence-generated-poetry)"}, "Sharlow, Bill. Artificial intelligence-generated poetry, via AI tidings website, viewed June 13, 2024"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Interactive poetry: Poems that are produced on and for computers having alternative elements which allow \"readers\" to invent their own \"virtual\" poetry within the boundaries of the computer program in use. Also called computer poetry and virtual poetry)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Turco, L.P. The book of forms, c2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Computer poems: Poets initially used computer programs to synthesize a database and a series of instructions to establish a work's content and shape. Labeled by its authors as \"Computer Poetry\" and \"computer-poems\" (among other terms), these works are generated by computer algorithm, arranged as a sequence of words, or signs and symbols according to a programming code. All works of text-generation, or archetypal computer poetry, can be seen as performing some type of permutation in that they transform or reorder one set of base texts or language (i.e., word lists, syllables, or pre-existing texts) into another form)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "A companion to digital literary studies, 2008, viewed Jan. 8, 2013", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(AI-generated poetry; AI poetry \"generators\" are already writing poetry)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Edwards, Lynnell. Our AI moment in poetry, via Good River review website, Aug. 17, 2023, viewed June 13, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Artificial intelligence (AI); AI programs are beginning to write poetry; AI and computer-generated poetry; AI poets; It's easy to dismiss AI poetry on the grounds that it has so far failed to produce any good poems; AI poems)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Holyoak, Keith. Can AI write authentic poetry?, via The MIT Press reader website, viewed June 13, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artificial intelligence-generated poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artificial intelligence-generated poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "AI poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "AI poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Virtual poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Virtual poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b22", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b23"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computational poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b23", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computational poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b25", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b26"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Interactive poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b26", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Interactive poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b28", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b29"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer-generated poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b29", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer-generated poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b31", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b32"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "AI-generated poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nf93fcccaef734b269a2e94b483dbcec6b32", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "AI-generated poetry"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026269"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026270", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aConcrete poetry", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026270", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b1"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Concrete poetry"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b3"}]}, "madsrdf:exampleNote": "Note under [Pattern poetry]", "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026326"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026588"}], "madsrdf:hasReciprocalAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026478"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b5"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b6"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b7"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b8"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b9"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b10"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Poems in which graphic effects created by typography replace the use of conventional verse forms, so that the typographical arrangement of text is integral to the experience and meaning of the work. For poems that are arranged to form a recognizable shape that generally illustrates the poem's theme see [Pattern poetry.]"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-01T11:27:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Concrete poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Concrete poetry: Poetry that exploits the graphic, visual aspect of writing: a specialized application of what Aristotle called opsis (\"spectacle\") and Pound \"phanopoeia.\" A concrete poem is one that is also a work of graphic art; the painter Paul Klee produced some early examples)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Harmon, W. A handbook to literature, c2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(concrete poetry: 20th-century form of poetry in which graphic effects created by words and letters replaces the use of conventional verse forms. It flourished in particular in the 1950s and 1960s, though one of the earliest (and most inventive) poets to use concrete poetry was the French writer Guillaume Apollinaire, whose 1918 poem Il pleut/It rains consists of words arranged in long vertical lines suggesting rain. Inspired in part by Dada and surrealism, concrete poetry was an attempt both to escape what were seen as the constraints of conventional verse, and also to bring a new vitality and range to poetry by fusing words and images. Though closely related to pattern poetry, concrete poetry differs in the importance it attaches to the visual impact of the poem. A pattern poem is a complete and independent text that has been arranged to form a recognizable shape. A concrete poem, by contrast, is generally one in which the visual element (which is often much more than a shape formed by the lines) is an integral part of the experience of the poem: the text is not meant to be read independently of the form in which it is embodied.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hutchinson encyclopedia, 2013", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(concrete poetry: An experimental form of poetry, flourishing in the 1960s, which concentrated on isolated and particular aspects of visual, phonetic or kinetic structure, abandoning normal forms of meaning for those disclosed at or below the level of the single word. Different accounts of concrete poetry have either stressed its novel and experimental nature, making links with structuralism and semiotics,or else have claimed a long tradition stemming from older texts whose visual aspects contributed to their meaning.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cambridge guide to literature in English, 2000", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Concrete poetry/verse: A recent development of the altar poem and the Carmen figuratum. The object is to present each poem as a different shape. It is thus a matter of pictorial typography which produces \"visual poetry.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cuddon, J.A. A dictionary of literary terms and literary theory, 1991", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Carmen figuratum: Latin: \"a shaped poem,\" the verses of which are so arranged that they form a design on the page. When the design is an object, such as a cross or an altar, it is usually the theme of the poem. In recent years, the vogue of shaped verse, called \"concrete poetry,\" has become worldwide phenomenon. Often poems consist of single letters, words, or phrases in a variety of type styles and colors, all of which challenge the reader to perceive the shape and the theme)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Beckson, K. Literary terms, 1989", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nadf85f22a2ee46bfad8f855058456f51b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Concrete poetry: Poetry in which the visual arrangement of words or letters suggests something about the subject of the poem. Many concrete poems consist of a single word--for example, the word shrink printed with gradually smaller letters)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Morner, K. NTC's dictionary of literary terms, c1991", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026270"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026271", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aConfessional fiction", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026271", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab1"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Confessional fiction"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026339"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab5"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab6"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab7"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab9"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Fiction in which the main character makes a first-person confession to crimes, transgressions, or sins."}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T12:58:49"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Confessional fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Confessional novel: A work of fiction whose main reason being is to allow the first-person protagonists to confess to the reader a crime, transgression, or sin, or a series of them--usually something potentially shocking or embarrassing. The protagonist is not necessarily a stand-in for the author. On the other hand, the protagonist might have a close relationship with the author.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Henry, L. The fiction dictionary, c1995", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Confession stories. UF Confessional stories)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 22, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Confessional novel: a rather misleading and flexible term which suggests an 'autobiographical' type of fiction, written in the first person, and which, on the face of it, is a self-revelation. On the other hand it may not be, although it looks like it. The author may be merely assuming the role of another character.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cuddon, J. A dictionary of literary terms and literary theory, c1998", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(In literature, confessional writing is a first-person style that is often presented as an ongoing diary or letters, distinguished by revelations of a person's heart and darker motivations. Fictionally, the confessional story is a story written, in the first person, about emotionally fraught and morally charged situations in which a fictional character is caught. These stories may be anything from thinly veiled recounting of the writer's life, to completely fictional works.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Nov. 9, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Confession fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n189fc7304a124b7ca2a4be786eb3f29ab10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Confession fiction"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026271"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026272", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aConfessional poetry", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026272", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b1"}, {"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Confessional poetry"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026232"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b5"}, {"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Poems that frankly reveal private and intimate aspects of the poet's life."}, {"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T12:57:34"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Confessional poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Confessional poetry. Emerging in the 1950s and 1960s, confessional poetry aims for a frank exposure of the poet's personal life. More concerned with the private aspects of life (along with the guilts and embarrassments) than is standard autobiography, confessional poetry reveals aspects of experience kept hidden in normal social intercourse; confessional poetry intensifies the reading process, drawing readers into private dramas and clouding their moral and aesthetic judgment with uncomfortable disclosures--the suicide of a father, nursing one's infant, etc.; even if they appreciate the artistry of confessional poems, readers may not share the poets' absorption in their own experience.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Kennedy, X.J. The Longman dictionary of literary terms, c2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8c915688753a4065a69cb8bc182b39b8b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Confessional Poetry: designates a type of narrative and lyric verse that deals with the facts and intimate mental and physical experiences of the poet's own life. Confessional poetry was written in rebellion against the demand for impersonality by T.S. Eliot and the New Critics. It differs in its secular subject matter from religious confessions in the lineage of Augustine's Confessions (c. 400 A.D.). It differs also from poems of the Romantic Period representing the poet's own circumstances, experiences and feelings ... in the candor and sometimes shocking detail with which the poet reveals private or clinical matters about himself or herself, including sexual experiences, mental anguish and illness, experiments with drugs, and suicidal impulses. Confessional poems were written by Allen Ginsberg, Sylvia Plath, Anne Sexton, John Berryman, and other American poets.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Abrams, M. A glossary of literary terms, c1999", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026272"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026273", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCounting-out rhymes", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026273", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb1"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Counting-out rhymes"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026459"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb5"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb6"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb8"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb11"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb14"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Rhymes that are traditionally used to eliminate a player in a child's game."}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T12:57:17"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Counting-out rhymes"}}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 22, 2014 (Counting-out rhymes. UF Counting rhymes; Number rhymes)", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(counting-out rhyme, gibberish formula used by children, usually as a preliminary to games in which one child must be chosen to take the undesirable role designated as \"It\" in the United States, \"It\" or \"He\" in Britain, and \"wolf,\" \"devil,\" or \"leper\" in some other countries. Among the most popular rhymes are those having the refrain \"Eeny, meeny, miny, mo.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Britannica academic edition online, Jan. 9, 2013", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Counting-out rhymes. Here are entered rhymes traditionally used to count out or eliminate a player in a child's game.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Children's Subject Headings online, Jan. 9, 2013", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Counting-out poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Counting-out poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Counting rhymes"}}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Counting rhymes"}}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Number rhymes"}}, {"@id": "_:nfb352cad0c7644e6ba482cf2f003a8cdb15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Number rhymes"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026273"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026274", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCut-ups (Literature)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026274", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b1"}, {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cut-ups (Literature)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026415"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b5"}, {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b7"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Literary texts composed by the cutting up and rearranging of an existing text."}, {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T13:00:32"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cut-ups (Literature)"}}, {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Cut-ups (Literary form). UF Cutups (Literary form))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 17, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(cut-up: a technique probably first used by the writer William Burroughs (1914-1997) in some of his books. A text, typed or written is cut up into fragments, segments (words, phrases, sentences, etc.) and then assembled at random in an \"accidental\" order which is eventually printed)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cuddon, J. A dictionary of literary terms and literary theory, 1998", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cutups (Literature)"}}, {"@id": "_:n9b9cc09e27044c04b92fea1c5c6cfda5b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cutups (Literature)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026274"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026275", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCyberpunk fiction", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026275", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b1"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk fiction"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026529"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b5"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b6"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b8"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b11"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b14"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b17"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Science fiction that depicts the relationship between human beings and the rapid advancement and omnipresence of technology, which leads to a radical change in the social order."}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T13:00:46"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Cyberpunk: phase of American science fiction in the 1980s and 1990s most often associated with William Gibson's novel Neuromancer (1984) and its sequels, and with the work of Bruce Sterling, who edited Mirrorshades: the cyberpunk anthology (1986). By contrast with earlier mainstream science fiction, which commonly implied a utopian confidence in technological progress, cyberpunk fiction is influenced by the gloomier world of hard-boiled detective fiction and by film noir thrillers; it foresees a near future in which sinister multinational corporations dominate the 'cyberspace' (that is, the world computerized information network) upon which an impoverished metropolitan populace depends.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Baldick, C. Oxford dictionary of literary terms, c2008", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Cyberpunk: a type of science fiction especially prevalent in the 1980s usually set in the near future and focusing on the relationships among human beings and computers and other forms of advanced technology; human beings who have been physically altered by new technology is a typical cyberpunk theme; cyberpunk protagonists tend to be isolated, rebellious male anti-heros from the margins of society (the 'punk' part of the word), drifting through a kind of meaningless, shattered world. The tone tends to be cool, calculatedly anti-sentimental.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Henry, L. The fiction dictionary, c1995", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Arguably, science fiction entered the postcyberpunk era in 1988 with the publication of Bruce Sterling's Islands in the Net. Just as Sterling's The Artificial Kid encapsulated many of cyber-punk's themes before the movement had a name, Islands in the Net prefigured a growing body of work that can (at least until someone comes up with a better name) be labeled postcyberpunk. ... Classic cyberpunk characters were marginalized, alienated loners who lived on the edge of society in generally dystopic futures where daily life was impacted by rapid technological change, an ubiquitous datsphere of computerized information, and invasive modification of the human body. ... Postcyberpunk uses the same immersive world-building technique, but features different characters, settings, and, most importantly, makes fundamentally different assumptions about the future. Far from being alienated loners, postcyberpunk characters are frequently integral members of society (i.e., they have jobs). They live in futures that are not necessarily dystopic (indeed, they are often suffused with an optimism that ranges from cautious to exuberant), but their everyday lives are still impacted by rapid technological change and an omnipresent computerized infrastructure. Neal Stephenson's The Diamond Age is perhaps the most popular postcyberpunk novel\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Person, L. Notes toward a postcyberpunk manifesto, via WWW, July 17, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberprep fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberprep fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Postcyberpunk fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Postcyberpunk fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Post-cyberpunk fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Post-cyberpunk fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk science fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:nee598cd574b949bea52e58819f9e3069b18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk science fiction"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026275"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026276", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDebate poetry", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026276", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb1"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Debate poetry"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026481"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb5"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb6"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb7"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb8"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb9"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb11"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb14"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb17"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb20"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb23"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb26"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Poems that feature a debate that is referred to a judge."}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-07-31T10:15:12"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Debate poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Debate poetry. UF D\u00e9bat; Streitgedicht; Verse debate. BT Allegories; Poetry; NT Jeux-partis)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 17, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(D\u00e9bat, Streitgedicht: contest in verse, allegorical, a lit. form)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Shipley dict. world lit. terms", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(D\u00e9bat: a type of lit. composition, usually in verse ... in which two persons or objects, frequently allegorical, debated some specific topic and then referred it to a judge)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Holman, W. A handbook to literature, c2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "introd. (Debate poetry: verse debate on a"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Medieval debate poetry, 1987:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(D\u00e9bat: a kind of poetic contest) p. 688 (tenson, tenzone: poetic composition, a debate bet. two poets, or with a poet versus an imaginary opponent)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cuddon, J. A dictionary of literary terms and literary theory, 1991", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "An anthology of medieval love debate poetry, c2006.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "D\u00e9bats"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "D\u00e9bats"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Poetic contests"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Poetic contests"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Streitgedichte"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Streitgedichte"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Jeux-partis"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Jeux-partis"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb23", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb24"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Debates, Verse"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb24", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Debates, Verse"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb26", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb27"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Verse debates"}}, {"@id": "_:n63e28a380f1544b398d805ad051f7fccb27", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Verse debates"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026276"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026277", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDecimas", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026277", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b1"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Decimas"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026481"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b5"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b6"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b7"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b9"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b12"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Spanish poems consisting of ten lines of eight syllables."}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-21T09:08:18"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Decimas"}}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Beverido de Risso, J. Recetario de cocina en d\u00e9cimas espinelas, 2007.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Decimas. UF Espinelas)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 17, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(D\u00e9cima is a Spanish term of the 14th and 15th centuries referring to any 10 line stanza. In the 16th century, the poet adventurer Vencinente Espinela developed the D\u00e9cima into the verse form of today the D\u00e9cima or D\u00e9cima Espinela or simply Espinela. By whatever title, it is commonly referred to as \"the little sonnet\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Poetry magnum opus online, Dec. 21, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Decima: a much used and classic Spanish stanza form. It consists of ten octosyllabic lines rhyming abbaaccddc)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cuddon, J. A dictionary of literary terms and literary theory, 1991", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Espinelas"}}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Espinelas"}}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "D\u00e9cimas espinelas"}}, {"@id": "_:n8587550484e8422089181bacdbf41f01b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "D\u00e9cimas espinelas"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026277"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026278", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDescorts", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026278", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b1"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Descorts"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026481"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b5"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b6"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b7"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b8"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b9"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Poems consisting of multiple stanzas that differ from one another in verse form or language."}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-01T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ilchalcs"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T13:01:23"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Descorts"}}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Descort: Either a synonym for the Proven\u00e7al lai or a term for a poem whose stanzas are in different languages. Also a Galician verse form expressing the 'discord' felt by the poet when he is suffering the pangs of unrequited love. Length and meter are variable)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cuddon, J.A. A dictionary of literary terms and literary theory, 1991", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Descort. 1: a medieval French lyric in which the stanzas are unlike. 2: a poem in old Proven\u00e7al literature with stanzas in different languages)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam-Webster online, Dec. 21, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Descort, Occitan (Old French = discordant), is a genre of poetry that is written with differing verse forms utilized from strophe to strophe. Each strophe is structured in a minimum of 2 elements, the verse forms chosen can be recognized verse or nonce forms. Elements such as line number, meter, rhyme, and language are all factors)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Poetry magnum opus online, Dec. 21, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Descort is French for \"discord\", and the form is almost more of a non-form. It requires that each stanza or verse have some similar lyrical elements; however, that they be totally different forms. Each stanza should be verse, that is, a structured portion of poetry, but not the same. They can conform to known verse structures or be nonce forms. Some descorts have even used different languages in different stanzas)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "PoetryBase online, Dec. 21, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6edde5082da14f95adbc1078cfd09f48b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Descort: Occitan and OF lyric genre, courtly in substance, heterostrophic in versification. The d., like the lyric lai, was constructed from stanzas or versicles, each different from the others but each showing two-fold (sometimes three- or four-fold) metrical and musical symmetry. The title (\"discord\") referred to this irregularity, to the contrast between lively tunes and melancholy words, and in one exceptional case, to the five different langs. used in the five stanzas)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The new Princeton encyclopedia of poetry and poetics, 1993", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026278"}
