nal, antiphonary: the liturgical book of the Western Christian rites containing the chants for the Office and thus antiphons as well as responses and other types of chant)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Harvard dict. of music", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7a820a965eb34beda079fdb68c519601b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7a820a965eb34beda079fdb68c519601b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Antiphonals (Service books)"}}, {"@id": "_:n7a820a965eb34beda079fdb68c519601b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Antiphonals (Service books)"}}, {"@id": "_:n7a820a965eb34beda079fdb68c519601b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7a820a965eb34beda079fdb68c519601b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Antiphoners (Service books)"}}, {"@id": "_:n7a820a965eb34beda079fdb68c519601b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Antiphoners (Service books)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026636"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026637", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aAntiphons (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026637", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nb086053a8e104df1bf7d96e9cd120491b1"}, {"@id": "_:nb086053a8e104df1bf7d96e9cd120491b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Antiphons (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb086053a8e104df1bf7d96e9cd120491b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026707"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027062"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:nb086053a8e104df1bf7d96e9cd120491b5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nb086053a8e104df1bf7d96e9cd120491b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nb086053a8e104df1bf7d96e9cd120491b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:50"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nb086053a8e104df1bf7d96e9cd120491b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Antiphons (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb086053a8e104df1bf7d96e9cd120491b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(In Latin Christian chant generally, a liturgical chant with a prose text, sung in association with a psalm. In Gregorian psalmody, for example, psalms and canticles are usually preceded and followed by a single antiphon, and the psalm tone used for the recitation of the psalm itself is often musically incomplete without the antiphon. Antiphons of this kind may be regarded as typical and are represented above all by the Gregorian antiphons to the psalms of Matins, Lauds and Vespers in the Divine Office. There are also other categories of antiphon, some of which may lack psalmody or have versified texts. The antiphon and responsory are the two musical genres with Latin prose texts that occur in all the Western liturgies and are the most abundant within the chant repertory. A given medieval Office source might have as many as 1500 antiphons, and even up to 2000; such a large number could occur in a monastic usage, whereas the secular (or canons') repertory would generally have fewer)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed May 28, 2014 :", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026637"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026638", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aArabesk (Popular music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026638", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b1"}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Arabesk (Popular music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027009"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b5"}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b6"}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b7"}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b8"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:50"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Arabesk (Popular music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Turkey: Arabesk: An Arab-influenced pop music)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "White, D. Dict. pop. mus. styles world", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Turkey, Popular music: Arabesk: A complex reaction of fascination and repulsion with Arab culture underlies the emergence of the arabesk genre. Egyptian influences have been important in Turkish popular music. Rural Turkish culture is the other important component of arabesk music. The term arabesk was first used in connection with the singer and virtuosic ba\u011flama player Orhan Gencebay, whose first best-selling single was released in 1969; his style is eclectic, combining elements such as baroque concerto form, Western rock techniques and Turkish art music, often adding a harmonized \"sub-structure\" and passages of counterpoint. In 1975 the introduction of stereo-recording technology facilitated a distinctive orchestral sound, typified in recordings by Orhan Gencebay, Ferdi Tayfur, and M\u00fcsl\u00fcm G\u00fcrses, using string orchestras (in Arab film style), large percussion sections, and instruments associated with Western rock and Turkish folk and urban art music; an antiphonal structure prevails, with solo vocal or instrumental statements answered by string chorus. While arabesk was rigorously excluded from state radio and television, its commercial presence was tolerated, and it thrived through cheap cassette production and live performance in clubs)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online WWW site, Jan. 3, 2005", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Arabesk is a popular music genre that emerged in Turkey in the late 1960s. The name alludes to elements borrowed from Arab music. Arabesk composers are mainly inspired by Turkish folk and art music but also experiment with musical material from other cultures. Typically, arabesk lyrics are deeply pessimistic. Despite its huge popularity, arabesk is strongly opposed by the Turkish cultural elite, who see it as a sign of cultural decadence. In fact, the term arabesk was originally derogatory)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Garland encyc. world mus.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2046800a8384ee5a11b5007c0d1d4b0b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2003345446: I\u015f\u0131k, C. Arabeskin anlam d\u00fcnyas\u0131, 2002.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026638"}
