{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026685", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCandlemas music", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026685", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nf1d06d64b838418dbc2308320f33dddeb1"}, {"@id": "_:nf1d06d64b838418dbc2308320f33dddeb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Candlemas music"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf1d06d64b838418dbc2308320f33dddeb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026795"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027062"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:nf1d06d64b838418dbc2308320f33dddeb5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Music that is traditionally associated with Candlemas."}, {"@id": "_:nf1d06d64b838418dbc2308320f33dddeb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nf1d06d64b838418dbc2308320f33dddeb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T09:59:46"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nf1d06d64b838418dbc2308320f33dddeb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Candlemas music"}}, {"@id": "_:nf1d06d64b838418dbc2308320f33dddeb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Presentation of Jesus at the Temple (Other tradition names include Candlemas, the Feast of the Purification of the Virgin, and the Meeting of the Lord; many motets and anthems have been composed to celebrate this feast and are performed as part of the liturgy)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed August 28, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026685"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026686", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCandombes (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026686", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b1"}, {"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Candombes (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027009"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b5"}, {"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b6"}, {"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b7"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Candombes (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Candombe: A dance and song genre of Uruguay. The word \"candombe\" has had various different but related meanings throughout the 19th and 20th centuries. In the colonial era it denoted the musical practices of the black communities; from the 1930s onwards it has described phenomena associated with the llamada (drum call) of the tamboriles, while in modern times it has designated a song whose rhythm is compatible with the drummed llamada so that it can be superimposed over it. During the 1940s the term had two further meanings: the first as a form conserved by the conjuntos lubolos, black societies in the official Carnival celebrations of Montevideo (together with milong\u00f3n and other Afro-Brazilian or Afro-Cuban genres); the second is associated with the \"traditional\", mainly white tango orchestras (especially those of the River Plate), whose repertory--particularly its \"milonguero\", or festive, aspect-- was readily compatible with the drummed rhythms of the llamada of the tamboriles. In the 1950s, when the tango candombe trend was in decline, a third candombe type emerged in the repertory of dance bands strongly influenced by commercial Afro-Cuban dance music. From the mid-1960s to the early 70s the candombe evolved further with the development of a fusion between the candombe of the conjuntos lubdos and of the \"tropical\" dance bands with influences deriving principally from jazz, but also from rock, Brazilian bossa nova and even Indian tabla drums as popularized by the Beatles.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, Aug. 10, 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Candombe: Music-dance genre of Afroamerican antecedence present in various countries of Latin America. Articles under Argentina and Uruguay. The plural form candombes is used in the articles.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dicc. de la m\u00fasica espa\u00f1ola e hispanoamericano", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0cdd44fd27c642cbaf6ca3fa9ba3ca90b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "prologue (Candombe is a fundamental style in the popular music of Uruguay. Many artists have employed this indigenous rhythm in their compositions. Candombe is an Afro-American rhythm identified with Uruguay in the same way that the samba is identified with Brazil)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2002539032: Machado, H.F. El toque de candombe, c2002:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026686"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026687", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCanons (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026687", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b1"}, {"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canons (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b3"}]}, "madsrdf:exampleNote": "Note under [Fugues]", "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026639"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027058"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b5"}, {"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Musical works with a melody that is imitated at a particular interval of time, generally note-for-note. For works based on canonic imitation that generally begin with a subject that recurs frequently throughout see [Fugues.]"}, {"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-01T10:42:11"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canons (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Canon; originally referred to an inscribed formula or instruction which the performer would implement in order to realize one or more parts from the given notation; one possibility was extracting a second voice from the given voice at a specified intervallic and temporal distance; the word 'canon' eventually came to mean the polyphonic texture of two or more voices created by the procedure, which is its primary meaning today)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed July 8, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5b2b884fb0c34ce3a8b5a8c09259d920b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Canon; strictest form of contrapuntal imitation; applied to counterpoint in which one melodic strand gives the rule to another, or to all the others, which must, at an interval of time, imitate it, note for note; simple forms of choral canon are the catch and the round)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford dictionary of music online, viewed July 8, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026687"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026688", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCantatas", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026688", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b1"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cantatas"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026639"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b5"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b6"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b7"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b8"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b9"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b10"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Vocal music that usually contains solos, duets, recitatives, and choruses with instrumental accompaniment. For sacred cantatas assign the additional term [Sacred music.]"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2021-12-20T10:33:26"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cantatas"}}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(cantata: a composition for one or more voices usually comprising solos, duets, recitatives, and choruses and sung to an instrumental accompaniment)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam-Webster dictionary online, Sept. 17, 2021", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cantata (The cantata is a vocal form consisting of solos, ensembles, and choruses accompanied by orchestra, piano, or other combinations of instruments. A cantata may have sacred or secular texts and can be narrative or dramatic. Defining the cantata in contrast to its relative the oratorio is difficult. The most reliable distinctions between the two genres are length and subject. Typically, oratorios tend to be religious, dramatic, and long, lasting for one or more hours, while cantatas are generally shorter and feature secular themes. Most cantatas are written for mixed chorus, some are for male chorus, and even fewer are for female chorus)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, Sept. 17, 2021:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)1182785201"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Bach, J.S. Secular cantatas, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Cantata. A composite vocal genre of the Baroque era, consisting of a succession of recitatives, ariosos, and set-pieces... A cantata may be either secular or sacred in subject matter and function, and its treatment may be lyrical, allegorical, or dramatic (although almost never actually staged). Cantatas range from intimate, small-scale works for solo singer or singers and restricted accompanimental forces (sometimes called chamber cantatas) to large ones with chorus and orchestral accompaniment)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Harvard dictionary of music, 4th edition", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(score of work composed in 1867) (OCoLC)2937985"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dubois, T. The seven last words of Christ : a sacred cantata for soli, chorus and orchestra, 1899", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc23f4f39d82c4941b355855674cf4c48b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Cantatas, Sacred. UF Sacred cantatas. SN Here are entered sacred cantatas for mixed voices or collections of sacred cantatas for various groups of voices (men's, mixed, women's, etc.); Cantatas, Secular. UF Secular cantatas. SN Here are entered secular cantatas for mixed voices or collections of secular cantatas for various groups of voices (men's, mixed, women's, etc.))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Sept. 17, 2021", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026688"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026689", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCanti carnascialeschi", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026689", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b1"}, {"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canti carnascialeschi"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026694"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026984"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b5"}, {"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canti carnascialeschi"}}, {"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(canti carnascialeschi: generic term encompassing several kinds of partsong, notably mascheratas, carri and trionfi, performed at festivals in Florence during the late 15th and early 16th century)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of mus. online, Jan. 20, 2004", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n46a1080ee4584d47aa4974a8e947ea14b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "2003441489: Osuna Garc\u00eda, J. C\u00e1diz, cuna de dos cantes, c2002.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026689"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026690", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCanticles", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026690", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n506bd791466a435991f73b6e3d34bea1b1"}, {"@id": "_:n506bd791466a435991f73b6e3d34bea1b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canticles"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n506bd791466a435991f73b6e3d34bea1b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027062"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:n506bd791466a435991f73b6e3d34bea1b5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n506bd791466a435991f73b6e3d34bea1b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n506bd791466a435991f73b6e3d34bea1b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n506bd791466a435991f73b6e3d34bea1b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canticles"}}, {"@id": "_:n506bd791466a435991f73b6e3d34bea1b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Canticle. A designation for hymns in the scriptures apart from the psalms; it is sometimes applied loosely to the Te Deum and other non-scriptural texts as well as to certain psalms, particularly in the Anglican rite.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 28, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026690"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026691", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCantillations", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026691", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n0eb766c7d09f4204afbdca8d94b119c1b1"}, {"@id": "_:n0eb766c7d09f4204afbdca8d94b119c1b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cantillations"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0eb766c7d09f4204afbdca8d94b119c1b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026707"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027062"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:n0eb766c7d09f4204afbdca8d94b119c1b5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n0eb766c7d09f4204afbdca8d94b119c1b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n0eb766c7d09f4204afbdca8d94b119c1b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n0eb766c7d09f4204afbdca8d94b119c1b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cantillations"}}, {"@id": "_:n0eb766c7d09f4204afbdca8d94b119c1b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Musical or semi-musical chanting of sacred texts, prayers, etc. by a solo singer in a liturgical context. The term primarily refers to such chanting in the Jewish synagogue tradition but is also used for the comparable public recitation of lessons in the various Christian traditions and in other religious rites.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of mus.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026691"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026692", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCanzonets (Part songs)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026692", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b1"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canzonets (Part songs)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026984"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b5"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b6"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b7"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b9"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canzonets (Part songs)"}}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Canzonetta [canzonet]; A title given to a light secular vocal piece, particularly in the Italian style, from the late 16th century to the late 18th... Its first appearance on a title-page was in the second editions of Orazio Vecchi's Canzonette ... libro primo a quattro voci (1580) ... Canzonettas of the type invented and popularized by Vecchi are shorter and simpler than the five- and six-voice canzoni of Ferretti and Conversi, but more refined and stylish than the three-voice villanellas of the 1560s and 70s. The texts are simple strophic poems of amorous, humorous or satirical character, often imitating the Petrarchistic style of madrigal verse. Each stanza consists of three to six lines, with lines of seven and 11 syllables freely intermixed... The musical features of the canzonetta include stanza forms of AABB or AABCC, clearly separated phrases, homophonic or lightly imitative textures, sprightly rhythms, high tessituras and madrigalian word-painting reflecting the text of the first stanza)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed July 14, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(canzonetta; canzonet)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(canzonet; canzonetta)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Harvard dict. of music", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 87750226: Vecchi, O. The four-voice canzonettas, 1993, c1992.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canzonettas (Part songs)"}}, {"@id": "_:naac15498516141f6bbdab9a184e2ac17b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Canzonettas (Part songs)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026692"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026693", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCarnatic music", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026693", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b1"}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carnatic music"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026639"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026623"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b5"}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b7"}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b10"}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b13"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carnatic music"}}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 9, p. 70 (In South Asia there are two recognized dialects of classical music ... In the Dravidian-speaking areas (i.e. most of peninsular India) ... Carnatic (or South Indian))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of mus.:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "South Asia (Carnatic system ... common in the southern, Dravidian-speaking areas [of the continent] such as the Tamil-speaking areas of Sri-Lanka)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Harvard Dict. of mus.:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Karnatic music"}}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Karnatic music"}}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "South Indian classical music"}}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "South Indian classical music"}}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Karnatak music"}}, {"@id": "_:n54375becf3da4c6fb6c8f655a40869f1b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Karnatak music"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026693"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026694", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCarnival music", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026694", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n6b9bfea374844600b9d6cecfd9dbbb75b1"}, {"@id": "_:n6b9bfea374844600b9d6cecfd9dbbb75b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carnival music"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6b9bfea374844600b9d6cecfd9dbbb75b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026795"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026689"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026817"}], "madsrdf:hasReciprocalAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026680"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:n6b9bfea374844600b9d6cecfd9dbbb75b5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Music composed for, or associated with, the pre-Lenten festivities known as Carnival."}, {"@id": "_:n6b9bfea374844600b9d6cecfd9dbbb75b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T10:20:16"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n6b9bfea374844600b9d6cecfd9dbbb75b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n6b9bfea374844600b9d6cecfd9dbbb75b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carnival music"}}, {"@id": "_:n6b9bfea374844600b9d6cecfd9dbbb75b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carnival music of Eastern Cuba, p2009."}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "OCLC database, viewed July 11, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026694"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026695", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCarols", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026695", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db1"}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carols"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027103"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026065"}, "madsrdf:hasReciprocalAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026716"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026781"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db5"}, "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db6"}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db9"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "For texts of carols that appear without a musical setting see [Carol texts.]"}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-07-13T14:04:29"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carols"}}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(During the Middle Ages, an English or Latin song of uniform stanzas beginning with a refrain that is repeated after each stanza; on any subject but mostly the Virgin or Saints of Christmas. In recent centuries, usually refers to strophic songs (some with refrains) associated with Christmas; many texts derived from medieval English carols)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of mus. online, Sept. 5, 2001", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db6", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db7"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Christmas carols"}}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db7", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Christmas carols"}}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Easter carols"}}, {"@id": "_:nd1dbc52d4e7a4340b61b16224cf94f4db10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Easter carols"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026695"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026696", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCavachas (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026696", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b1"}, {"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cavachas (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027009"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b5"}, {"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cavachas (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "booklet (Cavacha is a type of fast rhythm found in pop music from Zaire. It was first used in the 70s by Belobi Meridjo, the drummer of the group Zaiko Langa, Langa who wanted to imitate the sound of a speeding train. A dance named Cavacha quickly developed and became enormously popular not only in the Congo, but all over Africa, thanks to the powerful national radio station transmitters known locally as Tam-Tam d'Afrique)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Legends of Benin [SR] p2009:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n3f7ff3eaa3314659aa62f91b8871af53b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Congo, Democratic Republic of the. III.4 Popular music. (ii) The first generation of bands. The structure of Congolese rumba music provided a wide range of possibilities for changes and modification in the improvisation section, where composers introduced new rhythmic and melodic elements. Among the most prominent varieties of rumba have been: soukous (1966), kiri-kiri (1969), cavacha and ekonda sacade (1972), mokonyonyon (1977), n'goss and its variant zekete-zekete (1977-87), kwasa-kwasa (1986), madiaba (1988), mayebo (1990), mayeno (1991), sundama, kintekuna (1992), moto (1994) and ndombolo (1997))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove online, Aug. 31, 2009", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026696"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026697", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCeltic music", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026697", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b1"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Celtic music"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026809"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027009"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b5"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b6"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b7"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b8"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b9"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b10"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b11"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Traditional music of Celtic ethnic groups in general and musical compositions containing elements of, or inspired by, traditional music in the Celtic style."}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T10:00:04"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Celtic music"}}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 8, p. 319-322 (Celtic music: Scholars have never fully clarified their application of the adjective Celtic to music.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Garland encyc. of world music:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "MacInnes, S. A journey in Celtic music, Cape Breton style, c1997.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Celtic music: Irish in origin but more widely influential, contemporary Celtic popular music is an example of a hybrid, metagenre, variants of which have crossed over into the mainstream of popular music; frequently involves a blending of traditional and modern forms; in much of this work, traditional Irish melodies are given a pop/rock dimension, with lyrics sometimes in Gaelic.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Shuker, R. Key concepts in popular music, 1998", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Celtic music: Fanciful term which expresses a world-view or record-shelf category rather than actual links between music genres.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Companion to traditional Irish music, c1999", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Celtic music; styles: Celtic folk (blend of British folk and Celtic music; has structure of traditional British and Celtic music, but attitude and aesthetics of modern-day folk and contemporary singer/songwriters); Celtic gospel (gospel songs, both traditional and new compositions, performed in Celtic style); Celtic pop (melodies (and perhaps instrumentation) are unmistakably influenced by the authentic article, but at its core the music follows pop conventions and would rarely be mistaken for the traditional form; contemporary Celtic (started during the '80s and '90s by several artists (such as Clannad) that expanded boundaries of Celtic music by adding elements of pop, rock, folk, jazz, and New Age music); traditional Celtic; Celtic fusion (incorporates elements of traditional Celtic music with New Age and pop); Celtic New Age (hybrid of Celtic themes, instrumentation, and songs, plus new age production and sensibilities); Celtic rock (traditional Irish instruments combined with rock rhythms))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "All music guide WWW site, Nov. 30, 2001", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Celtic music: see Celtic chant, Ireland, Wales, Scotland, and Europe, Pre- and Proto-historic)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove, 2nd ed. WWW site, Nov. 30, 2001", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4cc1642d7d57482bb890b3976131d051b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under United States (Celtic music: music associated with the Celtic culture; style became in vogue in the 1980s)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "White, D. Dict. of popular music styles of the world, c1998:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026697"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026698", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCerdd dant", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026698", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b1"}, {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cerdd dant"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026811"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b5"}, {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b7"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cerdd dant"}}, {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (Ceinciau'r gorwel : deuddeg o alawon cerdd dant = 12 cerdd dant airs)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Ceinciau'r gorwel, 2003:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Bard (The ROBERT AP HUW Manuscript (GB-Lbl Add.14905)..., is an early 17th-century document, part of which appears to be copied from a manuscript of William Penllyn (fl 1550-70). The origins of the music can be traced back to named musicians of the 15th century and earlier; the compositional principles are based on the 24 measures of cerdd dant ('the craft of the string'), ostensibly formulated in about 1100 according to the document known as the Cadwedigaeth Cerdd Dannau ('The Conservation of Cerdd Dant').)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 9, 2014 :", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Penillion singing"}}, {"@id": "_:n402386aa1d9a4877899023dd7a83e687b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Penillion singing"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026698"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026699", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChaabi", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026699", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb1"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaabi"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027009"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb5"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb6"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb7"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb8"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb9"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb10"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb12"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb15"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Algerian popular music based on traditional proverbs or modern-day maxims that speak of everyday situations."}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T10:00:20"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaabi"}}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(La chanson populaire algerienne; v. 2)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Elanka, E.M. Le chaabi [SR]", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Britannica;Merriam-Webster;All music guide", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pkg. (Like the tango from Argentina or the fado from Portugal ... or the Negro blues of North America ... the chaabi emerged in the early 20th century out of the poorer quarters of the Casbah in Algiers ... It took its inspiration from Moorish music ... The words, based on traditional proverbs or modern-day maxims, speak of real life situations ... the instruments it uses (the mandolin, banjo, and piano) would seem to indicate modernist intentions)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: El Achab, A. Le chaabi des grands ma\u00eetres [SR] = The chaabi of the great masters, p2000:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Arab world. Algeria (chaabiya; chaabi. Popular music. Chaabi means popular) Egypt (shaabi, shaaby. Egyptian popular music, especially for the working class poor) Morocco (chaabi, chaabiya. Popular music)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "White, D. Dict. of popular music styles of the world, 1998:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Algeria. Urban musics (sha\u02bbb\u012b; sha\u02bbb\u012b music (people's music). Pieces consist of lengthy narrative songs sung by a single performer interspersed with vociferous choral sections involving the ensemble. As the music most associated with the working classes of the city of Algiers, sha\u02bbb\u012b was promoted by the first socialist, post-colonial regimes. Ensemble always includes the mand\u016bl, darbukka, violins, and often the banjo. Algerian sha\u02bbb\u012b, not to be confused with Egyptian and Moroccan musics with the same name)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove online:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Kheloui, L. Master of the Kabylian chaabi [SR] 2000.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 1, p. 545-546 (sha\u02bbb\u012b, a music widely popular throughout the Maghrib)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Garland encyc. of world music:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sha\u02bbb\u012b"}}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sha\u02bbb\u012b"}}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaabiya"}}, {"@id": "_:nfdb9b33d4d074f84bb6cee1a1bf8575fb16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaabiya"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026699"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026700", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChacareras (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026700", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb1"}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chacareras (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb5"}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb6"}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb7"}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb8"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chacareras (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 2, p. 979 (chacarera: Argentine social dance performed during jineteada) p. 261 (under Argentina, Folkloric music: recordings are usually used, but instrumental ensembles Evently accompany dances such as chacarera)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Garland encyc. world mus.:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Argentina: chacarera: dance music with Andean influence)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dict. pop. mus. styles world", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Argentina, Traditional music: Couple dances, such as the chacarera ... are ... accompanied by song; under Latin America, Iberian and mestizo folk music, South America: the Argentine chacarera; probably created by the farmers of the pampas, the plains of the province of Buenos Aires; musically, the Spanish ancestry is also evident in the use of hemiola rhythm (alternating 6/8 and 3/4 metres) in the instrumental introduction)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online WWW site, Sept. 30, 2005", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n97a6f64382624abfa31bbc3724d3b8afb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2005428618: Abecasis, A. La chacarera bien mensurada, 2004.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026700"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026701", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChaconnes (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026701", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b1"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconnes (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027156"}], "madsrdf:hasEarlierEstablishedForm": {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b5"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b8"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b9"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b10"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b11"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b12"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b13"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Heading changed from Chaconnes to Chaconnes (Music) in October 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-18T07:01:48"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconnes (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b5", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b6"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconnes"}}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b6", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconnes"}}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page ([by] Handel; arranged for two guitars) (OCoLC)12897332"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Chaconne in G with 21 variations, 1967:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page ([by] Johann Sebastian Bach; arranged for guitar by Siegfried Behrend)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Chaconne d-Moll, \u00a91957:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(chaconne: a form of continuous variation, similar to the passacaglia, which became popular during the Baroque era; in the 20th century the term was sometimes used to denote an instrumental piece of a particularly austere character using ground-bass variations)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxf. comp. mus., 2002", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Chaconne: before 1800, a dance, often performed at a quite brisk tempo, that generally used variation techniques, though not necessarily ground-bass variation; in 19th- and 20th-century music, a set of ground-bass or ostinato variations, usually of a severe character; the term is sometimes used interchangeably with \"passacaglia\"; many composers drew a distinction between the chaconne and the passacaglia, the nature of which depended on local tradition and to some extent on individual preference)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove, 2nd ed. WWW site, Nov. 15, 2002", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Chaconne: a continuous variation form of the Baroque, similar to the passacaglia, based on the chord progression of a late 16th-century dance imported into Spain and Italy from Latin America; although 18th-century theorists attempted to distinguish between the chaconne and the passacaglia, no consistent differences are readily apparent from the 18th century onward, except that the chaconne was more frequently in major)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Harvard dict. mus.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconnes"}}, {"@id": "_:n7a7b3eb729704265bb3dee80afc584c5b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconnes"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026701"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026702", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCha-chas (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026702", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ncf89f35dee044c01aadcf523231d5118b1"}, {"@id": "_:ncf89f35dee044c01aadcf523231d5118b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cha-chas (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ncf89f35dee044c01aadcf523231d5118b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:ncf89f35dee044c01aadcf523231d5118b5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf89f35dee044c01aadcf523231d5118b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ncf89f35dee044c01aadcf523231d5118b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ncf89f35dee044c01aadcf523231d5118b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cha-chas (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf89f35dee044c01aadcf523231d5118b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Cha-cha. A popular ballroom dance that grew in popularity during the 1950s, derived from a Cuban music and dance style.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 9, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026702"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026703", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChamam\u00e9 (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026703", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b1"}, {"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamam\u00e9 (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b5"}, {"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b6"}, {"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b7"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamam\u00e9 (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Argentina, II: Folk music, 3. Mestizo and creole music (Music in the littoral provinces resembles that of Paraguay and is often described as \"Guarani music,\" although the only Guarani element retained is the language which is mixed with Spanish in texts of a romantic nature; the various forms, such as the polca, the more modern chamam\u00e9, and galopa may also be danced)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of music:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 2, Argentina, pp. 261-270 (The introd. of the European polka in the northeast provinces resulted in a process of creolization giving birth to the chamam\u00e9; since the 1970's, the chamam\u00e9 has been the only traditional Argentinian music and dance form that is expanding, even reaching southern Chile)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Garland encyc. of world music:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na085dad16303413a896588d0021a7346b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "booklet (Chamam\u00e9, a Guarani word, is a rural dance music originating in the northeast province of Corrientes, resulting from a fusion of Guarani, European, and African elements. When sung, it is sung in both Guarani and Spanish. It has spread to the provinces of Misiones, Chaco, Formosa, Entre Rios, and Santa F\u00e9)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 00524943: Chamam\u00e9, p1997:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026703"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026704", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChamber music", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026704", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b1"}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamber music"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026639"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026984"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2018026024"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b5"}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b7"}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b10"}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b13"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamber music"}}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(consort: a composition for an instrumental ensemble, usually of two to eight players)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Harvard dict. of music", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(To limit the term to instrumental ensemble repertory, for example, is to exclude such hybrid works as Schubert's Der Hirt auf dem Felsen and Vaughan Williams's On Wenlock Edge and to bypass such vocal genres as 16th-century madrigals and 19th-century lieder which share many of the characteristics of chamber music.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of mus. online, Dec. 4, 2002", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Consorts (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Consorts (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Consort music"}}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Consort music"}}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Vocal chamber music"}}, {"@id": "_:n97d98c5f7f1f4735ad539ec337b066b8b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Vocal chamber music"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026704"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026705", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChampeta (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026705", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb1"}, {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Champeta (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027009"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb5"}, {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb7"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Champeta (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Champeta Criolla is an Afro-Colombian music style and dance from Cartagena. The champeta is a hybrid style made up of the rhythms of the Caribbean, Cumbia, Comps\u0300, Soca, Calypso, Reggae, combined with the sounds of several countries of the African continent, such as Soukous, Mbaqanga, Bikutsi, Highlife, Juju and Congolese rumba. They were intertwined and gave way to a new style in the barrios (districts) of Cartagena (Colombia). The lyrics are usually satirical. It is also known as terapia criolla)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "World Music Central WWW Home page, Mar. 8, 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 11 (Also known as terapia criolla)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Entre la champeta y la pared, 2004:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Terapia criolla"}}, {"@id": "_:n3b59f653aef140629396b3eaddd641ebb8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Terapia criolla"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026705"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026706", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChanchona (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026706", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb1"}, {"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chanchona (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027009"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb5"}, {"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chanchona (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "label (chanchona music) p. 3 of booklet (\"The Salvadoran Oriente is home to a distinctive style of music called chanchona.\" \"Both the general style of music and the musical ensemble itself found their namesake in the local name for the string bass--chanchona 'sow, female pig'--because of its size and shape.\") p. 4 of booklet (\"Today, the cumbia accounts for the vast majority of the chanchona repertoire--perhaps 80% or more--capturing the heart of the chanchona tradition.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Hermanos Lovo. Soy salvadore\u00f1o! [SR] p2011:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n65fd3804841a4ed99d18052c0284e4fbb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Music of El Salvador; Popular music (\"Salvadoran cumbia is related to, but very distinct from Colombian cumbia, which is better known outside of El Salvador. Chanchona ensembles, led by a pair or a single violin, are popular, especially among the immigrant community in the Washington D.C. area.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, June 6, 2011:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026706"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026707", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChants", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026707", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nb4d505212d264317820d0e474308115db1"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d505212d264317820d0e474308115db2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chants"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb4d505212d264317820d0e474308115db3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026952"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026637"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026691"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026843"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026978"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027020"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027021"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027029"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027049"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027088"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027089"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027154"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026151"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2020026073"}], "madsrdf:hasReciprocalAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026719"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:nb4d505212d264317820d0e474308115db5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Musical compositions that consist of the rhythmic speaking and/or singing of words or sounds."}, {"@id": "_:nb4d505212d264317820d0e474308115db1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T10:00:30"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d505212d264317820d0e474308115db2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d505212d264317820d0e474308115db3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chants"}}, {"@id": "_:nb4d505212d264317820d0e474308115db5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Chant; the rhythmic speaking or singing of words or sounds, often primarily on one or two main pitches called reciting tones)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed August 28, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026707"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026708", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChapka", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026708", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b1"}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chapka"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027103"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b5"}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b6"}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b8"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chapka"}}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 86 (\"Miscellaneous Song\" musically it indicates semi-art song between folk and art song) p. 129 (a type of folk song developed by professional folk singers)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Song, B. Source readings in Korean music:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 15 (Chapka originally sung by professional singers, became popular in 19th century)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2006571117: Kim Y\u014fng-im \u016di Ky\u014fnggi sipi-chapka, c2005:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 885 (Chapka are sung sitting down (hence an alternative name Chwach\u02bbang \"setting songs\") usually with instrumental accompaniments)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Garland Ency. of World Music:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chwach'ang"}}, {"@id": "_:n0461230ae8c0485fa5b1c2d5e2aa4213b9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chwach'ang"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026708"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026709", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChastushki", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026709", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b1"}, {"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chastushki"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026811"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b5"}, "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b6"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chastushki"}}, {"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Russian Federation. II, Russian traditional music. 4, Chastushki (a vocal-instrumental genre consisting of short, single-stanza couplets; theses usually have four lines (more rarely two or six,)... The rhythmic kinship of all chastushk (usually with a structure of 8 + & +8 + 7 syllables in trochaic tetrameters) contributed to their easy dissemination and to the combination of many texts with one melody (or with any melody belonging to one type)... Chastushki are usually performed with an accordion or balalaika. The predominant melodic type is an extremely varied and expressive recitative. The instrumental accompaniment gives much scope to a talented improviser... Chastushki may be performed solo, as a duet (often in the form of a dialogue between friends, called singing na otvet: \"to an answer\"), or by a chorus with or without instrumental accompaniment)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed July 14, 2014 :", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b6", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b7"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chastushkas"}}, {"@id": "_:n46eacdfe962e4d3a878b81935d26e623b7", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chastushkas"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026709"}
