 (real live circus music)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Circus fanfare, 1974:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n9dc93f09d8dd47369192f44c303f722bb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Circus music; a particular type of band music associated with traveling circuses)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "AmeriGrove", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026718"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026719", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aClausulas (Songs)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026719", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb1"}, {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Clausulas (Songs)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027062"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027103"}], "madsrdf:hasReciprocalAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026707"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb5"}, {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb7"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:51"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Clausulas (Songs)"}}, {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The musicians involved seem by nature to have been concerned not with creating new compositions but rather with taking up and developing an older practice. From the very beginnings of sacred polyphony, plainchant melodies were not always set in their entirety; often only certain sections were set polyphonically, the remainder being left monophonic. This practice became more common and was applied in a variety of ways. First, and most important, only specific parts of the plainchant were set. Later, these parts, in an organum style that was not yet modally rhythmic, came to be replaced by new passages (specifically called 'clausul sive puncta') that used modal rhythm... The chance combination of the clausula principle with modal rhythm was at its most powerfully influential on the revisions made to the two-voice settings in the Magnus liber which were carried out with the aim of modernizing its style. Hundreds of two-voice passages were composed for this work, and eventually also for the three- and four-voice organum settings. Some were incorporated into later versions of the Magnus liber; others, placed in liturgical order, were assembled in special fascicles of the appropriate manuscripts, so that a clausula could easily be introduced into an organum setting, or into a piece of plainchant. These clausulas thus became the most important area for experimentation in the compositional techniques of the day, especially for the development of discant... because of the practice of notating clausulas separately, there was a marked tendency for clausulas to increase in size and to become independent compositions.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed June 3, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(plural usage: clausulas; term used in medieval grammar and rhetoric in a number of senses, all denoting either the concluding of a passage, or the passage itself thus concluded; in its formal sense the word \"clausula\" is reserved nowadays almost exclusively for its most important historical application, the clausula of the Notre Dame period)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of mus. online, Dec. 4, 2002", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Clausulae (Songs)"}}, {"@id": "_:ne1d9d11dc3c74543bc95bf6564f1985eb8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Clausulae (Songs)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026719"}
