: {"@language": "en", "@value": "Darkwave (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb971cc302af84b68b931d8837d1fd7fcb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "White, D. Dict. pop. mus. styles world;Wicke, P. Handbuch der popul\u00e4ren Musik;Grovemusic.com WWW site, Feb. 10, 2003", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:nb971cc302af84b68b931d8837d1fd7fcb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Goth rock (During the '90s, the goth sound began to cross-pollinate with industrial music, producing hybrids that appealed to both sides, as well as the darkwave subgenre (which also incorporated '80s synth-pop and dream-pop))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "All music guide WWW site, Feb. 10, 2003:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb971cc302af84b68b931d8837d1fd7fcb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2002501638: Das Gothic- und Dark Wave-Lexikon, c2002.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb971cc302af84b68b931d8837d1fd7fcb8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb971cc302af84b68b931d8837d1fd7fcb9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dark wave (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb971cc302af84b68b931d8837d1fd7fcb9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dark wave (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026759"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026760", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDastg\u0101hs", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026760", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab1"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dastg\u0101hs"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026639"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2020026034"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2020026035"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2020026036"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2020026037"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab5"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab6"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab7"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab8"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab9"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab10"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab13"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2020-11-13T13:46:08"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dastg\u0101hs"}}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mode. V. Middle East and Asia. (v) Modal nucleus and modal complex in Persian music (\"The sense of a larger modal complex is implicit in the concepts of \u0101v\u0101z or dastg\u0101h, used to denote whole collections of modally-related gushehs arranged in a fixed order. For example the expressions dastg\u0101h-e ch\u0101h\u0101rg\u0101h and \u0101v\u0101z-e ch\u0101h\u0101rg\u0101h refer either to the complete series of gushehs whose principal modal nucleus is called ch\u0101h\u0101rg\u0101h, or to a performance of selected items from the same series\") Iran. II. Classical traditions. 3. The modal system (names the 12 dastg\u0101hs, noting that 5 of the 12--including the 2 performed in the album Peyd\u00e2 cited immediately above--\"are commonly considered as subordinate dastg\u0101hs (\u0101v\u0101z)\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, March 6, 2020:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 19 (A dastg\u0101h has been taken to be the counterpart of the Indian raga and the maq\u0101m of the Turko-Arabian music traditions. It has also been translated as a mode in western musical terminology. None of these describes a dastg\u0101h adequately. Two separate ideas are, in fact, addressed by the dastg\u0101h concept. It identifies a set of pieces, traditionally grouped together, most of which have their own individual modes. It also stands for the modal identity of the initial piece in a group) p. 20 (The normal performance duration of a dastg\u0101h in modern times rarely exceeds 30 minutes ... About half of this period is likely to be taken up by composed pieces; the improvised portions centre on the opening mode (dar\u0101mad [entry, introduction]) and two or three of the other modes in the dastg\u0101h)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Farhat, H. The dastg\u0101h concept in Persian music, 1990:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "container (santur and tombak performance of 2 \u00e2v\u00e2zes) (DLC)2020615128"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Peyd\u00e2, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(includes performances of dastg\u0101hs Shur and R\u0101st-Panjg\u0101h)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Iran, \u21172010", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Iran II.3 (Persian classical music is represented by a corpus of amorphous pieces that are subject to extemporized renditions. They adhere to a modal principle that is defined by a set of pitches (maq\u0101m) and a certain melodic contour (m\u0101yeh). The pieces are collectively known as the radif ('row', 'line-up'). 19th-century performing practices have tended to place these pieces into 12 groups, known as the 12 dastg\u0101hs (a large unit with inner components). The pieces within each dastg\u0101h are generically called gusheh, and they carry their own individual names ... The performance of a dastg\u0101h usually begins with one or more sections called dar\u0101mad (introduction). It is in the dar\u0101mad that the mode (maq\u0101m) and the melodic character (m\u0101yeh) of the dastg\u0101h are revealed. After the dar\u0101mad, selections from the gushehs that are constituent parts of the dastg\u0101h are presented ... A rendition of a dastg\u0101h can vary greatly depending on the number of gushehs included and the degree of improvisatory freedom taken, and it may last just a few minutes or well over an hour.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed May 7, 2013:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u0100v\u0101zes"}}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u0100v\u0101zes"}}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dastg\u0101h-h\u0101"}}, {"@id": "_:n6c5ec30889f541b69546e5eab27f1d0ab14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dastg\u0101h-h\u0101"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026760"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026761", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDeath metal (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026761", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db1"}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Death metal (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026854"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db5"}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db6"}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db7"}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db8"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Death metal (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "death metal page (Discarding harmony and nihilistically embracing the chromatic scale as law, early death metal bands espoused beliefs in the evil and orderless, the chaotic and the painful. Their rhythmic violence and insistence upon wildly-constructed and atonal guitar solos made them an instant target of both critique and shameless ripoff. By 1992 the peak had been reached, and afterwards soundalikeness pervaded all but the most individually-conceived bands. The overuse of death metal's nihilistic inventions -- chromatic open phrasing and chaotic soloing -- had made that genre, like hardcore punk a decade before, the anti-commercial musical breakdown that in the end made it easier for ripoffs to dress up rock n roll in new production values to create a new product flow to meet a genre-identified need.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The First Bosnian Metal WWW Site, 21 May 2003:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(the definition death metal was called into being because of the drift of the lyrics - death in all its shapes - and the death rasp which the \"singers\" use)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Death: more than death metal WWW Site, 21 May 2003", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Death Metal grew out of the thrash metal in the late '80s. Taking the gritty lyrics and morbid obsessions of thrash to extremes, death metal was--as its name suggests--solely about death, pain, and suffering. These relentlessly bleak lyrics were set to loud, heavy riffs that owed as much to the lumbering metal of Black Sabbath as it did to Metallica)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "AMG All Music Guide WWW Site, 28 May 2003", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n1be90d759bf949579e3d8bfd5dded99db8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. i (Death metal is the leviathan of Heavy Metal taken to its most mind-numbing extreme. Even though death metal bands operate on the very perimeters of the genre, the scope of musical variety is wide ranging. In general, the style relies on acts pursuing faster more aggressive patterns. Although this is apparent within the ferocity of the guitars and the almost insane pummelling of drums, it is the vocals that personify Death Metal. Vocalists use their throats as tortured instruments to such an extent that very often the guttural growls are completely unintelligible)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Sharpe-Young, G. Rockdetector A-Z of death metal, 2001:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026761"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026762", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aD\u00e9cimas (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026762", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b1"}, {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "D\u00e9cimas (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027103"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b5"}, {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b7"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "D\u00e9cimas (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(D\u00e9cima. A verse form, commonly sung, comprising ten lines (rhyme scheme abbaaccddc), which develops a theme introduced by a quatrain (rhymed abab). Textual material may be set or improvised, religious or secular.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 12, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 18 (Spanish playwright Lope de Vega referred to the decima poetic form as espinela; they since have become synonymous)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Los siete siglos de la espinela, 2000:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Espinelas (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n3c74790c57304ebd920d85cac8e7edb2b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Espinelas (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026762"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026763", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDhrupad", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026763", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b1"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhrupad"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026859"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027103"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b5"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b6"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b7"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b8"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b9"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b10"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b11"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b12"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b13"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b16"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b19"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhrupad"}}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "t.p. (Dhruvapada; variant: Dhruvapad)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dhruvapada aura usak\u0101 vik\u0101sa, 1976:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Web. 3", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 272."}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Neuman, D.M. Life of Music in North India, c1980:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dhrupad; a type of vocal composition in North Indian art-music; has two or four rhymed lines of verse, in Hindi, usually on religious (Hindu or Islamic) or philosophical themes; set to music in any classical mode and in one of a number of special metres; sung by one or two singers, usually male; accompaniment is provided by the drone lute tamb\u016br\u0101 and the barrel drum pakh\u0101vaj)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 29, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pp. 11, 21, 24."}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Kaufmann, W. Ragas of North India, c1968:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Dagar Brothers. Rag kambhoji [SR] p1989.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dhruvapada see Dhrupada; usage: Dhrupad)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Bhargava's standard ill. dict. of the Hindi lang. Hindi-English ed.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhrupada"}}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhrupada"}}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhruvapada"}}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhruvapada"}}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhruvapad"}}, {"@id": "_:n055c1728e07b417da6a24d1e7d2c6196b20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhruvapad"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026763"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026764", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDhuns", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026764", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b1"}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhuns"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026859"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b5"}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b6"}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b7"}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b8"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dhuns"}}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"DHUN: A light tune, free from the discipline of a Raga.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "WWW santoor, glossary", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"A dhun in instrumental music corresponds to thumri in singing. It is a form of light classical music emphasizing the emotional expression of mystic love.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Inde, la voix du sit\u00e2r, 200u:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Bengali Music, Key Figures in Bengali Music (\"The compositional style aimed at entertaining filmgoers and record-buyers and grew out of a combination of many genres, such as khay\u0101l, tapp\u0101, thumr\u012b, ghazal, dhun and k\u012brtan.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove Online:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf52a93c8a10e4d7bbb18de9d4256d2c9b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Dhun: A light classical piece normally played in instrumental form based on a folk melody.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "WWW gurbani, glossary", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026764"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026765", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDialogues (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026765", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b1"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dialogues (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026639"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026775"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b5"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b6"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b7"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b9"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dialogues (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Noske, F. Saints and sinners, 1993.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dialogue; sacred recitative dialogue)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Harvard dict.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Dialogue (As applied to music, used in two general senses: to denote the setting of a text involving conversational exchanges between two or more characters; and to describe a musical work (or part of a work) that uses devices such as alternation, echo or contrast in a way that seems analogous to spoken dialogue; in the second sense \"dialogue\" has been used as a title for certain instrumental works (particularly for organ) that exploit contrasts in tone colour; used in its first sense, is now most frequently encountered in connection with the dialogue of opera and other stage works; during the 16th and 17th centuries also used more specifically to denote the independent dialogue settings included in collections of madrigals, motets and cantatas)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of mus. online, July 10, 2002:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dialogue, Sacred)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Recitative dialogues"}}, {"@id": "_:n902099fb8cab48bd99f8cc7c6841e367b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Recitative dialogues"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026765"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026766", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDikir barat", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026766", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b1"}, {"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dikir barat"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027103"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b5"}, {"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b6"}, {"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b7"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dikir barat"}}, {"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Jasman Ahmad. Dikir barat, 1996.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "types of music in Malaysia/independent songs (Dikir barat is a style of call and response singing originating in Kelantan)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Musical Malaysia, via WWW, Aug. 1, 2002:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n3baf1a63243e452581063d9ffe5c83e9b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Singapore (Dikir barat; secular vocal genre, performed by a vocal ensemble with instruments such as the kompang, bonang, and gong; Malay)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 29, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026766"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026767", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDirges (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026767", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ndb6abb13b55b4c0690ac7f1ccdb7257ab1"}, {"@id": "_:ndb6abb13b55b4c0690ac7f1ccdb7257ab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dirges (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndb6abb13b55b4c0690ac7f1ccdb7257ab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026822"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027103"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:ndb6abb13b55b4c0690ac7f1ccdb7257ab5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:ndb6abb13b55b4c0690ac7f1ccdb7257ab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ndb6abb13b55b4c0690ac7f1ccdb7257ab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ndb6abb13b55b4c0690ac7f1ccdb7257ab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dirges (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:ndb6abb13b55b4c0690ac7f1ccdb7257ab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(A burial song or (less commonly) one sung in commemoration of the dead; a song of mourning or an instrumental piece expressive of similar sentiments. The word is a contraction of 'dirige', the first word of the first antiphon in the first nocturn at Matins in the Roman Office for the Dead ('Dirige, Domine Deus meus, in conspectu tuo viam meam'). When, as often happened, the invitatorium ('Venite, exsultemus Domino') was omitted, the office would begin directly with the antiphon, and so in late medieval English the word 'dirge' came to be used in reference to the service as a whole. However, as in the similar case of 'placebo' (the initial word at Vespers in the same Office for the Dead), it soon took on a more general meaning and could be used for any song in the vernacular sung at a burial. In this sense a dirge has much the same connotation as a Threnody or a lament, though each term carries its own shade of meaning. The dirge has perhaps the most doleful character of them all; it is more specifically associated with the time of burial and often has a march-like tread, reminiscent of a funeral procession. As a poetic form the dirge is peculiarly English, and most settings of dirges are therefore by British composers. )"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed May 30, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026767"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026768", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDisco (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026768", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ndfe39b0d90524fe3ad52e112e824499db1"}, {"@id": "_:ndfe39b0d90524fe3ad52e112e824499db2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Disco (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndfe39b0d90524fe3ad52e112e824499db3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027009"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:ndfe39b0d90524fe3ad52e112e824499db5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:ndfe39b0d90524fe3ad52e112e824499db1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ndfe39b0d90524fe3ad52e112e824499db2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ndfe39b0d90524fe3ad52e112e824499db3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Disco (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:ndfe39b0d90524fe3ad52e112e824499db5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Disco. A form of dance music that dominated popular music during the late-1970s. It features regular bass drum accents on every beat, frequent use of orchestral instruments and synthesizers, Latin percussion, and simple lyrics oriented around dancing, romance and a party-led life style. The term derives from discoth\u00e8que: venues that began playing pre-recorded dance music in the early 1960s.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 28, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026768"}
