u (Songs)"}}, {"@id": "_:n09de5412d51147f1a5e4a7cec87f3245b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dodoitsu)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hennepin", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n09de5412d51147f1a5e4a7cec87f3245b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 378 (dodoitsu)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Toyotaka, K. Japanese music and drama in the Meiji era, 1956:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n09de5412d51147f1a5e4a7cec87f3245b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 551 (Dodoitsu)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n09de5412d51147f1a5e4a7cec87f3245b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dodoitsu: popular light shamisen song (love song) of the geishas)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Kaufmann, W. Selected musical terms of non-Western cultures, 1990", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n09de5412d51147f1a5e4a7cec87f3245b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a form of Japanese poetry; often concerning love or work, and usually comical; poems consist of 4 lines with the syllabic structure 7-7-7-5 and no rhymes)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed December 5, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026771"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026772", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDondang sayang", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026772", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb1"}, {"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dondang sayang"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027103"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb5"}, {"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb6"}, {"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb7"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dondang sayang"}}, {"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Low, Kim Chuan. Dondang sayang in Melaka, 1976.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "types of music in Malaysia/syncretic forms (The dondang sayang or love ballad originated in Melaka sometime in the 15th century)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Musical Malaysia, via WWW, Aug. 1, 2002:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n78d92874da154b22aecc58ccbd6a8a7eb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Malaysia (Dondang sayang; \"song of love or affection; one performer sings a four-line pantun and a second singer answers with another pantun. The pantuns are created spontaneously; the subject-matter may focus on love, wisdom, courtesy, jokes, advice, food etc. An entertaining and convivial atmosphere is created in the performance through the cajoling and teasing repartee) under Singapore. 2, Malays (a vocal repartee genre based on Malay poetry) under Singapore. 4, Eurasians and Peranakans (the vocal genre based on pantun, the Malay poetic form, and accompanied by rebana (frame drums), violin and gong)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 29, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026772"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026773", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDoo-wop", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026773", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b1"}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doo-wop"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027052"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b5"}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b6"}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b8"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doo-wop"}}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(doo-wop; a style of vocal rock-and-roll popular in the 1950's and early 1960's, essentially unaccompanied, close harmony singing)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of Amer. mus.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(du-wop)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "OCLC, 10/28/92", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "labels (doowop) container (doo wop)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 20 great doowop recordings [SR], 198-:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doowop"}}, {"@id": "_:nf53ab4afdb2b477a8a03cfd8010c74d5b9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doowop"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026773"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026774", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDoom metal (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026774", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b1"}, {"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doom metal (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026854"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b5"}, {"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b6"}, {"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b7"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Doom metal (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Inspired largely by the lumbering dirges and stoned, paranoid darkness of Black Sabbath, doom metal is one of the very few heavy metal subgenres to prize feel and mood more than flashy technique (though the latter can certainly be present). Even more indebted to Sabbath than most metal, doom metal is extremely slow, sludgy, and creepy, feeling so heavy it can barely move; its deliberate pace and murky guitars are meant to evoke (what else?) a sense of impending doom. The movement began to take shape in the mid-'80s,...\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "All music guide WWW site, Sept. 30, 2010", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Doom metal is an extreme form of heavy metal music that typically uses slower tempos, low-tuned guitars and a much 'thicker' or 'heavier' sound than other metal genres. Both the music and the lyrics intend to evoke a sense of despair, dread, and impending doom. The genre is strongly influenced by the early work of Black Sabbath, who formed a prototype for doom metal with songs such as \"Black Sabbath\" and \"Into the Void\". During the first half of the 1980s, a number of bands from England (Pagan Altar, Witchfinder General), the United States (Pentagram, Saint Vitus, Trouble) and Sweden (Candlemass) defined doom metal as a distinct genre.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Sept. 30, 2010", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n26eb172fba244c668310b5fa45358c44b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Body (Musical group : Rhode Island). All the waters of the earth turn to blood [SR] 2010", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026774"}
