lk songs)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed August 29, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026776"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026777", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDrum circles", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026777", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb1"}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Drum circles"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026795"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb5"}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb6"}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb7"}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb8"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Drum circles"}}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove Music online ed.;New Harvard dict. of music;Web. 3", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Drum circle; any group of people playing (usually) hand-drums and percussion in a circle; it is an end in itself rather than preparation for a performance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed August 29, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "ECIP t.p. (Drum circle) data view (Drum circles are a form of recreational music making, which means that the focus is not on performance but rather on personal or group development and wellness, or just plain fun ... A drum circle can be simply defined as \"a group of people working together to create in-the-moment music using drums and percussion instruments.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2004013016: Kalani. Together in rhythm, 2004:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n67af53f6a07e4f719905c8323c12cf5fb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(A community music-making event where people sit or stand in a circle while playing world percussion instruments. People play improvised rhythms. Music is created in the moment)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Mandrake Press WWW page, June 3, 2004", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026777"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026778", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDub (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026778", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb1"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dub (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027045"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb5"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb6"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb7"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb8"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb9"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb10"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb12"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb15"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dub (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "ECIP galley, ch. 4 (There are at least three phases of dub's evolution. In the first phase, dub was one-off acetates with the vocals taken out, which were created around 1967. These instrumental singles were not produced for mass consumption but for sound systems to play at dances. The second phase of dub emerges in the latter part of 1968 [as] studio engineers became musicians actively creating a new music by remixing the A side of a record. These versions became the B side, replacing the old idea of having two singles on a record. The remixed version enabled particular instruments such as the organ or bass to dominate the rhythm track and also allow punchy sayings or chants to punctuate the mix. The third phase of dub arrived in 1972 in the form of sophisticated stand-alone mixes.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Jesus dub, 2006:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dub (music) (Dub is a genre of music which grew out of reggae music in the 1960s, and is commonly considered a subgenre, though it has developed to extend beyond the scope of reggae. Music in this genre consists predominantly of instrumental remixes of existing recordings and is achieved by significantly manipulating and reshaping the recordings, usually by removing the vocals from an existing music piece, emphasizing the drum and bass parts (this stripped down track is sometimes referred to as a 'riddim'). Other techniques include dynamically adding extensive echo, reverb, panoramic delay, and occasional dubbing of vocal or instrumental snippets from the original version or other works; dub reggae)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Feb. 9, 2012:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dub (music). Dub is characterized as a \"version\" of an existing song, typically emphasizing the drums and bass; instrumental tracks are typically drenched in sound processing effects, with most of the lead instruments and vocals dropping in and out of the mix. The music sometimes features processed sound effects and other noises, such as animal sounds, babies crying, and producers shouting instructions at the musicians. A major reason for producing multiple versions was economic: A record producer could use a recording he owned to produce numerous versions from a single studio session. [The dub] version was also an opportunity for a producer or remix engineer to experiment and vent their more creative side. The version was typically the B-side of a single, with the A-side dedicated to making a popular hit, and B-side for experimenting and providing something for DJ's to talk over.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Reference.com WWW site, Jan. 31, 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Sly & Robbie. Dub reggae [SR] p1999.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dub (ii). A technique of reggae in which records are remixed to create new backing tracks for improvised vocal solos (\"toasting\"). The remixing of records may include such techniques as the adding of sound delay or reverberation, and sound effects may also be incorporated.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove 2nd ed. WWW site, Jan. 31, 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2006001787: Beckford, R. Jesus dub, 2006.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Reggae dub [SR] c2001.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dub reggae"}}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dub reggae"}}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Reggae dub"}}, {"@id": "_:n8169eef6b4014572b307e58fbc1fd5cfb16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Reggae dub"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026778"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026779", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDubstep (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026779", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b1"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dubstep (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027153"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b5"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b6"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b7"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b8"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b9"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b10"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b12"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b15"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2018-07-13T13:04:08"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dubstep (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(sound recording titles: Adventures in dubstep & beyond; Aotearoa dubstep allstarz; Chilled deep dub step; Dubstep #1; Dubstep 2007; Dubstep allstars; Dubstep invasion; Dubstep madness; Enter the dubstep; Generation bass presents Transnational dubstep; I love dubstep; Now is the future dubstep; The roots of dubstep; Scientist launches dubstep into outer space; Sound of dubstep)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "OCLC, Dec. 1, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(As Skrillex, Sonny Moore has infiltrated the US charts; he is the figurehead for the current unprecedented explosion of electronic dance music - including a high-sugar, hyperactive version of dubstep - into the middle American mainstream; many fans of an underground UK genre have not taken kindly to a Los Angeleno Korn fan who dresses like an emo kid, and who used to be the singer for the screamo band From First to Last, taking dubstep to daytime radio and the pop charts; his electro house and amped-up dubstep sound has found its way into the fabric of American subculture in a way no other rave genre has before)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Muggs, J. Is Skrillex the most hated man in dubstep?, in The guardian, 29 Sept. 2011, viewed online Dec. 1, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Skrillex. Scary monsters and nice sprites [SR], p2010.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pp. 87-88 (dubstep; dubstep is unique in the last 20 years in taking the appreciation of bass to the level of obsession; dubstep unravelled Garage into stark, brooding instrumentals; nurtured in South London; began to emerge as a club phenomenon in 2005; borrowing organically from all underground forms, dubstep pumps fresh blood through the ossifying structures of dance culture)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Wire primers : a guide to modern music, 2009:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(dubstep: a kind of electronic dance music with a strong beat and a lot of bass)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Open dictionary, via Macmillan dictionary website, Dec. 1, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dubstep. A genre of electronic dance music described as tightly coiled productions with overwhelming bass lines and reverberant drum patterns, clipped samples, and occasional vocals that has its roots in London's early 2000s UK garage scene and became extremely popular in 2010.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dubstep music, via Last.fm website, Dec. 1, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dubstep; genre of electronic dance music that originated in south London, England. Its overall sound has been described as \"tightly coiled productions with overwhelming bass lines and reverberant drum patterns, clipped samples, and occasional vocals\"; the earliest dubstep releases date back to 1998 and were darker, more experimental, instrumental dub remixes of 2-step garage tracks attempting to incorporate the funky elements of breakbeat, or the dark elements of drum and bass into 2-step, which featured B-sides of single releases. In 2001, this and other strains of dark garage music began to be showcased and promoted at London's night club Plastic People, at the \"Forward\" night (sometimes stylized as FWD>>), which went on to be considerably influential to the development of dubstep. The term \"dubstep\" in reference to a genre of music began to be used by around 2002, by which time stylistic trends used in creating these remixes started to become more noticeable and distinct from 2-step and grime)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Dec. 1, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dub step (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dub step (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Brostep (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:naff4feced70e4878a3cb44c5c9a15185b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Brostep (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026779"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026780", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDumy (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026780", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b1"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dumy (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026790"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026811"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b5"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b6"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b7"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b9"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:52"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dumy (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(duma, dumas)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Encyc. of Ukraine", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 19 (duma, dumy)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of music and musicians:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(duma, dumy)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "\u010cy\u017eevskyj, D. A hist. of Ukrainian lit., 1975", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Ukraine II, Traditional music, 3. (Traditional melodies may be broadly classified in four ways: formulaic recitative with a narrow pitch range, common in ritual, ceremonial and epic genres; declamatory recitative with a non-strophic structure, used for dumy...) under Kolessa, Filaret (Mykhaylovych) ( In 1908 he carried out a monumental expedition to record Ukrainian historical epic songs (dumy), published in meticulous musical transcriptions in 1910 and 1913, later laying the basis for asserting their folk origins by showing their relation to folk laments.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 23, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dumas (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n1763790996fc4f27b542a43538741807b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dumas (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026780"}
