oul (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n43ebdb9d827046e190f03a3d793fad8fb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "data view (Sound of Philadelphia)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2004015648: Jackson, J.A. The rise and fall of Philadelphia soul, 2004:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n43ebdb9d827046e190f03a3d793fad8fb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Philadelphia (or Philly) soul is a style of soul music characterized by lush instrumental arrangements often featuring sweeping strings and horns. The result is a much smoother sound compared to the more funky and gritty Southern and deep soul styles; there is an emphasis on sound and arrangement with players being relatively anonymous and thus is often considered a producers' genre)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Freedictionary WWW site, July 2, 2004", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n43ebdb9d827046e190f03a3d793fad8fb7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n43ebdb9d827046e190f03a3d793fad8fb8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sound of Philadelphia (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n43ebdb9d827046e190f03a3d793fad8fb8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sound of Philadelphia (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n43ebdb9d827046e190f03a3d793fad8fb10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n43ebdb9d827046e190f03a3d793fad8fb11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Philly soul (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n43ebdb9d827046e190f03a3d793fad8fb11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Philly soul (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026998"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026999", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aPhl\u0113ng thao", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014026999", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b1"}, {"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Phl\u0113ng thao"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026639"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b5"}, {"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:57"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Phl\u0113ng thao"}}, {"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 4, p. 279 (under Thailand: Phleng thao: in music, refers to a composition played continuously in three rhythmic densities, sam chan (third), sawng chan (second), and chan dio (first), with or without singing; originated during the mid-1800s and reached their zenith during first three decades of 20th cent.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Garland encyc. of world music:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n39aa2f5eced040cdaf6aaedd0c8fc915b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Thailand, Classical music, Genres: Phl\u0113ng thao: tripartite form created in mid-19th cent.; begins in slow tempo level (s\u0101m chan) and proceeds to medium (so\u031c\u0304ng chan), then fast tempo (chan dieo), usually ending with fast coda; each tempo level consists of one or more sections based on same principal melody and ending with same cadence; so\u031c\u0304ng chan sections consist mostly of original tunes extracted from the old phl\u0113ng ruang, which are extended in slow section, while fast section is a condensed version; may be performed by any kind of ensemble as entertainment music with or without vocalist)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove, 2nd ed. WWW site, Feb. 4, 2002", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026999"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027000", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aPibrochs", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027000", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b1"}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Pibrochs"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027156"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b5"}, "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b6"}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b9"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:57"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Pibrochs"}}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Pibroch [Gael. p\u00ecobaireachd, piping]. A type of solo Scottish bagpipe music, also termed ce\u00f2l mor (great music), consisting of highly figured variations on a theme, called an urlar.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Harvard dictionary of music, 4th ed.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b6", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b7"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ce\u00f2l mor"}}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b7", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ce\u00f2l mor"}}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "P\u00ecobaireachd"}}, {"@id": "_:nb62ced9f154d4a118962501a1230d9e3b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "P\u00ecobaireachd"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027000"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027001", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aPilgrimage music", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027001", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b1"}, {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Pilgrimage music"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026795"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027192"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027193"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b5"}, {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b7"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Sacred and secular songs and other music of pilgrims and pilgrimage."}, {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T10:53:13"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Pilgrimage music"}}, {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Pilgrimage: Pilgrimage provides a metaphor for life's sacred journey for religions throughout the world. Music is used not only to contextualize and to articulate the different stages of the pilgrim's journey, but to facilitate the spiritual and physical transformation of the pilgrim's life. Pilgrimage music metaphorically traces the passage of a human being through life and, upon completing the sacred journey, pilgrims re-experience the event through subsequent performance of pilgrimage repertories. In most religions pilgrimage repertories are distinct from other sacred repertories and, when they are performed, are especially powerful forms of remembering the genealogies of those who have passed along the path. Pilgrimage repertories depend on mass participation and therefore they use familiar forms (e.g. hymns) and repetitive structures (e.g. Buddhist mantras). Mass participation, moreover, involves the entire body, not only in the passage along the sacred journey and in processions at the shrine, but also in dance and other forms of ritualized expression. The metaphors of pilgrimage also lend themselves, through stylization and dissemination, to popular and art musics. Metaphors of the sacred journey in the African diaspora pervade blues, gospel and reggae repertories. In addition music representing pilgrimage spills from sacred to secular genres, accompanying processes of sacralization. The music of modern and postmodern pilgrimage often historicized the political response to rising nationalism, racism and political repression.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "label (Pilgerlieder) booklet (full title: Pilgerlieder des Sp\u00e4tmittelalters und der Renaissance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2006568160: Corbett, S. Bentlager Liturgie, 1999:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Pilgrim music"}}, {"@id": "_:n043e118e456e4cdeb400a88522189bf7b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Pilgrim music"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027001"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027002", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aPolkabilly music", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027002", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b1"}, {"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Polkabilly music"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026739"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026809"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b5"}, {"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:57"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Polkabilly music"}}, {"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "data view (distinctive regional style of folk music begun in Wisc. and spreading to Upper Midwest; contains elements of dance and song representing various northern European countries, British Isles, and American folk and country music)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2005023081: Leary, J.P. The Goose Island Ramblers redefine American folk music, 2006:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n051559289895454db4d6811d1beeec20b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(part polka, part hillbilly, polkabilly is a vigorous fusion of such European couple dances as the polka, waltz, and schottische with the jigs and reels of Anglo-Celtic settlers; is a blend of musical and ethnic dialects)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wisconsin history WWW site, Aug. 9, 2005:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027002"}
