5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Saibara is a genre of accompanied vocal Japanese court music that existed during the Heian period in the region of Nara and Kyoto. It draws from traditional folk music (=fuzoku) from the Nara period accompanied by togaku instruments, save for uchimono, which are replaced by Shakubyoshi)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Aug. 11, 2009", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5ba26716d43f48d5ac9a291352756e83b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Japan, V: Court music. Gagaku, the ancient traditional music of the Japanese court, today comprises the following repertories: t\u014dgaku, komagaku, saibara, r\u014dei and Shint\u014d ritual music and dance ... During the Heian period (794-1185), gagaku flourished under court patronage as part of a rich calendar of ceremonies and festivals. Performances were by both high-ranking noblemen and the professional musicians who staffed the Gakusho (Gakuso), the new government department established in the early to mid-10th century to regulate the performance of music and dance at court. The Heian period also saw the creation of a number of new genres, including two that survive to the present: saibara ($a Originally Japanese folk-texts set to the melodies of t\u014dgaku and komagaku) and r\u014dei (a tradition of singing Chinese poetry))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove Music Online, WWW site, August 11, 2009", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5ba26716d43f48d5ac9a291352756e83b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. xvii (The term Saibara refers to a genre of measured Japanese Court Song, traditionally believed to derive from native folk-song, and known to have existed as part of the Court Repertory at least since 859 A.D. when the death of Princess Hiroi, a singer of Saibara is recorded. Songs of this kind appear to have been sung both by men and women, and served ... as entertainment music, practised by noble amateurs)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 82004326: Markham, E.J. Saibara, 1983:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027064"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027065", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSainetes (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027065", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b1"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sainetes (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026876"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2016026059"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b5"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b6"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b7"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b8"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b9"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b10"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b11"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b12"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b13"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b14"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b17"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b20"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b23"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b26"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2016-06-02T12:44:37"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sainetes (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Zarzuela: g\u00e9nero grande (\"large style\" zarzuelas in three acts) gave way [in the latter half of the 19th century] to g\u00e9nero chico (\"small style,\" one-act farces, often with social or political satire and containing less music))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxf. comp. mus., 2002", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Zarzuela: the modern type of zarzuela, known as genero chico, embodied elements of the Viennese operetta, and still later annexed American jazz rhythms)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Baker's dict. mus.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Zarzuela: the continual demand for new works made the one-act zarzuelas referred to as g\u00e9nero chico (small genre) more practical)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Harvard dict. mus., 4th ed.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Sainete: Short Spanish theatrical piece, at first equivalent of the entrem\u00e9s (intermezzo), a little play between acts; came to be called sainete when intended for the end of a performance after the main play rather than in the middle of it. 17th century: usually a little spoken piece which could also include musical numbers. 18th century: musical numbers came to be deemed essential. Late 19th century sainete and sainetillo also used to describe short, comic theatrical pieces without music, with a highly farcical plot bearing little relation to reality; when after 1870 the g\u00e9nero chico type of zarzuela developed, many composers called their pieces sainete or its diminutive sainetillo. Tom\u00e1s Bret\u00f3n's g\u00e9nero chico piece La verbena de la paloma (1894) and Ruperto Chap\u00ed's La revoltosa (1897), for instance, were described by their publishers as sainetes, but during the period dozens of words were used to describe theatrical pieces with no specific significance. In the late 19th century the words sainete and sainetillo were also used to describe short, comic theatrical pieces without music, with a highly farcical plot bearing little relation to reality. In France, composers such as Herv\u00e9 and Planquette, among others, wrote lightweight sayn\u00e8tes)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove dict. of opera WWW site, Jan. 20, 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(g\u00e9nero chico; zarzuela chica)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LC database, Jan. 20, 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCCN 2005422335: Moral Ruiz, C. El g\u00e9nero chico : ocio y teatro en Madrid (1880-1910), c2004.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Zarzuela: A type of shorter zarzuela, usually in one act--the so-called g\u00e9nero chico--developed after the revolution of 1868. Its main characteristics were extended dialogue and a relatively small amount of music; the plots were mostly set in the working-class districts of Madrid, and composers drew on such popular music as the schottische and the mazurka, which the madrile\u00f1os had come to regard as part of their folklore. The turn of the century almost coincided with a renewal in the ranks of zarzuela composers. The elder ones were almost all gone by 1910 and the g\u00e9nero chico started a speedy decline, despite some late landmarks such as Chap\u00ed's El pu\u00f1a de rosas (1902))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online WWW site, Jan. 20, 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Zarzuela: \"G\u00e9nero chico\" usually refers to one-act plays with music, premiered from 1868 to 1910 or 1915, performed by the hour at certain Madrid theaters; the sainete l\u00edrico is the most representative; the designation \"sainete\" for a short comic zarzuela appears in 1867 in Ramos Carri\u00f3n y Arrieta's play El fiel enamorado, which is not a true sainete; historians agree that the first sainete is La canci\u00f3n de Lola, by Ricardo de la Vega, with music by Chueca and Valverde)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Diccionario de la m\u00fasica espa\u00f1ola e hispanoamericana", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(G\u00e9nero chico: Spanish genre of short light dramas; subgenre of zarzuela, the Spanish operetta; differs from zarzuela grande and most other opera forms both by being short and by aiming at a proletarian audience. The main model for g\u00e9nero chico is the sainete l\u00edrico or one-act lyrical farce, thanks to the successful La canci\u00f3n de la Lola by Chueca and Valverde (1880). The sainete, established in its definitive form by Ram\u00f3n de la Cruz, is the direct heir of the comic interludes or brief farces that were previously so popular. These are essentially short, independent pieces, with music and often dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia WWW site, Jan. 20, 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "G\u00e9nero chico (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "G\u00e9nero chico (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sainetellos (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sainetellos (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sainetes l\u00edricos (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sainetes l\u00edricos (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b23", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b24"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sayn\u00e8tes (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b24", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sayn\u00e8tes (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b26", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b27"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Zarzuelas chicas"}}, {"@id": "_:n21ea639712c249fba09a0851059e1866b27", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Zarzuelas chicas"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027065"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027066", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSalsa (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027066", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n8a01cd1a050d4f81a591f78b8022a11ab1"}, {"@id": "_:n8a01cd1a050d4f81a591f78b8022a11ab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Salsa (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8a01cd1a050d4f81a591f78b8022a11ab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027009"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:n8a01cd1a050d4f81a591f78b8022a11ab5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n8a01cd1a050d4f81a591f78b8022a11ab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:58"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n8a01cd1a050d4f81a591f78b8022a11ab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n8a01cd1a050d4f81a591f78b8022a11ab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Salsa (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n8a01cd1a050d4f81a591f78b8022a11ab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(salsa: a lively, vigorous type of contemporary Latin American popular music, blending predominantly Cuban rhythms with elements of jazz, rock, and soul music)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Random House", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027066"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027067", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSaltarellos (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027067", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab1"}, {"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Saltarellos (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab5"}, "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab6"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:58"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Saltarellos (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Saltarello; modern It. salterello; lively dance of Sp. and It. provenance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford dict. of music (Oxford music online), April 1, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab6", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab7"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Salterellos (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n044a348f894044ffb004fea7bb5188dab7", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Salterellos (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027067"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027068", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSaluang jo dendang", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027068", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b1"}, {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Saluang jo dendang"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027103"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b5"}, {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b7"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:58"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Saluang jo dendang"}}, {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "insert, p. 11 (Saluang jo dendang is the name of a musical genre of songs accompanied by bamboo flute; generally speaking ... they call it saluang)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: 2003602011: Saluang music of Sumatera Barat, p1997:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Indonesia. Genres and ensembles (Specialist performance of lyric singing (that is, verses not part of religious or shamanistic observance and without extended narrative) by a soloist, typically accompanied by one instrument, is equally widespread ... A common pattern is for a male singer and a female singer to trade serious or teasing verses, typically on the themes of love and courtship: for example, dendang jo saluang among the Minangkabau, where the singers are accompanied by an oblique flute)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove, 2nd ed. WWW site, Feb 27, 2007:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dendang jo saluang"}}, {"@id": "_:ne0924e1952eb4aee92327adee7d534b9b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dendang jo saluang"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027068"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027069", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSambas (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027069", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b1"}, {"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sambas (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b5"}, {"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b6"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:58"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sambas (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "2001298065: Sandroni, C. Feiti\u00e7o decente : transforma\u00e7\u00f5es do samba no Rio de Janeiro, 1917-1933, c2001.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n56469e4f4ae84a09b9a43d57386a9a78b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Samba: An Afro-Brazilian dance and dance music form ... Characteristics shared among the samba types include ... verses for solo singer alternating with choral refrain...)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Harvard dict. of music", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027069"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027070", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSanjo", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027070", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b1"}, {"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sanjo"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026809"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b5"}, "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b6"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:58"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sanjo"}}, {"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Korea 3, Folk music (A more recent (late 19th-century) virtuoso instrumental form, partly derived from p'ansori, is sanjo, played on a single melody instrument (which may be any of a number of standard Korean string or wind instruments) with Changgo drum accompaniment. Unlike p'ansori, in which rhythmic patterns vary according to the emotional content of the story, sanjo presents four or more rhythmic patterns in ascending order of metrical speed, each movement being set to one rhythmic pattern and the slow movements being more intensely ornamented in melody.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 28, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b6", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b7"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sanzo"}}, {"@id": "_:nacb777ccf6524b17853d87eeff26da52b7", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sanzo"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027070"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027071", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSarabandes (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027071", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b1"}, {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sarabandes (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b5"}, {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b6"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b7"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:58"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sarabandes (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Sarabande. One of the most popular of Baroque instrumental dances and a standard movement, along with the allemande, courante and gigue, of the suite... The French spelling 'sarabande' was also used in Germany and sometimes in England; there, however, 'saraband' was often preferred. The Italian usage is 'sarabanda', the Spanish 'zarabanda'.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed August 28, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Sarabande (Sarabande. [Fr.; also Ger.: It. sarabanda; Sp. Zarabanda]. A Baroque dance movement in triple meter.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Harvard dictionary of music, 4th ed.:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b7", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b8"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sarabands (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5806f13009849b2bbf3206d10324ad2b8", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sarabands (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027071"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027072", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSardanas (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027072", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb1"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sardanas (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb5"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb6"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb7"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb8"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb9"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-02-13T13:06:58"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sardanas (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "2001378695: Havart, M. Una visi\u00f3 nord-catalana de la sardana i la m\u00fasica per a cobla, c2000.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Sardana: The national dance of Catalonia; an elegant and solemnly executed circle-dance performed to the music of the cobla, an ensemble traditionally consisting of flaviol and tambor (one-handed flute and drum), tiple, and tenora (double-reed aerophones), but now with more varied instrumentation including two cornets, fiscorno (flugelhorn), other brass instruments, and string bass. Stylistically the music resembles that of Provence rather than other Spanish music)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grovemusic.com WWW site, July 28, 2003", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(sardana; national dance of Catalonia)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Grove", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 8, p. 1056 (sardana: a Catalonian urban-rural circle dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Garland encyc. world mus.:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6402ec3c3d2841d5911053b0bf59c91bb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "New Harvard dict. mus.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027072"}
