hovuoth music"}}, {"@id": "_:nec5165711cef483c878c37ef2bc8fc05b65", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Shovuoth music"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027193"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027197", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aConspiracy films", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027197", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b1"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Conspiracy films"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026452"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026692"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b5"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b6"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b7"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b8"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b9"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b11"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b14"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b17"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b20"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-03-04T14:24:09"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-18T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Conspiracy films"}}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Donovan, B.W. Conspiracy films, \u00a92011.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: All the President's men [VR], 2006.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 9, p. 197 (The \"paranoid\" conspiracy film. The sense that oppressive forces were at work against individual liberty, and that the law could not protect its citizens from them, was central to another subgenre of 1970s film noir. In both mood and theme, the conspiracy film was a type of paranoid political thriller that placed the blame for American society's corruption on plotters pursuing secret agendas to control national life.) p. 198 (The Parallax View, locus classicus of the conspiracy film in its account of an \"assassination bureau\" that controls the nation's politics through seemingly random political murders) p. 200 (films about conspiracy began to appear in 1973; the first was the theatrical feature Executive Action; All the President's Men (1976) has been called \"the centerpiece of the conspiracy subgenre\") p. 204 (By the end of the decade, the conspiracy subgenre was so well codified that it could be parodied)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "History of the American cinema, 1990- :", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Executive action [VR], \u00a92011.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(conspiracy thriller films; examples listed include: All the President's Men; Angels and Demons; The Assassination Bureau; Blow Out; Capricorn One; The China Syndrome; Coma; The Constant Gardener; The Conversation; The Da Vinci Code; Executive Action; The Fugitive; I Witness; The Insider; The Interpreter; The Long Kiss Goodnight; The Manchurian Candidate; Marathon Man; Network; The Parallax View; Seven Days in May; The Stepford Wives; The X-Files; Z)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "List of conspiracy-thriller films and television series, via Wikipedia, Dec. 18, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Conspiracy Film (Conspiracy Thriller, Conspiration Film, Paranoid Thriller, Political Conspiracy Film, Political Paranoid Thriller, Poltical Thriller). The term conspiracy film covers films which deal with political or terrorist assassinations, government cover-ups and conspiracies in general; paranoid fear that unknown enemies may strike at any time, the endangered person not even aware of what motivates the attack, or without any means of defense against the invisible threat, is the main theme governing many conspiracy films. See also Political Thriller)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "L\u00f3pez, D. Films by genre, \u00a91993", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Political conspiracy films"}}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Political conspiracy films"}}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Paranoid thrillers (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Paranoid thrillers (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Political paranoid thrillers (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Political paranoid thrillers (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Conspiracy thrillers (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:n5ff8ae8b19084ad1805fc71767940e71b21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Conspiracy thrillers (Motion pictures)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027197"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027199", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aPunk films", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027199", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb1"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Punk films"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026539"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026619"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb5"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb6"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb7"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb8"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb9"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb11"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-18T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-03-04T14:24:09"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Punk films"}}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(films about the punk subculture)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "List of punk films, via Wikipedia, Dec. 18, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(subgenre listed under Rock film: Punk Films. Punk defines a youth subculture which derives from and has as its core the world of rock music. Punk is also a strand of minimal rock music. Any film which deals with this type of music or its subculture is a punk film. Examples: The Punk Rock Movie (1977); Jubilee (1978); Asphaltnacht (1980); Breaking Glass (1980); A Right of Passage (1980); The Decline of Western Civilization (1981); Liquid Sky (1982); Smithereens (1982); Suburbia (1983); Sid and Nancy (1986))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "L\u00f3pez, D. Films by genre, \u00a91993", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"As musical subgenres go the nihilistic, apocalyptic, hate-fueled punk I knew didn't last long, really only about 15 years from the mid-'70s to the late '80s, but it both inspired and was inspired by a wider range of films than any other musical trend can lay claim to. ... The whole style of punk rock, the sound and the look (the look especially often coming directly from films), also inspired other filmmakers, who found it an easy and colorful way to put youthful disaffection up on the screen\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Knipfel, J. 30 must see punk rock movies, via Den of geek! website, viewed Dec. 18, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "The punk rock movie [VR], c2006.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Punk rock has been a part of cinema ever since the music was created in places like Los Angeles, New York and London in the mid to late '70s. Aside from the music, either live or on record, the big screen provided another medium to convey what punk truly stands for: not giving a fuck what anyone else says or thinks, having integrity, staying true to your identity, and rebelling against the status quo. Pump Up the Volume (1990); Control (2007); SLC Punk (1998); Sid and Nancy (1986); SLC Punk(1998); Rock n Roll High School (1979); Suburbia (1983); Repo Man (1984); Summer of Sam (1999); The Runaways (2010); What We Do Is Secret (2007))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Distefano, A. The 10 best punk rock movies, via OC weekly, Aug. 4 2014, viewed online Dec. 18, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: The decline of western civilization [VR], c1985.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Punk rock films"}}, {"@id": "_:n007e350d37d64792ad2f45a5e4417eebb12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Punk rock films"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027199"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027202", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aKammerspiel films", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027202", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b1"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kammerspiel films"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026406"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b5"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b6"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b7"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b8"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b9"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b10"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b11"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b12"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b13"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b14"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b15"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b18"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b21"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b24"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Films produced in 1920s Germany that focus on the everyday lives of one or a few characters in an intimate, naturalistic, and psychological manner."}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-12-14T11:05:29"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-26T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kammerspiel films"}}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Beaver, F.E. Dictionary of film terms, c2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Kammerspielfilm; a type of German film that offers an intimate, cinematic portrait of lower middle class life. The name derives from a theater, the Kammerspiele, opened in 1906 by a major stage director Max Reinhardt to stage intimate dramas for small audiences. Few Kammerspiel films were made, but nearly all are classics.[2] Kammerspielfilme (the plural form) formed a German film movement of the 1920s silent film period that was developed around the same time as the more commonly known Expressionist movement in cinema. The Kammerspielfilm was known as the \"chamber drama\" as a result of the influence from the theatrical form of the chamber play. It is characterised by its focus on character psychology and its lack of intricate set design. Also, unlike Expressionist films, Kammerspielfilme seldom used intertitles to narrate the story)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Dec. 30, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Variety (Motion picture : 1925). Variety [VR], c2010.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Letzte Mann (Motion picture). Der letzte Mann [VR], 2001.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Kammerspiel; Kammerspiel or \"Chamber Drama\" Film was a distinct trend in German filmmaking during the 1920s. The name \"Kammerspiel\" can be traced back to a German theatre of the same name that was opened by stage director Max Reinhardt in 1906. Kammerspiel films contrasted greatly from the German Expressionist films that were popular at the time. Rather than placing emphasis on fantasy, spectacle and extreme emotion, Kammerspiel focused on one or a few characters, detailing a problem or conflict in their everyday lives. These films concentrated on character psychology and featured slow, methodical acting, contemporary settings and unhappy endings. Kammerspiel films contrasted greatly with the German Expressionist films that were being made during the same period. Expressionism emphasized fantasy, horror, extreme emotion, distortion and exaggeration. Expressionist films often took place in the past or in exotic locations. Kammerspiel, on the other hand, was characterized by slow, deliberate acting, intricate character psychology and emphasis on a few characters and a conflict in their daily lives. Kammerspiel films were often set in mundane, everyday surroundings and took place over a short amount of time. Kammerspiel films are also characterized by a lack of intertitles. The audience must rely on actors and setting to understand the unfolding narrative. Almost all the Kammerspiel films have unhappy endings, usually a brutal death)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "CineWiki website, Dec. 30, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 26 (the last grand manifestation of Kammerspielfilms, The Last Laugh)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Expressionist film--new perspectives, 2006, viewed online via Google books, Dec. 30, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(With a concentration on a small number of characters and spaces, a focus on inner conflicts and a clear restricted timeframe, the key components of the \"Kammerspiel\" film genre that came about in the 1920s appear ascetic. But often a particular sense of drama emerged from the extreme paucity of place, time and plot that was conveyed and intensified by the subjectifying use of light and by a moving camera, which in the protagonists' immediate vicinity recorded the tiniest changes in gestures and facial expressions)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Magical history tour - \"Kammerspielfilm\", via Arsenal website, Dec. 30, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Kammerspielfilm. The word means \"chamber-play film\" in German and describes a type of silent film made in Germany during the 1920s which was both a development from Max Reinhardt's Kammerspiel theater and a reaction to the expressionist films of the time. These works are intimate, naturalistic, and psychological, with bleak and dark settings. A key feature is the sparse use and sometimes total lack of titles, which results in continuous narrative and greater visual impression than normal while placing more emphasis on characters' facial expressions and behavior. Sometimes this emphasis results in slow and ponderous scenes and overacting, but it also is responsible for some deeply moving images of human emotion. The most notable example of this type of film is Murnau's The Last Laugh (1924))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Konigsberg, I. The complete film dictionary, 1997", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Kammerspielfilm (Chamber Film). The Kammerspiele was a small Berlin theater in which Max Reinhardt (1873-1943) directed intimate \"chamber plays.\" Its influence on German cinema resulted in the Kammerspiel films of the 1920s, a film style which was a reaction against expressionist film, the other concurrent German cinematic style of the epoch. The Kammerspiel films deal with the lives and worries of the humble people and present a psychological analysis of their character. The films are set in a simplified, sparse d\u00e9cor with just a few characters, and the overall impression is naturalistic. As in theater, the films kept the three unities of action, time and place. Hintertreppe (1921); Scherben (1921); Sylvester (1923); Der letzte Mann (1924); Nju (1924); Vari\u00e9t\u00e9 (1925))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "L\u00f3pez, D. Films by genre, \u00a91993", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(This month Arsenal cinema reflects on the Kammerspiel movement. The film genre, which emerged in 1920s Berlin, was named after the chamber theatre built by Deutsches Theater director Max Reinhardt. With its modest and intimate set design, 'chamber theatre' was a concept conceived with the aim of creating unity between the stage and audience. Reinhardt's ideas heavily influenced Kammerspiel film and as such its distinct style is indebted to chamber theatre; with similar spacial and temporal restrictions and a stripped back plot - it explored character psychology and didn't shy away from unhappy endings. Kammerspiel provided a strong contrast to the German Expressionism film which was popular at the time; whilst Expressionism put the spotlight on emotion, exaggeration and fantasy, Kammerspiel brought a dose of realism to post-war cinema)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Kammerspielfilm at Arsenal Cinema, via Don't panic website, Dec. 30, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kammerspielfilme"}}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kammerspielfilme"}}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamber dramas (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamber dramas (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b21", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b22"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamber films"}}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b22", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamber films"}}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b24", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b25"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kammerspielfilms"}}, {"@id": "_:nf28b44b663d149d6ad5e4bdb388afda4b25", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kammerspielfilms"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027202"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027203", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aArrangements (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2014027203", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n4e698d4abf574f1f888434b7ed1379bfb1"}, {"@id": "_:n4e698d4abf574f1f888434b7ed1379bfb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Arrangements (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4e698d4abf574f1f888434b7ed1379bfb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026084"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026952"}], "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027204"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027205"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027206"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027210"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026077"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": {"@id": "_:n4e698d4abf574f1f888434b7ed1379bfb5"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n4e698d4abf574f1f888434b7ed1379bfb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2014-12-31T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n4e698d4abf574f1f888434b7ed1379bfb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2015-03-04T14:24:09"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n4e698d4abf574f1f888434b7ed1379bfb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Arrangements (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n4e698d4abf574f1f888434b7ed1379bfb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The reworking of a musical composition, usually for a different medium from that of the original...In the sense in which it is commonly used among musicians, however, the word may be taken to mean either the transference of a composition from one medium to another or the elaboration (or simplification) of a piece, with or without a change of medium. In either case some degree of recomposition is usually involved, and the result may vary from a straightforward, almost literal, transcription to a paraphrase which is more the work of the arranger than of the original composer.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed December 15, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027203"}
