: "Illustrated literary miscellanies that were given as gifts in the 19th century."}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-07-13T14:02:29"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-04-28T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Gift books"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(gift books. UF books, gift; annual gift books; annuals (gift books); Christmas books; giftbooks; gift-books. BT document genres - <documents by function>)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Art & architecture thesaurus online, Apr. 28, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "The youth's diadem : a gift book for all seasons, 1852.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a gift book)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Sigourney, L.H. The young ladies' offering, or, Gems of prose and poetry, 1848", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "The newest keepsake for 1840, 1840.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "The annualette : a Christmas and New Year's gift, 1845.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Thesaurus for graphic materials, via WWW, Apr. 28, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: The Magnolia, or, Gift-book of friendship, 1852.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Gift books. SA subdivision Gift books under individual plants and groups of plants and individual wars, e.g. Corn--Gift books; World War, 1939-1945--Gift books. UF Annual gift books; Annuals (Gift books); Christmas books; Gift-books (Annuals, etc.); Giftbooks; Keepsakes (Books). BT Anthologies; Souvenirs (Keepsakes))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Apr. 28, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(gift book: An elaborately printed, expensively illustrated, ornately bound book of poetry or prose, usually published annually, popular as a gift item during the early part of the 19th century. Also known as a keepsake. In modern usage, a book purchased as a gift for another person (or persons). Coffee table books are often purchased for this purpose. Also spelled giftbook)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Reitz, J.M. ODLIS : online dictionary for library and information science, viewed Apr. 28, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(giftbook: An elaborately printed and bound book of prose and poetry, frequently an annual, popular in the earlier part of the 19th century. Synonymous with keepsake)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The ALA glossary of library and information science, 1983", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb15", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(giftbook 1 : a book intended for giving away. 2 : an illustrated literary miscellany (as of verse, tales, and sketches) in vogue for gift purposes in the second quarter of the 19th century in the U.S. and published annually in ornamental format --called also annual, keepsake)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam-Webster dictionary online, Apr. 28, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb16", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. xiv (The contents consisted of prose--short stories and essays--and selected poetry often with flowery borders about each page. There were illustrations--engravings or colored plates--dignified in the catalogues as embellishments. These numbered anywhere from one to twenty-five or thirty and were either illustrative of the text, or had no connect whatever with the contents.) p. xxi (The literature of the \"gift-books\" was remarkable for its freedom from the slightest taint of impropriety. There was never a contribution that savored of the lack of refinement. These volumes were intended as gifts ... The list of contributors to the English Annuals included all the foremost writers of the time. ... It was the proper thing to write for these \"tokens.\") p. xxiii (In the United States much the same course was followed by the \"gift-book\" publishers)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Faxon, F.W. Literary annuals and gift books : a bibliography with a descriptive introduction, 1912:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb17", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Gift books. Scope Note: Use for illustrated literary miscellanies, popular in the 19th century, intended to be given as gifts and often published annually. Used For: Annual gift books; Christmas books. Broader Term: Purpose of work (Gathering term; do not assign). History Note: Prior to 1997 was a NT of \"Commemorative works\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Genre terms : a thesaurus for use in rare book and special collections cataloging, via RBMS controlled vocabularies website, Apr. 28, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb18", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Klinefelter, W. A bibliographical check-list of Christmas books, 1937.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Books, Gift"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Books, Gift"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb22", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb23"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Giftbooks"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb23", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Giftbooks"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb25", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb26"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Annuals (Gift books)"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb26", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Annuals (Gift books)"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb28", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb29"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Christmas books"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb29", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Christmas books"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb31", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb32"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Annual gift books"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb32", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Annual gift books"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb34", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb35"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Keepsakes (Gift books)"}}, {"@id": "_:nffa724cea4744c88a574ef4a3bbce34fb35", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Keepsakes (Gift books)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026072"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026073", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aMemory plays", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2017026073", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b1"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Memory plays"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026297"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b5"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b6"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b7"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b8"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b9"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b10"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b11"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b12"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b13"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b14"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b15"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-07-13T14:02:29"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-05-02T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Memory plays"}}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Graves, Warren. The last real summer : a memory play, 1986.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 4, p. 415 (The Glass Menagerie (1944). \"Memory play\" in which Tom Wingfield recalls his life in St. Louis with his mother Amanda)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "McGraw-Hill encyclopedia of world drama, c1972:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(titles: Character motivation and definition through dialog in the memory plays of Harold Pinter; Critical response to The Glass Menagerie, a memory play by Tennessee Williams; The drama of illusion : memory plays in American drama, 1920-40; Dressing memory lane : costuming characters in Warren Leight's memory play, Side man; Crossing the barriers of realism : the unreliable narrator in memory plays; Memorable acts/active rememberers : contemporary Canadian memory plays)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "OCLC, May 2, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Memory Play: The term coined by Tennessee Williams to describe non-realistic dramas, such as The Glass Menagerie, in which the audience experiences the past as remembered by a narrator, complete with music from the period remembered, and images representing the characters' thoughts, fears, emotions, and recollections projected on a scrim in the background)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wheeler, L.K. Literary terms and definitions, via WWW, May 2, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Williams, Tennessee. The glass menagerie, [2000?]", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Literature Study Guides > The Glass Menagerie (The Glass Menagerie; Tennessee Williams; a memory play, and its action is drawn from the memories of the narrator, Tom Wingfield)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "SparkNotes website, May 2, 2017:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Memory Play: A play that focuses on the past as narrated by the main character. Usually, the play is a dramatic representation of the playwright's life -- or at least loosely based upon the playwright's experiences. Some memory plays involve narration throughout (such as the play adaptation of A Christmas Story. Other memory plays begin with a recollection made by the narrator and then shifts into a play without an interrupting narrator. (Tennessee Williams' The Glass Menagerie is an example of this type of memory play))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "ThoughtCo. Plays and drama website, May 2, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "The Glass Menagerie (The Glass Menagerie is a five-character memory play by Tennessee Williams that premiered in 1944) Memory play (Memory play is a play in which a lead character narrates the events of the play, which are drawn from the character's memory. The term was coined by playwright Tennessee Williams, describing his work The Glass Menagerie; the 1970s works of Harold Pinter, including Landscape, Silence, A Kind of Alaska, Betrayal and Old Times have been described by Michael Billington and others as memory plays; Brian Friel's Dancing at Lughnasa is a late 20th-century example of the genre; Da, by Hugh Leonard is another example of a memory play)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, May 2, 2017:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Literature Notes > The Glass Menagerie > About The Glass Menagerie (The structure of the play involves the presentation of the scenes through the memory of one of the characters; The stage directions call for the use of several technical devices in order to convey the idea that this is a memory play. For example, some of the scenes should be presented with some type of net or gauze between the audience and the actors. Or in many places, Williams suggests the use of titles and images to be projected on a scene in order to force or reinforce the idea of memory and to recall certain events that occurred during the time of the play)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "CliffsNotes website, May 2, 2017:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Memory play consists of reminiscent scenes seen through light effects and music. The dramatist uses expressionistic technique in such plays with psychological presentation of characters. The protagonists cling to their happy memories of the past and day dream of happy days to come. The Glass Menagerie by Tennessee Williams is an excellent example. In this memory play the narrator Tom regards his unhappy and troubled mother and sister in a mood of guilty sorry and tender love; the fourth wall of the building is transparent and the curtains are gauze to give the effect of a memory play)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Sebastian, A.J. Literary terms in drama, theatre, and cinema, 2002", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Memory drama"}}, {"@id": "_:n20529fc78c614e749ae42da425e66af9b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Memory drama"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026073"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026077", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aHymn concertatos", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2017026077", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b1"}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Hymn concertatos"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026872"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027203"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b5"}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b6"}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b7"}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b8"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-05-24T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/castsmf"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-09-19T07:30:39"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Hymn concertatos"}}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"A hymn concertato is a hymn that has been arranged for congregation, choir, organ, and various instruments (often brass). ... As in a concerto for solo instrument and orchestra, the hymn concertato contrasts the small choir with the large choir--the congregation.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Reformed Worship, via WWW, May 23, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"A genre of hymn arrangement for choir in which varied treatments of stanzas are written out, all based on the familiar tune, and almost always ending with a verse for the congregation to join on. Organ with brass instruments and/or tympani are the usual accompaniment.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, May 23, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 1, p. xiii (\"Concertatos: arrangements for congregational singing on a given hymntune that include introduction, free harmonizations, choral settings for various stanzas, descants and frequently with brass or other instruments as well as organ.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wasson, D. D. Hymntune Index and Related Hymn Materials, 1998:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n11d125c1c0c0453f9b2dbb65d0d9c239b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "t.p. (Triumphant lamb and Lord of all: hymn concertato)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Kosche, K.T. Triumphant lamb and Lord of all, c1994:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026077"}
