48319a1c04a91480d1edb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "1940-41 season (It happens on ice, a musical icetravaganza; ice revue with original musical numbers interspersed with comedy scenes, all performed by ice skaters)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dietz, D. The complete book of 1940s Broadway musicals, 2015:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5237a35d4b1748319a1c04a91480d1edb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Duke, V. Between you and me and the lamppost, 1940.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5237a35d4b1748319a1c04a91480d1edb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Ice show; an entertainment production which is primarily performed by ice skaters. Such shows may primarily be skating exhibitions, or may be musical and/or dramatic in nature)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, August 28, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n5237a35d4b1748319a1c04a91480d1edb8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n5237a35d4b1748319a1c04a91480d1edb9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ice revue music"}}, {"@id": "_:n5237a35d4b1748319a1c04a91480d1edb9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ice revue music"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026100"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026101", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aMemorial music", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2017026101", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb1"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Memorial music"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026064"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026821"}], "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026114"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb5"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb6"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb7"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb8"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb9"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb10"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb11"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb12"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb13"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb14"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-09-02T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-11-03T13:39:55"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Memorial music"}}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"A staggering number of classical works have been written after, and about, the Sept. 11 attacks. There are string quartets, pieces written for local choruses, commissions from the New York Philharmonic, including on Sept. 30, the world premiere of 'One Sweet Morning,' by John Corigliano. Yes, there have also been pop songs, artworks, plays, novels and dance pieces about the event. But I don't think any other field can show the density of commemorative works that the classical music world has produced. ... In 1960, 'Threnody to the Victims of Hiroshima' helped put the young Krzysztof Penderecki on the map. Benjamin Britten's 'War Requiem' premiered in 1962 for the rededication of bombed Coventry Cathedral, remains a go-to work of commemoration. ... Few memorial works today have the subversive punch of Shostakovich's 13th Symphony, 'Babi Yar' (1962), which commemorated a Nazi slaughter of Jews but was also a not-so-veiled critique of the Soviet regime\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Midgette, A. 9/11 commemorative music should do more than comfort, in Washington post, Aug. 27, 2011, viewed online Sept. 2, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Memorial music. BT Music. BT Sacred music. RT Funeral music)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Sept. 2, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 156 (because it avoids a direct, mimetic, relationship with historical events, commemorative music can easily appear coy when confronted with the ever-suspicious gaze of the historical imagination; the function of music in such circumstances seems to lie precisely in its presumed otherworldliness, in the qualities such as nobility, or theological gravitas, that we imagine it can bestow)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Music in the post-9/11 world, \u00a92007:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Tower, J. In memory : for string quartet, 2005.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(memorial music; music at a loved one's memorial service)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Funeral and memorial music, via Cremation Solutions website, Sept. 2, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(commemorative music dedicated to the Srebrenica genocide)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Commemorating a Bosniak tragedy: music and the creation of the Srebrenica genocide metanarrative, via WWW, Sept. 2, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"This is a collection of practical repertoire for church organists playing at funerals and memorial services\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford book of funeral and memorial music for organ, via Oxford University Press website, Sept. 2, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Joan Tower - In Memory (string quartet) (2002); \"The Tokyo String Quartet commissioned this string quartet composition in 2001. This one-movement piece about death and loss was written in memory of one of my friends, and later, of those who died in the September 11th terrorist attacks\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Music sales classical website, Sept. 2, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "McNees, P. Music for funerals and memorial services, via Pat McNees website, Sept. 2, 2017.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commemorative music"}}, {"@id": "_:ne5f256f6d6624faba43276167143a11eb15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Commemorative music"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026101"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026102", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDemographic surveys", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2017026102", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b1"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Demographic surveys"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b3"}]}, "madsrdf:exampleNote": "Note under [Census data]", "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026113"}, "madsrdf:hasReciprocalAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026059"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b5"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b6"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b7"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b8"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b9"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b10"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b12"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b15"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b18"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Reports about the social, economic, health, or other characteristics of a population that are generally based on estimates calculated through sampling techniques. For official counts of the population of a place or ethnic group that sometimes include data relating to economic and social conditions, see [Census data.]"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-12-14T12:43:11"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-09-07T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Demographic surveys"}}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 4, pp. 508-509 (population or demographic survey; a survey whose main objective could be measurement of population composition and changes, or the understanding and explanation of levels and trends of the demographic phenomena (fertility, nuptiality, mortality, migration); can classify population surveys into a few large categories according to their main objective, e.g., 1. Measurement surveys. 2. Opinion or behavioral surveys. 3. Mixed surveys. 4. Fertility)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Caselli, G. Demography, \u00a92006:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. xiv (nationwide survey of men and women of reproductive age that is designed to provide information on fertility and child mortality levels; fertility preferences; use of family planning methods; maternal, child and newborn health, including breastfeeding practices, nutrition levels, anaemia and the presence of iodine in cooking salt; knowledge and attitudes towards HIV/AIDS and other sexually transmitted infections (STI); and community-level data on accessibility and availability of health and family planning services) p. xvii (nformation on basic demographic and socioeconomic characteristics for all usual residents and visitors (e.g. age, sex, educational attainment and current school attendance) was collected using a household questionnaire)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Vanuatu demographic and health survey 2013, 2014, via WWW, viewed Sept. 7, 2017:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "home page (The Demographic and Health Surveys (DHS) Program has collected, analyzed, and disseminated accurate and representative data on population, health, HIV, and nutrition through more than 300 surveys in over 90 countries) What we do > Demographic and Health Survey (DHS) (Demographic and Health Surveys (DHS) are nationally-representative household surveys that provide data for a wide range of monitoring and impact evaluation indicators in the areas of population, health, and nutrition) Survey process (The time to complete a survey depends on survey type, survey instruments, and sample size. DHS surveys take on average 18-20 months; The first step involves preparatory activities, including designing the sample and developing the survey questionnaires to meet specific host-country needs)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The DHS Program website, Sept. 7, 2017:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Demographic Surveys, History and Methodology of (Demographic surveys are surveys that wholly or primarily collect information on population characteristics and on the causes and consequences of population change. In addition, demographic surveys can be a name given to surveys that contain mostly demographic information although they also contain information of a non-demographic nature. Population censuses attempt to measure characteristics of the total population of a country or territory through the full enumeration of all persons and relevant events. Surveys have emerged as alternatives to census taking with the development of statistical sampling techniques that permit interviewing only a part of the population of interest to obtain estimates that are valid for the population as a whole. Population surveys have a long history, including the 1086 Domesday survey in England. This survey, as well as most other early surveys, was a social survey dealing with living conditions and poverty; Demographic surveys are often taken in conjunction with a census. This was done for the first time in 1940, in the United States)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Encyclopedia of population, 2003, via Gale Virtual Reference Library, Sept. 7, 2017:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Demographic surveys. UF Population surveys. BT Census. BT Social surveys)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Sept. 7, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. I-1 (Data from the 2010 Census; derived from a limited number of basic questions asked of the entire population and about every housing unit) subj. hdgs. (Demographic surveys--Washington (State); Washington (State)--Population--Statistics; United States--Census, 2010)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Washington : 2010 : population and housing unit counts, 2012:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(demographic questions are an important aspect of any survey. Demographic questions are designed to help survey researchers determine what factors may influence a respondent's answers, interests, and opinions; question examples: Age (or birth date); Ethnicity; Education; Household Composition; Professional or Employment Status. Other demographic questions may include religion; household income; number of children in a household; or geographic location)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wyse, S.E. 5 examples of survey demographic questions, via SnapSurveys website, Sept. 7, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Surveys, Population"}}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Surveys, Population"}}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Population surveys"}}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Population surveys"}}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Surveys, Demographic"}}, {"@id": "_:n6187a72ef0664dc798a2fc23a67bb8f5b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Surveys, Demographic"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026102"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026103", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aImpromptus (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2017026103", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b1"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Impromptus (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026639"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b5"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b6"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b7"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b8"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b9"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b10"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b11"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-09-08T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-12-14T12:43:11"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Impromptus (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "container insert (Impromptu, op. 9 / Yu Julian)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Memories lost, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(impromptu (plural impromptus) 1. (music) a short musical composition for an informal occasion often with the character of improvisation and usually to be played solo. 2. any composition, musical or otherwise, that is created on the spot without preparation.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wiktionary, Sept. 8, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Impromptu: A composition for solo instrument, usually the piano, the nature of which may occasionally suggest improvisation, though the name probably derives from the casual way in which the inspiration for such a piece came to the composer; the swift figuration and the thematic material of each of Chopin's four celebrated impromptus are so akin as to suggest that he intended them to form a coherent group--each one after the first to be, so to speak, improvised from, or casually derived from, the material of the previous one; examples of impromptus can be found in the work of Sterndale Bennett, Skryabin, Faur\u00e9, Lennox Berkeley and Roberto Gerhard)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, Sept. 8, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(impromptu noun 1 : something that is impromptu 2 : a musical composition suggesting improvisation)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam-Webster dictionary online, Sept. 8, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Impromptu: Literally 'improvised' or 'on the spur of the moment', but in 19th cent., name given to short piece of instr. mus., often in song\u2010like form, e.g. those by Schubert, Chopin, and Schumann)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford dictionary of music, via Oxford music online, Sept. 8, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(impromptu: An instrumental composition, not necessarily (despite its name) of an improvisatory character. 'Impromptu' was used as a title for short piano pieces in the 19th century, the earliest known examples being by the Czech composer Vo\u0159i\u0161ek (1822). The most famous are those of Schubert (he adopted the name only after his publisher, Haslinger, had called the first four of his 1827 set 'impromptus') and Chopin (opp. 29, 36, 51, and 56--the last better known as the 'Fantaisie Impromptu', 'Fantaisie' having been added to the title by Chopin's editor))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford companion to music, via Oxford music online, Sept. 8, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n49197e7823084e6f82fc0e3646c924c7b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(impromptu noun A short piece of instrumental music, especially a solo, that is reminiscent of an improvisation)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford dictionaries website, Sept. 8, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026103"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026104", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChamber operas", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2017026104", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b1"}, {"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamber operas"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026976"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b5"}, {"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b6"}, {"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b7"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-09-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2017-12-14T12:43:11"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chamber operas"}}, {"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Chamber opera. A term used to designate 20th-century operas of small and relatively intimate proportions using a chamber orchestra. Examples include Strauss's Ariadne auf Naxos (1916), Hindemith's Cardillac (1926, revised 1952), Stravinsky's The Rake's Progress (1951) and Britten's The Turn of the Screw (1954). The term has also been applied, retrospectively, to small-scale 18th-century works such as Pergolesi's La serva padrona (1733))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, Sept. 10, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Rautavaara, E. Auringon talo : kaksin\u00e4yt\u00f6ksinen kamariooppera = The house of the sun : chamber opera in two acts, 1992.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n429eb7b8417f4203a8dbbf525925dd47b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(chamber opera. An opera written for small forces, especially instrumental ones. Economical to stage, it also has the advantage of being suited to small theatres. Strauss's Ariadne auf Naxos, in its original version of 1912, is one of the first examples of the genre, which subsequently proved particularly appealing to 20th-century composers. It encapsulates a number of key features of the nascent form, not least its reduced forces--a band of 36 players, in contrast to the massive orchestra of Strauss and Hofmannsthal's previous collaboration, Der Rosenkavalier. ... By analogy, 18th-century operas for small forces, such as Pergolesi's La serva padrona, which is strictly speaking an intermezzo, have retrospectively attracted the description 'chamber opera'. The emergence of chamber opera in the years leading up to World War I was an expression of the general reaction against late Romantic luxuriance (though Strauss himself returned to full-scale forces in Die Frau ohne Schatten, 1919), contemporary with the aesthetic shift represented by Stravinsky's work after The Rite of Spring. A similar shift, but born of practical considerations, is found in the work of Britten, who, immediately after the success of Peter Grimes in 1945, also turned to chamber scoring in The Rape of Lucretia (1946), which has a band of 12 instrumentalists and piano. This marked the beginning of a series of chamber operas by Britten which includes Albert Herring (1947) and The Turn of the Screw (1954). The personal choice of instrumentation that chamber opera offered merged with more radical musical and dramatic ideas to produce music theatre in the 1960s and 70s. Chamber opera, however, has survived as a showcase for an emerging composer (e.g. Ad\u00e8s's Powder her Face, 1995))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford companion to music, via Oxford music online, Sept. 10, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2017026104"}
