{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2018026139", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $a\u02bb\u012ad al-A\u1e0d\u1e25\u0101 fiction", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2018026139", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b1"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u02bb\u012ad al-A\u1e0d\u1e25\u0101 fiction"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026502"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b5"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b6"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b7"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b8"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b9"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b11"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b14"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b17"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b20"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b23"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b26"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2018-10-05T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-01-24T10:09:20"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u02bb\u012ad al-A\u1e0d\u1e25\u0101 fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "2013404171: \u00dclker, Munise. The most pleasant festival of sacrifice : Little Batul's Eid celebration, 2013.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(subj. hdgs.: \u02bb\u012ad al-A\u1e0d\u1e25\u0101--Juvenile fiction. Fasts and feasts--Islam--Juvenile fiction.) bk. jkt. (Eid, the biggest holiday of the Muslim year) author's notes p. (Eid al-Adha, the holiday in this story, is a celebration that occurs at the end of the Hajj pilgrimage; lesson of the holiday is that people should love God more than anything else in their lives)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Mobin-Uddin, Asma. The best Eid ever, 2007:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Eid al-Adha, or Eid ul-Adha, \u02beId al-Adha: a major festival of Islam, beginning on the tenth day of the last month of the calendar and lasting for four days, usually characterized by the sacrificing of a sheep, whose flesh is divided among relatives and friends in memory of the ransom of Ishmael with a ram)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dictionary.com, Oct. 5, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "2018946151: Oxley, Jennifer. The Eid al-Adha adventure, 2018.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\u02bb\u012ad al-A\u1e0d\u1e25\u0101. UF Eid al-Adha; Feast of Sacrifice (Islam); Great Festival (Islam); \u02bb\u012ad al-Kab\u012br; \u02bb\u012ad al-Na\u1e25r; \u02bb\u012ad al-Qurb\u0101n; Major Festival (Islam); Sacrifice, Feast of (Islam); Sacrificial Feast (Islam). BT Fasts and feasts--Islam)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 5, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Eid-ul-Adha: an annual Muslim festival marking the end of the pilgrimage to Mecca. Animals are sacrificed and their meat shared among poor people)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Collins English dictionary online, Oct. 5, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Eid al-Adha fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Eid al-Adha fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u02bb\u012ad al-Qurb\u0101n fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u02bb\u012ad al-Qurb\u0101n fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Eid fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Eid fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Eid ul-Adha fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Eid ul-Adha fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b23", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b24"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u02bb\u012ad al-Kab\u012br fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b24", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u02bb\u012ad al-Kab\u012br fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b26", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b27"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u02bb\u012ad al-Na\u1e25r fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n4800decd0ab642d799c2364c70a70559b27", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u02bb\u012ad al-Na\u1e25r fiction"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2018026139"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2018026140", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCootie catchers", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2018026140", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb1"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cootie catchers"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026158"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb5"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb6"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb7"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb8"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb9"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb10"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb11"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb12"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb13"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb14"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb17"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb20"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb23"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb26"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb29"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb32"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb35"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb38"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb41"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb44"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2018-10-08T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-01-24T10:09:20"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cootie catchers"}}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 104 (origami fortuneteller; fortuneteller)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Teaching literacy in the digital age, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(cootie catcher (plural cootie catchers): A form of origami with hidden fortunes, activities, etc., written on the folds, and used mainly in children's games, where a person is asked to choose a fold to open. Synonyms: chatterbox, fortuneteller)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wiktionary, Oct. 7, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "origami for kids > fortune teller (origami fortune teller)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Origami-Instructions.com, Oct. 7, 2018:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Paper fortune teller (A fortune teller (also called a cootie catcher, chatterbox, salt cellar, whirlybird, or paku-paku) is a form of origami used in children's games. Parts of the fortune teller are labelled with colors or numbers that serve as options for a player to choose from, and on the inside are eight flaps, each concealing a message. The person operating the fortune teller manipulates the device based on the choices made by the player, and finally one of the hidden messages is revealed. These messages may purport to answer questions (hence the name) or they may be activities that the player must perform.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Oct. 7, 2018:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "cover (tear-'em-out, fold-'em-up, fortune tellers)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "98206160: The cootie catcher book, \u00a91997:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Consists of a single sheet folded and refolded again so that the four exterior flaps, bearing text, may be opened at random to reveal one's fortune in the format called a cootie catcher)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Elliott, Steven. Cootie catcher : your own personal fortune teller, \u00a91999", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(cootie catcher; centuries-old origami performed by kids (usually); the fortune teller also goes by chatterbox, whirlybird, or salt cellar, and that last name is actually reflective of how the origami figure was first introduced to the United States; by the 1950s, cootie catchers had started to appear in England and the United States, and propagated from there. Today, the game is played all over the world, and each place has its own name for the fortune teller)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Schneider, C. A brief history of cootie catchers, 2015, via WWW, Oct. 7, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(an artist's book consisting of 1 volume + 1 sheet + 1 cootie catcher) colophon (\"There are four components: 1. The house shaped book 'Vertices' featuring the poem 'Vertex' by Sarah Kathryn Moore, inkjet printed on artist-made abaca paper which is laminated to artist-made cotton paper with hand-cut windows. 2. 'Love in a maze,' a watermark print in an artist-made cotton/abaca blend paper. 3. The 'Fortune teller,' an origami game featuring pulp printed symbols and hand stitched text representing potential outcomes of perhaps the most consequential intersection of male & female. 4. The envelope also features pulp printed symbols and is folded from an artist-made cotton/abaca blend paper to house the first three components.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Hiebert, Helen. Vertices, 2016", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Cootie catchers. UF Catchers, Cootie; Fortune tellers (Paper work); Origami cootie catchers. BT Paper work)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Oct. 7, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Paku-pakus (Cootie catchers)"}}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb9ea272eb15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Paku-pakus (Cootie catchers)"}}, {"@id": "_:ne5c7e8754a4548dc922b133fb