e": "Annotated bibliographies"}}, {"@id": "_:n77e9d79a90da4fdeacbd1822fb45c142b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Cheng, J. An annotated bibliography for Taiwan film studies, 2016.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n77e9d79a90da4fdeacbd1822fb45c142b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Annotated Bibliographies. BT Bibliographies)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "ERIC thesaurus online, Jan. 18, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n77e9d79a90da4fdeacbd1822fb45c142b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Swingle, J.K. Annotated bibliography of the red tree vole (Arborimus longicaudus), Sonoma tree vole (A. pomo), and white-footed vole (A. albipes), August 2016.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n77e9d79a90da4fdeacbd1822fb45c142b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(annotated bibliography: A bibliography in which a brief explanatory or evaluative note is added to each reference or citation. An annotation can be helpful to the researcher in evaluating whether the source is relevant to a given topic or line of inquiry)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Reitz, J.M. ODLIS : online dictionary for library and information science, Jan. 18, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n77e9d79a90da4fdeacbd1822fb45c142b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Chaucer's Squire's tale, Franklin's tale, and Physician's tale : an annotated bibliography, 1900 to 2005, 2017.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026015"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026016", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDumky (Art music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026016", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b1"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dumky (Art music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026639"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b5"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b6"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b7"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b8"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b9"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b11"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-07-12T12:02:17"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-01-19T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dumky (Art music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dumka (musical genre) (Dumka is a musical term introduced from the Ukrainian language, with cognates in other Slavic languages. The word \"dumka\" literally means \"thought\". Originally, it is the diminutive form of the Ukrainian term duma, pl. dumy, \"a Slavic (specifically Ukrainian) epic ballad ... generally thoughtful or melancholic in character\". Ukrainian and other Slavic classical composers drew on the harmonic patterns in the folk music of their countries to inform their more formal classical compositions; dumky)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Jan. 19, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(dumka, plural dumky: an Eastern European folk ballad or lament usually with alternating slow and fast sections)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam-Webster dictionary online, Jan. 19, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(dumka (pl. dumky): A Slavonic folk ballad from Ukraine, with a lamenting quality. In the 19th century the name was also given to a type of instrumental music, most notably by Dvo\u0159\u00e1k, whose sympathies were more pan-Slavonic than narrowly Bohemian. He interpolated faster, cheerful passages, breaking up the prevailing melancholy mood, and his \"Dumky\" Piano Trio op. 90 consists of a string of six movements of this nature)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford companion to music, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Dumka (Pol., Ukr., pl. dumki; Cz., pl. dumky). (1) A sung lament or an instrumental piece generally of a ruminative nature. 2) A sung lament, usually in Polish or Ukrainian, strophic, in the minor key (sometimes modulating to the relative major), of a plaintive character and mostly in duple time. (3) An instrumental piece with a ruminative, often melancholy character, usually for chamber or solo instrumental forces. Instrumental dumky were written in the latter half of the 19th century and early 20th century by composers from several northern and west Slavonic nations and contributed greatly to the dissemination of the term, which became a musical symbol of pan-Slavonicism; the most familiar examples are Czech, many of them by Dvo\u0159\u00e1k)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, Jan. 19, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Hutchinson pocket dictionary of classical music, 2005", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(contents include: Dumka in D minor, op. 35 / Anton\u00edn Dvo\u0159\u00e1k -- Dumka in D minor, op. 7 no. 5 / Josef Suk -- Dumka op. 12 no. 1 ; Furiant op. 12 no. 2 / Dvo\u0159\u00e1k -- Dumka in C minor, op. 59 : Russian rustic scene / Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky -- Dumka-shumka : op. 18 : second piano rhapsody on Ukrainian folk themes / Mykola Lysenko -- Dumka no. 1, H. 249 : Contemplation ; Dumka : Contemplation : no. 2, H. 250 : \u00c9legie ; Dumka no. 3, H. 285bis / Bohuslav Martin\u016f) booklet, p. 3 (Derived from the Ukrainian folk-song tradition, a diminutive of the heroic duma narratives of ancient battles, the dumka blossomed in the art music of Bohemia, Moravia, Poland, 'Little Russia' (Ukraine) and Russia in the second half of the 19th century; every dumka in this programme conforms to the pattern of a lyrical, wistful opening with a contrasting, sometimes dance-like, central section; these dumky)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Dumka, 2014", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dumki (Art music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nd291b7df8ffd45e4b88ecd96ff6d9ce0b12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dumki (Art music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026016"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026017", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCyberpunk comics", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026017", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b1"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk comics"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026530"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b5"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b6"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b7"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b9"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b12"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b15"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b18"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Science fiction comics that depict the relationship between human beings and the rapid advancement and omnipresence of technology, which leads to a radical change in the social order."}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-01-21T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-04-09T11:06:04"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk comics"}}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Akira (manga) (Akira is a Japanese manga series written and illustrated by Katsuhiro Otomo; Otomo uses conventions of the cyberpunk genre to detail a saga of political turmoil, social isolation, corruption, and power. It is considered a landmark work in the cyberpunk genre, credited with spawning the Japanese cyberpunk subgenre) Transmetropolitan (Transmetropolitan is a cyberpunk transhumanist comic book series written by Warren Ellis)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Jan. 21, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(cyberpunk graphic novels)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: \u014ctomo, K. Akira, \u00a92009-2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(cyberpunk graphic novels)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Ellis, W. Transmetropolitan, \u00a91998-\u00a92004", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Akira is an award winning cyberpunk manga series by Japanese comic book writer and film director Katsuhiro Otomo. Its story follows the group of several people in the dystopian post-nuclear city of Tokyo, and describes the themes of youth alienation, government corruption, superhuman powers and mostly societal pressures and political machination; \"Transmetropolitan\" is an American comic written by Warren Ellis. Set in the overcrowded futuristic, dystopian, post-cyberpunk city, it follows the life of the journalist Spider Jerusalem who writes about the various aspects of this dark environment)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "List of dystopian comics, via Utopia and dystopia website, Jan. 21, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Postcyberpunk comics"}}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Postcyberpunk comics"}}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk science fiction comics"}}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk science fiction comics"}}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Post-cyberpunk comics"}}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Post-cyberpunk comics"}}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberprep comics"}}, {"@id": "_:ndbd4b0deabea49389ef3b1fda11cfbd4b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberprep comics"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026017"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026019", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSpace operas (Comics)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026019", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb1"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space operas (Comics)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026216"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026530"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb5"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb6"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb7"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb8"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb9"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb10"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb11"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Large-scale action and adventure comics involving space travel."}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-04-09T11:06:04"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-01-22T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space operas (Comics)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(space opera graphic novel)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Johns, G. Green Lantern. The Sinestro Corps war, 2007", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(graphic novel; summary: The adventures of the intrepid Sardine, who travels across the universe meeting cosmic squids, space leeches, talking clouds, and other strange beings)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Guibert, E. Sardine in outer space, 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(psychedelic, long-haul stories will launch you eyes-first through space, time, and every weird point in between; title listed include: ODY-C; Eternals; Southern Cross; The 6 Voyages of Lone Sloane; Valerian; The Incal; Starstruck; Black Science; Green Lantern: The Sinestro Corps War; Bucky Barnes The Winter Soldier; Descender)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Space opera graphic novels, via Comixology website, Jan. 22, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(comics are the perfect medium for space opera. Before advances in visual effects, comic books and strips were arguably the best places to experience epic stories of swashbuckling heroes, princesses, and extraterrestrials in galaxies far, far away; visions of war, the future, heroism, and cool stuff blowing up brought to life on the comic book; list includes: Valerian and Laureline; Fourth World Saga; Dreadstar; The Incal; Infinity Gauntlet; Annihilation; Sinestro Corps War; Saga; Starlight; Space Riders)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dalton, L. The top ten space opera comics, via Goomba stomp website, posted Apr. 18, 2017, viewed Jan. 22, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pp. 35-37 (space opera; tales involving space flight, set aboard spaceships, stories about spaceships; variety of adventure story; space opera is primarily spaceship fiction, stories featuring spacecraft to a large extent and with the action frequently set on board such spacefaring vessels; the adventure genre that prefigures it is the sea story; space opera is a subgenre of sf; space-travel fiction)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Space and beyond : the frontier theme in science fiction, 2000:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 262 (\"space-opera\" comics)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Brooke-Rose, C. A rhetoric of the unreal : studies in narrative and structure, especially of the fantastic, 1981:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb37d447ee9f44f2ab5e20fe74aaebe5cb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 1, p. 225 (the space-opera comic strip Flash Gordon) v. 1, p. 262 (the first Fantagraphics comic was the magazine-size black-and-white space opera The Flames of Gyro (1979))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Icons of the American comic book : from Captain America to Wonder Woman, \u00a92013:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026019"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026020", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSpace operas (Motion pictures)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026020", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb1"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space operas (Motion pictures)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026005"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026556"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb5"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb6"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb7"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb8"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb9"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb10"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb12"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb15"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb18"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb21"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb24"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Large-scale action and adventure films involving space travel."}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-01-22T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-04-09T11:06:04"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space operas (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 282 (space opera films)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wright, G. The science fiction image : the illustrated encyclopedia of science fiction in film, television, radio and the theater, \u00a91983, via Google books, viewed Jan. 22, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 274 (subgenre listed under Science Fiction Film: Space Operas. Applied to space adventure stories with spaceships on some rescue mission or with intergalactic starships battling enemy forces, bug-eyed monsters and the like. A famous recent example is Star Wars (1977). Examples listed include: Flash Gordon (1936); Buck Rogers (1939); Barbarella (1967); Star Trek (1979); Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan (1982); Space Raiders (1983); The Last Starfighter (1984); separate subgenre also listed: Space Travel Films (Space Exploration Films, Space Films, Space Flight Films). \"Space operas fit into a different class of space flight films.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "L\u00f3pez, D. Films by genre, \u00a91993:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Last starfighter (Motion picture). The last starfighter, 2009.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 185 (Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan (Nicholas Meyer, 1982); the film is a good example of the subgenre of space opera, where heroic spaceship captains save the world after monumental and visually exciting space battles) p. 190 (three key features that a space opera must have: a spaceship, an exciting adventure story and a tendency towards a formulaic plot or mediocrity)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Erickson, M. Science, culture and society, 2016:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Category: Space opera films; films belonging to the space opera subgenre of science fiction; category has the following 2 subcategories: American space opera films; Space adventure films\u200e)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, Jan. 22, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Flash Gordon (Motion picture : 1936). Rocket ship, 1980.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Space opera\" is a phrase that defines a genre of science fiction literature, and can also be applied as a category for movies. Typically, space opera occurs on a galactic scale, involves intelligent races other than humans, and has multiple types of spacecraft, from speedy corvettes to battleships capable of destroying planets. There is plenty of action and adventure in space opera. One thing you can be nearly certain of when watching a space opera film is that you won't get bored)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Evans, J. Top 5 space opera films, via ScienceFiction.com website, posted July 28, 2011, viewed on Jan. 22, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space adventure films"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space adventure films"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space exploration films"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space exploration films"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space flight films"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space flight films"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb21", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb22"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space travel films"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb22", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space travel films"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb24", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb25"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space films"}}, {"@id": "_:n214e9057445b4d46979076d7c96c8b6bb25", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Space films"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026020"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026021", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCyberpunk films", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026021", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb1"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk films"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026556"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb5"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb6"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb7"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb8"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb9"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb11"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb14"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb17"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb20"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Science fiction films that depict the relationship between human beings and the rapid advancement and omnipresence of technology, which leads to a radical change in the social order."}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-01-24T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-04-09T11:06:04"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk films"}}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Blade runner (Motion picture : Final cut). Blade runner, 2007.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Cyberpunk is science fiction, often set in a dystopian future, in which society is dominated, for better or worse, by computers and the internet. Rather than direct government overlords, society-at-large is usually controlled by mega-corporations who skew the balance of power away from the poor and needy, fueling tensions and inspiring revolution. The people are represented by radical groups using their soldiers--or hackers--to shock the system. The internet is used as a great equalizer, a weapon of the oppressed to even the playing field against their tormentors. Then again, the danger of computers taking over society and our daily lives is often a theme in the genre. Aesthetically, Cyberpunk often borrows from the film-noir detective genre, but with vivid splashes of electronic neons and proliferation of wildly sci-fi technologies. Cybernetically-enhanced humans coexist with their unaugmented brethren, but a looming sense of foreboding hangs above everyone; a Cyberpunk world, if not already in full-scale urban warfare, is always on the brink of one revolution or another. Finally, Cyberpunk is empowering to outcasts, misfits, minorities, and other people who are so often characterized as \"less than.\" In Cyberpunk, anyone can be powerful, as long as their computer software is up to date.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wojnar, Z. 13 best cyberpunk movies of all time, via Screen rant website, posted Jun 08, 2016, viewed Jan. 24, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(cyberpunk is a subgenre of science fiction; it visualises a high-tech dystopian future, where the line between virtual and physical reality is blurred; films like Runner, Ghost in the Shell, Total Recall, and RoboCop; some focus on the cyber aspect and others are more interested in the punk element; computers are everywhere, the Internet is ubiquitous; androids and cybernetic body augmentation are ordinary, and humans occupy cyberspace as much as they occupy real space)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "An introduction to cyberpunk films, via YouTube, viewed Jan. 24, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 1 (Ridley Scott's 1982 film Blade Runner; the first truly cyberpunk film)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Film criticism, fall 1996:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Post-Cyberpunk, aka: Cyberprep. Post-Cyberpunk picks up where Cyberpunk left off. Whereas cyberpunk is/was a Darker and Edgier riposte to older Science Fiction, intended to portray what might happen if we don't all destroy ourselves, Post-Cyberpunk is intended to present a less pessimistic and more realistic vision. Where Cyberpunk is anti-corporate and anti-government, Post-Cyberpunk is willing to give both parties redeeming features; Postcyberpunk involves reconstruction of concepts Cyberpunk deconstructed, or deconstruction of Cyberpunk Tropes (such as the Dystopia); what Post-Cyber Punk has that separates it from pure-Cyberpunk works, is an emphasis on positive socialization; Cyberpunk characters frequently seek to topple or exploit corrupt social orders. Postcyberpunk characters tend to seek ways to live in, or even strengthen, an existing social order, or help construct a better one)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "TV tropes website, Jan. 24, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pp. 126-127 (post-millennial cyberpunk film; race in cyberpunk film) p. 149 (cyberpunk films)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Matrix trilogy : cyberpunk reloaded, 2005:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Post-cyberpunk films"}}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Post-cyberpunk films"}}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Postcyberpunk films"}}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Postcyberpunk films"}}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberprep films"}}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberprep films"}}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk science fiction films"}}, {"@id": "_:n9e0150888b30420098978a2efc5388dfb21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Cyberpunk science fiction films"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026021"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026022", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aRawinsonde observations", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026022", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b1"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Rawinsonde observations"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026113"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b5"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b6"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b7"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b8"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b9"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b10"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b12"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b15"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b18"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-01-30T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-05-14T08:16:56"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Rawinsonde observations"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(rawinsonde observation: A combined radiosonde and radio wind observation; radiosonde observation: An evaluation of pressure, temperature, and relative humidity data received from a balloon-borne radiosonde. The processed data are usually presented in terms of geopotential height, temperature, and dewpoint at mandatory and significant pressure levels. If the position of the radiosonde is measured to determine winds aloft, then the observation is called a rawinsonde observation)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Glossary of meteorology, via American Meteorological Society website, Jan. 30, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 1 (rawinsonde observations; rawinsonde data; for this atlas, summary 10-mb data were selected)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: BOMEX : rawinsonde atlas, 1975:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The inability of kite and aircraft meteorographs to achieve high altitudes, operate in all weather, and provide data in real-time helped foster the development for the radio transmission of upper-air data. In the late 1920's, scientists began suspending crude radio transmitters from free balloons and by the early 1930's the first radio-meteorographs or \"radiosondes\" were being flown into the stratosphere. ... advances were made in radio-direction finding or radio-theodolite technology that allowed the radiosonde to be tracked in flight so that winds aloft could be obtained. Such observations became known as \"rawinsonde\" observations. ... By the 1980's, technological advances in telemetry and computers made rawinsonde observations almost fully automated. This significantly reduced manual involvement in taking rawinsonde observations)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "A brief history of upper-air observations, via National Weather Service website, Jan. 30, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Rawinsonde data obtained during the Line Islands Experiment, 1971.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Reeves, R.W. Water-vapor and mass divergence computations based on BOMEX aircraft and rawinsonde data, 1974.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Weekly synoptic analyses 5-, 2-, and 0.4-millibar surfaces for 1968 : based on meteorological rocketsonde and high-level rawinsonde observations, 1971.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Since the late 1930s, the NOAA National Weather Service (NWS) has taken upper air observations with radiosondes. The radiosonde is a small, expendable instrument package (weighs 250 to 500 grams) that is suspended below a large balloon inflated with hydrogen or helium gas. As the radiosonde rises at about 300 meters/minute (about 1,000 feet/minute), sensors on the radiosonde transmit pressure, temperature, relative humidity and GPS position data each second. ... Observations where winds aloft are also obtained from radiosondes are called \"rawinsonde\" observations)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Radiosonde observation, via National Weather Service website, Jan. 30, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Rawinsonde data"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Rawinsonde data"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Observations, Rawinsonde"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Observations, Rawinsonde"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Data, Rawinsonde"}}, {"@id": "_:nb5218bb9a7054d1f818acb1dd5ee26f6b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Data, Rawinsonde"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026022"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026023", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aRadiosonde observations", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026023", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b1"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Radiosonde observations"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026113"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b5"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b6"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b7"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b8"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b9"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b10"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b11"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b12"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b13"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b14"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b17"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b20"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b23"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b26"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-05-14T08:16:56"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-01-30T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Radiosonde observations"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: sn 99028078: Radiosonde data of North America, v. 1 (1946-1965), published 1993.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Radio-meteorograph, see Radio-sondes; Radio-sondes: the name applied to free balloons carrying specially designed instruments which transmit messages by radio-telegraphy. These messages give the values of pressure and temperature in the atmosphere in the position which the balloon occupies at the time the message is transmitted)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The meteorological glossary, 1939", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "What is a radiosonde? (A radiosonde is a small weather station coupled with a radio transmitter. The radiosonde is attached to helium- or hydrogen-filled balloon, called variously a weather balloon or a sounding balloon, and the balloon lifts the radiosonde to altitudes exceeding 115,000 feet. During the radiosonde's ascent, it transmits data on temperature, pressure, and humidity to a sea-, air-, or land-based receiving station; to add a radio transmitter to the meteorograph--thus a radio-meteorograph--so that data could be transmitted to a ground station in real time, and recovery of the device would not be necessary. This technique created a vertical profile of the temperature, humidity, and pressure above the launch site. The first recorded launch of such a device was by Robert Bureau in France, who gave the name \"radiosonde\" to his device) TMs, mags, and books > Magazines articles (radio-meteorograph; radiometeorograph; radio sounding)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Radiosonde Museum of North America website, Jan. 30, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(radiosonde observation (Acronym raob.): An evaluation of pressure, temperature, and relative humidity data received from a balloon-borne radiosonde. The processed data are usually presented in terms of geopotential height, temperature, and dewpoint at mandatory and significant pressure levels. If the position of the radiosonde is measured to determine winds aloft, then the observation is called a rawinsonde observation; radio sounding: Observations of meteorological variables such as temperature, pressure, and relative humidity by a radiosonde in the upper air. The data are transmitted instantaneously to the observing station)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Glossary of meteorology, via American Meteorological Society website, Jan. 30, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Summaries of radiosonde observations in Canada, 1961-1962.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Since the late 1930s, the NOAA National Weather Service (NWS) has taken upper air observations with radiosondes. The radiosonde is a small, expendable instrument package (weighs 250 to 500 grams) that is suspended below a large balloon inflated with hydrogen or helium gas. As the radiosonde rises at about 300 meters/minute (about 1,000 feet/minute), sensors on the radiosonde transmit pressure, temperature, relative humidity and GPS position data each second; radiosonde data; radiosonde observations)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Radiosonde observation, via National Weather Service website, Jan. 30, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Canada. Meteorological Branch. Monthly bulletin. Canadian radiosonde data, Dec. 1964.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(in titles: radio meteorograph data; radio-meteorograph investigation; helicopter-borne radiometeorograph; radio sounding; radio soundings; radio-sonde data; radio-sonde sounding data; radio-sonde observations)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "OCLC, Jan. 30, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The inability of kite and aircraft meteorographs to achieve high altitudes, operate in all weather, and provide data in real-time helped foster the development for the radio transmission of upper-air data. In the late 1920's, scientists began suspending crude radio transmitters from free balloons and by the early 1930's the first radio-meteorographs or \"radiosondes\" were being flown into the stratosphere)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "A brief history of upper-air observations, via National Weather Service website, Jan. 30, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Radio soundings (Radiosonde observations)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Radio soundings (Radiosonde observations)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Data, Radiosonde"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Data, Radiosonde"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Radio-meteorograph data"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Radio-meteorograph data"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b23", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b24"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Radiosonde data"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b24", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Radiosonde data"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b26", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b27"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Observations, Radiosonde"}}, {"@id": "_:nb82d2528b9094d3ab60c2804f932c423b27", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Observations, Radiosonde"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026023"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026024", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aInstallation works (Art)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026024", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb1"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Installation works (Art)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017027218"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026722"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb5"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb6"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb7"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb8"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb9"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb10"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb13"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Art constructions or assemblages that use the physical space where they are displayed as part of their design."}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-02-12T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-04-09T11:06:04"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Installation works (Art)"}}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Installation (Environment); Installation art; Term used to describe an art construction or assemblage erected in a particular space. Installation art encompasses a broad range of media, styles, definitions, practices, and origins, but it is most commonly associated with artworks since the 1950s that have called attention to the physical spaces that contain them and incorporated those spaces as important formal components that are integral to a viewer's experience of the work. The term includes, but is not limited to, sculpture, light, sound, video, or performance pieces, and architectural, environmental, or assemblage constructions ... Implicit in much installation art is an assumption that it has been created to occupy a very particular space (whether as part of an exhibition or on its own); one that the viewer is meant to enter physically.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Grove encyclopedia of American art, Oxford art online, May 22, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Installations (Art); UF Installation art; BT Art, Modern--20th century; Environment (Art); NT Film installations (Art); Multimedia installations (Art); Site-specific installations (Art); Sound installations (Art); Video installations (Art))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, May 22, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Thesaurus for graphic materials, May 22, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Installation; Installation art is an assemblage or environment specifically created for a particular interior (very often a gallery) ... Whereas early Installations were viewed as temporary, many are now intended to be permanent and are viewed as collectable.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The concise Oxford dictionary of art terms (2 ed.), Oxford art online, May 22, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(installations (visual works); variants: installation (visual work); installation works; installation art; note: Works dating from the 1960s or later that use their exhibition space as part of their design. Demanding a viewer's active engagement, installations are often created by artists in direct opposition to the notion of permanent artwork, or art as a commodity.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Art & architecture thesaurus online, May 22, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Installations (Art)"}}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Installations (Art)"}}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Installation art"}}, {"@id": "_:n63ab98b109b2473ea5034424599a4f1bb14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Installation art"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026024"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026025", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aMultiples (Art)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026025", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb1"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Multiples (Art)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017027218"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb5"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb6"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb7"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb8"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb9"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb10"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Art works that are intended for sale or distribution and are identically produced, sometimes in unlimited numbers, so that there is no original."}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-04-09T11:06:04"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-02-12T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Multiples (Art)"}}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Art terms (multiple; a series of identical artworks, usually a signed limited edition made specifically for selling.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Tate website, May 24, 2018:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Multiple art; UF Art, Multiple; UF Multiples (Art); BT Art, Modern--20th century)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, May 24, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Thesaurus for Graphic Materials, May 24, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(multiples; variants: multiple; note: works of art designed to be produced so that there is more than one original, theoretically in unlimited numbers.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Art & architecture thesaurus online, May 24, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "MoMA Learning, Glossary of art terms (Multiple; A term for small-scale, three-dimensional works conceived by artists, and often produced commercially, in relatively large editions.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Museum of Modern Art website, May 24, 2018:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Multiple art"}}, {"@id": "_:n4353c1dec52a4501a0951785f5a3b78fb11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Multiple art"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026025"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026026", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aNegatives (Photographs)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026026", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb1"}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Negatives (Photographs)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017027249"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb5"}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb6"}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb8"}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb11"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Photographs in which the tonal values are reversed from their appearance in nature and are intended to be used to produce positive prints."}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-04-09T11:06:04"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-02-12T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Negatives (Photographs)"}}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Negatives; UF Photonegatives; BT Photographs; NT Film negatives; Glass negatives; Paper negatives; Pellicles; note: Photographs in which the tonal values are the opposite of those in the subject to which the negative was exposed. Their purpose is to be a matrix for obtaining multiple positive images of the subject.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Thesaurus for graphic materials, May 24, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(negatives (photographs); variants: negative (photograph); photographic negatives; photonegatives; note: Photographs in which the tones or colors are reversed from their appearance in nature, usually on a transparent support of celluloid, acetate, or on paper, intended for the purpose of producing positive prints.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Art & architecture thesaurus online, May 24, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Photography--Negatives; UF Negatives; RT Photography--Developing and developers)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, May 24, 2018", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Photographic negatives"}}, {"@id": "_:nd29d8b4cb6014a6386c96ca6e0c35aeeb9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "m