npkmc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nd61d997c55694b8fb4fb8aaed459a1e3b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "School comics"}}, {"@id": "_:nd61d997c55694b8fb4fb8aaed459a1e3b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. vii (school comics)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The best American comics 2012, 2012:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd61d997c55694b8fb4fb8aaed459a1e3b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(comics that explore the emotions of the new school year and changing routines and social dynamics after summer vacation)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "11 comics summing up mixed back-to-school emotions, 5 Sep. 2017, via Go Comics website, viewed 5 June 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd61d997c55694b8fb4fb8aaed459a1e3b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "abstr. (\"Looie, Tutti, Roxy and Rella are off to school, and learning has never been so funny or fun. They're quick and clever and ready for everything the school day dishes out...\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Locker talk : cool school comics, 2002", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd61d997c55694b8fb4fb8aaed459a1e3b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a collection of comics focusing on a high school setting)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Archie's favorite high school comics, 2015", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd61d997c55694b8fb4fb8aaed459a1e3b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a graphic novel focusing on social dynamics in a middle school setting)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Chmakova, S. Awkward, 2015", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd61d997c55694b8fb4fb8aaed459a1e3b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"comics that explore [school] times or whose characters are living out similar experiences in school-like settings\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Tan, A. Top 10 school comics, 5 Sep. 2012, via Comics bulletin website, viewed 5 June 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd61d997c55694b8fb4fb8aaed459a1e3b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 28 (\"school comics, which focus on the adventures of school-age\" children)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hart, C. Kids draw manga shoujo, 2005:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026088"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026089", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDevotional poetry", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026089", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab1"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Devotional poetry"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026503"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026090"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab5"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab6"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab7"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab8"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab9"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab10"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab11"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab12"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-06-05T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-10-04T13:40:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Devotional poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Devotional Poetry: The first major genre entirely confined to devotional expression was the mawlud, a poem recited on the Prophet Muhammad's birthday. Parallel with the mawlud is a narrative that describes the wondrous acts of the Prophet, of the first four caliphs, or very often of Sufi saints. Both folk and \"high\" Muslim devotional poetry are found throughout the Islamic world)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford dictionary of Islam, 2003, via Oxford reference online, June 5, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Devotional poetry. BT Devotional literature)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, June 5, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Devotional Poetry; distinction between religious poetry in general and devotional poetry in particular is never absolute; devotional poetry is often recognizable for its tendency to address a divinity, a sacred thing, or a religious figure; shows a close kinship with lyric poetry, and it often uses an intimate, first-person voice; in general, the term excludes polemical and homiletic poetry, as well as epics and other long narrative forms, though these may include devotional forms and serve devotional purposes. Much devotional poetry portrays the relation to the divine in terms of wonder and praise, but some concerns painful experiences of affliction, guilt, unfulfilled longing, estrangement, and doubt. Its most common forms are prayer, meditation, and hymn; its purposes include praise (see epideictic poetry), supplication, complaint, lament, consolation, self-examination, and confession)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Princeton encyclopedia of poetry and poetics, \u00a92012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Devotional Poetry. Poetry expressing religious worship or prayer. This is a large and varied category of verse, distinguished from secular poetry of all kinds, especially in the study of medieval lyrics. A secular love poem may express devotion to the beloved, but it would still not be classed as devotional poetry)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Baldick, C. The Oxford dictionary of literary terms, 2008, via Oxford reference online, June 5, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Devotional Poetry. Poems that express a feeling and experience of devotion deliberately and self-consciously ask readers to feel and repeat their faith through meter and rhythm: the sound the poem makes, measured through prosody, forges a path to God; devotional poetry as a genre of affective listening able to tune the believer to God's presence and love)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford handbook of British poetry, 1660-1800, 2016, via Oxford handbooks online, June 5, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pp. xxi-xxii (devotional lyric poems as distinguished from broader category of religious poetry; vast majority of the poems are addresses to the unknown, conversations (albeit one-sided) with the divine, in whatever way these authors have interpreted that term; the vast majority of the poems included in this volume are written from an identifiably Christian perspective) p. xxx (The devotional lyric proves a supple and flexible medium for representing the varieties of spiritual encounter, from hosannah to lamentation to accusation to bewilderment to defiance to denial, as well as the dynamic interplay between these various, even contradictory, stances)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Before the door of God : an anthology of devotional poetry, 2013:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The most common feature of devotional verse is the presence of a speaker who seeks self-definition through a source that is felt to be external to and/or greater or other than the self. It is therefore a flexible and potentially very powerful genre--something that contributed to its wide appeal and usage during the Victorian period. A large body of religious poetry makes up this genre, and this is most frequently situated within the various branches of the Christian tradition)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Morgan, V.N. Devotional verse, 2019, via Oxford bibliographies website, June 5, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7e631c8182604395af51980024c4d55ab12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "introd. (the genre of devotional poetry; most devotional poetry takes specifically spiritual experience for its subject matter; tends to take specifically religious life as it subject; also definable by its effect on a reader; devotional poetry fixes our thoughts on the spiritual life and inspires us toward excellence in it)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The soul in paraphrase : a treasury of classic devotional poems, 2018, via Google books, viewed June 5, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026089"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026090", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aMawlids (Poetry)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026090", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b1"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mawlids (Poetry)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026403"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026089"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b5"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b6"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b7"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b8"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b9"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b10"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b12"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b15"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b18"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-06-06T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-10-04T13:40:01"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mawlids (Poetry)"}}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Princeton encyclopedia of poetry and poetics, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 84 (the mevlid genre; mevlids) p. 81 (Although some earlier Turkish mes\u0332nev\u012bs contained material resembling mevlids, the oldest generally accepted Turkish mevlid, and still the most popular, is that of S\u00fcleym\u0101n \u00c7elebi, formally titled Ves\u012blet \u00fcn-nec\u0101t, which he composed in 812/1409)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Birnbaum, E. Ottoman Turkish and \u00c7a\u011datay MSS in Canada, 2015:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Devotional Poetry: The first major genre entirely confined to devotional expressions was the mawl\u016bd, a poem recited on the occasion of the Prophet's birthday on the twelfth day of the third lunar month. Mawl\u016bds were first composed in the early thirteenth century in prose, but these prose versions soon gave way to lengthy poems in the vernaculars. The most famous mawl\u016bd (Tk., mevl\u00fbt) in Turkey is one by S\u00fcleyman \u00c7elebi of Bursa (d. 1419) that is recited to this day not only on the Prophet's birthday but also on special occasions such as the fortieth day after a death or the anniversary of one, or in fulfillment of a vow. Muslims in other areas besides Turkey have produced a remarkably large body of mawl\u016bds. The name mawl\u016bd is applied in some languages, such as Sindhi, not only to long elaborate stories but even more to short devotional poems in which the Prophet's miracles or his wonderful qualities are described)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford encyclopedia of the Islamic world, 2009", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mawlid (Mawlid, also spelled Mawl\u016bd or M\u012bl\u0101d, in Islam, the birthday of a holy figure, especially the birthday of the Prophet Muhammad (Mawlid al-Nab\u012b); The mawlid poems, which relate Muhammad's life and virtues, are also widely popular outside the times of regular feasts. Mawlids are also recited in commemoration of deceased relatives)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Britannica online, June 6, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: S\u00fcleyman \u00c7elebi. Ves\u00eelet\u00fc'n-nec\u00e2t. Mevlid, 1993.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 9 (The Mevlid is a festival in honour of the birthday of the Prophet; celebrated by a panegyric on the life of the Prophet, on his miracles and on his death) p. 12 (the Mevlid, a kind of hymn on the birth of the Prophet) p. 15 (In Republican Turkey the Mevlid continues to be chanted in mosques and in homes, and the recital takes place either on some religious festival or at a time of rejoicing; perhaps its commonest occurrence is on the fortieth day after a death; the Mevlidi Sherif, as the finest expression of reverence for Muhammed, forms an essential part in the religious pattern of most Turkish minds; poem)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "S\u00fcleyman \u00c7elebi. Ves\u00eelet\u00fc'n-nec\u00e2t. English. The mevlidi sherif, 1943:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Mawlid (a.), or Mawl\u016bd (pl. maw\u0101lid), is the term for (1) the time, place or celebration of the birth of a person, especially that of the Prophet Mu\u1e25ammad or of a saint, and (2) a panegyric poem in honour of the Prophet; Central to these celebrations is the recitation of a mawlid, i.e. of a panegyrical poem of a legendary character. These poems normally follow a standard sequence of introductory praises to God, an invocation, a description of the creation of al-n\u016br al-mu\u1e25ammad\u012b, then proceed through various stages and digressions (e.g. on the Prophet's ancestry) to the actual physical birth, which is preceded by an account of a miraculous announcement to his mother Amina that she is bearing the Prophet. In the Arab world, mawlid recitation became a common feature of the celebrations in the course of the 9th/15th century and had become universal at the end of the 12th/18th; mawlids became a predominant element in the celebration; The most popular of the mawlids in Turkish was composed by S\u00fcleym\u0101n \u010celebi (d. 825/1421). It is still recited in mosques throughout Turkey and in mosques of the Turkish-speaking Sunn\u012b community in West and South-Eastern Europe as part of the celebrations for the birthday of the Prophet)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Encyclopaedia of Islam, Second ed., via WWW, June 6, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mawl\u016bds (Poetry)"}}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mawl\u016bds (Poetry)"}}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Maw\u0101lid (Poetry)"}}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Maw\u0101lid (Poetry)"}}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mevlids (Poetry)"}}, {"@id": "_:nba4c7641bda2445985a989dcc9fd5024b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mevlids (Poetry)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026090"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026091", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSch\u00fcttelreims", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026091", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb1"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sch\u00fcttelreims"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026379"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb5"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb6"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb7"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb8"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb9"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb10"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb11"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb12"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb13"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb14"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb15"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb16"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb19"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb22"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-06-06T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-10-04T13:40:01"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sch\u00fcttelreims"}}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 21 (the Sch\u00fcttelreim; unique German verse form; double-rhyme form; we do have some Sch\u00fcttelreime in English; in Germany there are whole volumes of these double-rhymed verses; the shake-up rhyme; consist usually of a rhyming couplet or a series of rhyming couplets; sometimes the juggling is kept up for twenty, forty or even sixty lines)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "House, R.T. Gustav Mueller and the Schuettelreim, in The American-German review, Feb. 1948:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Mittler, F. Gesammelte Sch\u00fcttelreime, 1991.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Mirsky-Tauber, R. Sch\u00fcttelreime, 1904.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 256 (the Sch\u00fcttelreim; \"shake-up rhymes\"; Sch\u00fcttelreime)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Books abroad, spring 1937, via JSTOR, viewed June 6, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Schuttelreim is a German poetic device synonymous with the word play technique of Spoonerisms, named for English educator W.A. Spooner 1844-1930, which is the swapping of the beginning sounds of 2 different words such as big rats/rig bats. The Schuttelreim takes spoonerism a step further and in a rhymed couplet, the initial consonant of the last 2 words of the first line are reversed in the second line. The device is most often used in light verse.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "http://poetscollective.org/poetryforms/schuttelreim/"}, "Poetry forms, via Poets Collective website, June 6, 2019"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 689 (the popular \"shake-up\" rhymes (Sch\u00fcttelreime))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Freud, S. Works. Selections. English. 1938. The basic writings of Sigmund Freud, 1938, via Google books, viewed June 6, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 7 (Gifts from my father were usually accompanied by witty poems or ditties, often a parody on German romantic poetry, or by Schuettelreime -- Spoonerisms, at which he excelled.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cohn, C. My 9 lives, \u00a92010, via Google books, viewed June 6, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Papentrigk, B. Benno Papentrigk's Sch\u00fcttelreime, 1956.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Sch\u00fcttelreim. In Ger. verse, a rhyme, usually in a single line or couplet, that achieves witty and memorable effect by transposing the initial consonants of two words or syllables (in Ger., sch\u00fctteln means \"to shake\"). Sch\u00fcttelreim has existed since the 13th c. and was once considered a serious poetic form. Since the 19th c., however, it has been used as a comic verse form often of a lewd or suggestive nature. The sudden sound reversal lends itself well to humorous and satirical ends in Ger.; Among the shortest known sch\u00fcttelreims are \"Du bist Buddhist,\" and \"Wo bist, Bovist?\" Regine Mirsky-Tauber (Sch\u00fcttelreime, 1904) and Anton Kippenberg have written entire cycles of light verse in this form. The nearest equivalent in Eng. is the spoonerism)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Princeton encyclopedia of poetry and poetics, 2012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sch\u00fcttelreim (The Sch\u00fcttelreim is a rhyme form in which the (initial) consonants of the last two stressed syllables are interchanged; a German poetry form known since the 13th century; since the 19th century Sch\u00fcttelreime have been used mainly for entertaining two-liners)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "German Wikipedia, June 6, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb15", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 53 (Permutations of sounds can produce various forms of wordplay like spoonerisms, French contrep\u00e8teries or German Sch\u00fcttelreime)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Crossing languages to play with words, 2016:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Rhymes, Shake-up"}}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Rhymes, Shake-up"}}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sch\u00fcttelreime"}}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sch\u00fcttelreime"}}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb22", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb23"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Shake-up rhymes"}}, {"@id": "_:n552f8a5ce98f468da32ea256838b878eb23", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Shake-up rhymes"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026091"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026092", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSea poetry", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026092", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b1"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sea poetry"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026481"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b5"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b6"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b7"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b8"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b9"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b10"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b11"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b12"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b13"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b14"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b15"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b16"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b17"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b18"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b21"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b24"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-06-06T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2020-01-21T12:35:19"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sea poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Sea poems, 2005.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pp. 343-347 (sea poetry; nautical poetry; poems of the sea and the sailor; stirring adventures, heroic deeds, and jovial character of seamen inspire the poet; tragedy, joy, and humour of the seaman's life; partings and homecomings; perils and tribulations; nautical life; violence and inhumanity of the sea, with its variableness; valour, hardihood, and usefulness of those who dared its perils; reflection of the character of the seaman in his professional and social aspects, the environment in which he lived, and the conditions of sea life delineated in poetry) p. 351 (ballad poetry of the sea) p. 360 (ballads and poetry of sea service; nautical poetry that presents the seaman engaged in his occupation and handling his ship, or exhibits a vivid picture of the vicissitudes of his calling) p. 385 (naval history in verse)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Robinson, C.N. The British tar in fact and fiction, 1909, via Google books, viewed Oct. 4, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pp. 109-110 (1853 anthology of poetry called Thalatta was the first American anthology of poetry devoted entirely to the subject of the sea; nautical verse; sea poetry; explores poets feelings and ideas, their culture, and their relationship to the past through the inexhaustible resources of the sea; most of it was written in response to what we might term \"the call of the sea\"; many poems deal with real voyages, but the majority of them are metaphorical; sea poetry tends to be symbolic because the subject of the sea tempts one to representational expression; figurative associations; call of the sea is the summons to explore life's conditions and challenges, its tragedies and triumphs; sea itself may be viewed as a chasm of catastrophe or as an avenue to salvation; all elements of the life-voyage are in the paradigm of the sea) p. 114, etc. (common themes: call of the sea as a voice of death; often that of dead or dying sailor calling his lover to him; the ghost ship; past and recent sea disasters and tragedies; the turbulent sea; ebb and flow of tides is the most persistent manifestation in sea poetry of the sea's vitality and fertility; emblematic association of the sea--its sounds and rhythms, its mystery, magnitude, and beauty; the sea's creative power; metaphor of the sea journey as the journey of life is the broadest application of sea poetry to human experience)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "America and the sea : a literary history, c1995:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "t.p. (maritime poetry)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Proctor, G.H. The fishermen's memorial and record book, 1873:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hands to action stations! : naval poetry and verse from World War Two, 1980.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Downey, T. Naval poems, 1813.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Starck, C. At sea : ten nautical poems, 2008.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(under Portugal, Poetry of: Many of the cantigas, esp. the cantigas de amigo, invoke the sea and ships as motifs in amatory complaint and yearning and thus establish a trad. of maritime poetry in Portugal)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Princeton encyclopedia of poetry and poetics, \u00a92012", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "subj. index (Sea Poetry)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Columbia Granger's index to poetry in anthologies, c2007:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Fulton, E.B. Maritime poems of World War II, \u00a91981.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b15", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "v. 14, p. 779 (under sea: l. Applied to works of art or literature, narratives, etc., representing the sea or life at sea, as sea-eclogue, -sonnet, -story, -subject, -tale, -yarn, etc. So also sea-painter, -poet.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford English dictionary, 1989:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b16", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "The eternal sea : an anthology of sea poetry, \u00a91946.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b17", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Masefield, J. The sea poems, 1978.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Nautical poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Nautical poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b21", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b22"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Maritime poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b22", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Maritime poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b24", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b25"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Naval poetry"}}, {"@id": "_:n24c9de524549408687cdeb6b18a94e55b25", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Naval poetry"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026092"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026094", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aWitch fiction", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026094", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b1"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Witch fiction"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026469"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b5"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b6"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b7"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b8"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b9"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b10"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b11"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b12"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b13"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b14"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b15"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b16"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b19"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b22"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Fiction that features witches and/or warlocks and the practice of witchcraft."}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-06-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-10-01T15:50:22"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Witch fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(witchy fiction)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Seattle Public Library genre guide website, June 10, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(witchy fiction)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Witchy fiction to look out for in 2017, via Book riot website, 1-24-17, viewed June 10, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Updike, J. The witches of Eastwick, 1996.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Witch stories for bedtime, 1987.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "The witch and warlock : 25 tales of magic-users, 2015.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Genres > Paranormal > Witches (Witches are humans who participate in magic rituals and who have an unusual affinity to create magic. Depending on the origin of the story/fiction, witches are both male and female.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://www.goodreads.com/genres/witches"}, "Goodreads website, June 10, 2019:"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Adam, H. Ghosts and grinning shadows : two witch stories, \u00a91979.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Mr. Updike's titles are often quite literal, and \"The Witches of Eastwick\" is just what it says. It's indeed about witches, real ones, who can fly through the air, levitate, hex people and make love charms that work, and they live in a town called Eastwick; His version of witchcraft is closely tied to both carnality and mortality)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Atwood, M. Wondering what it's like to be a woman, in New York times, May 13, 1984, viewed online June 10, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "t.p. (a Lone Star witch novel)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Adams, T. Flamebound, 2013:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Harvey, A. The witches of London trilogy, 2018.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b15", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Chase, D. The warlock's enchanted kiss, 2018.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Witchy fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Witchy fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Witchcraft fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Witchcraft fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b22", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b23"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Warlock fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n7abded5475a9411aba8d722c82434214b23", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Warlock fiction"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026094"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026095", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChopped and screwed (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026095", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb1"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chopped and screwed (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027037"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb5"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb6"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb7"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb8"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb9"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb10"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb11"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb12"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb13"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb16"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb19"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb22"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-06-12T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/npkmc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-09-12T13:45:38"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chopped and screwed (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Screwed and chopped music is the antithesis of the relentless, ballistic bounce of Atlanta's crunk: Hip-hop records are literally slowed down to a molasses-like pace ... over the last 14 years has exerted a major influence on Southern hip-hop culture\"; invented by DJ Screw, a Houston hip-hop DJ, in the 1990s; originally popularized by mixtapes called \"Screw tapes\" or \"Gray tapes\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Patel, J. Chopped & screwed : a history, 2007, via MTV.com, viewed 12 Jun. 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a rap album of the chopped and screwed style)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Z-Ro. The rain : chopped & screwed, 2009", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(screw; chopped & screwed; a rap subgenre; created by slowing down songs, adding EQ, effects and scratches; \"there's a subculture of codeine-based prescription cough syrup around screw\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Clayton, J. Music, in Frieze, issue 135 (Nov.-Dec. 2010)", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(rap album; includes song \"My side of town slowed and throwed\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Baby Bash. Menage a trois, 2004", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Hip hop genres--Chopped and screwed (\"chopped and screwed, or screwed and chopped, or just plain screw\"; remixing hip hop music by slowing the tempo \"and applying various DJ techniques such as skipping beats, record sctratching, stop-time, and sending portions of the music through stand-alone effects to make a 'chopped-up' version of the original song\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Sfetcu, N. The music sound, 2014:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a rap album of the chopped and screwed style)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "FatPat. Fat Pat's greatest hits. Volume 1, slowed & chopped, [2004?]", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p.14 (\"'chopped and screwed'--or, rather, 'slowed and throwed'\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Rabaka, R. Hip hop's amnesia : from blues and the Black Women's Club Movement to rap and the hip hop movement, 2012:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 437 (chopped and screwed; new form of hip hop music introduced in the early 1990s by DJ Screw in Houston, Texas; influenced by the recreational use of codeine cough syrup) p. 450 (sound diversified throughout the decade, including the \"straight chopped and screwed mix, in which a song would be slowed down to 33 rpm, their voice deepened, and verses intersected by scratches. There was the Screwed chorus, in which a regular beat would feature a halftime (aka screwed time) sample as a refrain. Conversely, there were screwed beats that were noticeably slower than most tracks in which the rappers would rhyme over in double-time\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hess, M. Hip hop in America : a regional guide, 2009:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Slowed and throwed (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Slowed and throwed (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Slowed and chopped (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Slowed and chopped (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Screw (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Screw (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb22", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb23"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Screwed and chopped (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nbec93ef017c040978b532920ab83b62eb23", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Screwed and chopped (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026095"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026096", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aMythological television programs", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026096", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b1"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mythological television programs"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026673"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b5"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b6"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b7"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b8"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b9"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b10"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b11"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b12"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Television programs based on mythological stories and themes."}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2020-01-21T12:35:19"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-06-12T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mythological television programs"}}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hercules, the legendary journeys. Season five, \u00a92005.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Mahabharat (1988-1990); Devon Ke Dev... Mahadev (2011-2014); Mahabharat (2013-2014); Ramayan (1987-1988); Shri Krishna (1993- ); Radha Krishn (2018- ); Little Krishna (2009- ); Vikram Aur Betaal (1988- ))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "25 best Hindi mythological television series, via WWW, Oct. 1, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pp. 19-20 (the mythological genre in television; high degree of allegory and metaphor; depict certain utopian or dystopian settings; extremely strong othering of outsiders (demons, aliens, monsters etc.); usually feature heroes with a highly exaggerated sense of morality and dedication, or with superpowers)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Kneis, P. The emancipation of the soul : memes of destiny in American mythological television, \u00a92010:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 251 (mythological television serials in India)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Abu-Lughod, L. Dramas of nationhood, \u00a92005:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 161 (the mythological TV serials Ramayan and Mahabharat)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cultural studies, Jan. 1994:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 7 (teleserials of mythological nature) p. 25 (mythological programmes) p. 38-39 (mythological T.V. serial programmes; serialization of the epics; mythological TV programmes) p. 73 (based on Hindu mythology as depicted in the Epics and Puranas) p. 82 (big budget mythologicals on air; Sahara TV ... is in the midst of an extensive relaunch exercise, with mythologicals as one of its main weapons) p. 84 (series based on mythologies; mythological serials) p. 193 (Dominance of the male members have been shown in all mythological TV serials. This represents the patriarchal structure of Hindu religion)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Rahman, A. Socio-cultural implications of mythological T.V. serials on urban middle class of Delhi, 2007:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Greek mythological television mini-series)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Helen of Troy, \u00a92003", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2779a91389fe40f49137b216557dfaf5b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(An artistic recreation of the ancient Babylonian poetic myth of the goddess Ishtar (c. 1800 BCE); produced at PEGASYS Public Access Television Studios, Ithaca, N.Y.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: The descent of Ishtar, 1995", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026096"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026100", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aTraffic surveys", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026100", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab1"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Traffic surveys"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026113"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab5"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab6"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab7"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab8"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab9"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab10"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab11"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab12"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab13"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab14"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab15"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab16"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab19"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab22"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab25"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab28"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab31"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2020-01-21T12:35:19"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-06-28T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Traffic surveys"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Columbia River Valley traffic survey, 1931.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Annual traffic census (Hong Kong, China). The annual traffic census, \u00a92003-", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Traffic Survey (A traffic survey is a survey undertaken to determine the volume and/or nature of traffic utilising a particular route. A traffic survey can be manual or automatic. Types of Traffic Surveys: Volume Surveys (traffic count); Speed Surveys; Classification Counts; Gap Studies; Turning Movement; Multi-lane Classification; Pedestrian Surveys; License Plate Surveys)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Local government & municipal knowledge base, via WWW, Oct. 16, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Traffic survey of Aberdeen and Hoquiam, Washington, 1953.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Traffic surveys. UF Traffic census; Traffic statistics. BT Surveys)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, June 28, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(A traffic survey can include many types of traffic data collection: 1. Traffic Counts (Traffic Volume Counts) a) Hose counts at spots along the road b) Turning movement counts at intersections c) Pedestrian and bicycle counts 2. Speed data collection 3. Trip generation data collection 4. Origin - destination data collection)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "What is a traffic survey?, via Yarger Engineering, Inc. website, Oct. 16, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Census of motor traffic on main international traffic arteries in Europe : results of 1995 E-road census, \u00a91999.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 9 (road traffic surveys) p. 11 (traffic flow/volume survey) p. 13 (traffic surveys; traffic counts) p. 17 (Origin-Destinations survey is a special way of carrying out traffic counts/survey, whereby the data collected relates to the use of the road by vehicle category) p. 26 (Origin-Destination Surveys; Origin and Destination (O-D) survey; O-D survey) pp. 47-49 (traffic census count survey; road traffic census count survey; traffic census survey)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://www.vegvesen.no/_attachment/336339/binary/585485"}, "Traffic data collection and analysis, 2004:"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Traffic surveys consist of various types of data collection techniques done by surveying various aspects of traffic flow. These include the following services: pedestrian counts, turning movement counts, bicycle counts, turning movement counts, link flow traffic counts, rail and bus punctuality surveys, travel time surveys, roundabout counts, parking survey methodology, queue length surveys, number plate recognition, illegal movement surveys and traffic saturation flows)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Traffic survey, via VTRAC Worldwide website, Oct. 16, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(traffic surveys: 203,00 hits; road traffic census count surveys: 1 hit (the tentative list); road traffic surveys: 29,000 hits; traffic census count surveys: 6 hits; traffic census surveys: 414 hits; traffic censuses: 27,400 hits; traffic counts: 1,390,000 hits)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Google, Dec. 17, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab15", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Traffic surveys aim to capture data that accurately reflects the real-world traffic situation in the area. It may be counting the number of vehicles using a road or collecting journey time information for example, but there are many other types of data that traffic surveys collect. In the past this has involved having people standing by the side of roads and recording their observations on paper pads. In recent years, this approach has been largely replaced by recording traffic using video cameras, and then analyzing the video footage later in the office.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "What is a traffic survey?, via Video Datapad FAQs website, Oct. 16, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Traffic counts"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Traffic counts"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Surveys, Traffic"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Surveys, Traffic"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab22", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab23"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Counts, Traffic"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab23", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Counts, Traffic"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab25", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab26"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Road traffic surveys"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab26", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Road traffic surveys"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab28", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab29"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Traffic censuses"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab29", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Traffic censuses"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab31", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab32"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Censuses, Traffic"}}, {"@id": "_:n0bd547f0451e458dbe2833801ba7a40ab32", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Censuses, Traffic"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026100"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026101", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aMu\u011fams", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2019026101", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb1"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mu\u011fams"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026952"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb5"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb6"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb7"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb9"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb12"}], "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-11-20T08:02:29"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2019-07-05T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mu\u011fams"}}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Azerbaijani mugham (\"traditional musical form, characterized by a large degree of improvisation\"; \"This musical genre shares artistic characteristics with the Iraqi Maqam, the Persian Radif and the Turkish Makams. In the past, Mugham was primarily performed on two secular occasions: the toy, the traditional wedding feast and the majles, a gathering of connoisseurs in private settings. It was also cultivated by members of the Sufi orders and by performers of religious dramas known as ta'zie or shabih. Official competitions and informal contests served to establish the reputation of accomplished musicians\"; \"features a male or female singer accompanied by musicians playing traditional instruments, such as the tar (a long-neck lute), the kamancha (a four-string spiked fiddle) and the daf (a type of large tambourine). Since Mugham cannot be transcribed in a fixed form, multiple versions are transmitted by masters who train students in the fine art of interpretation to ensure the variety of this artistic expression\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/azerbaijani-mugham-00039"}, "UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage WWW site, July 5, 2019:"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "book [accompanying a set of sound recordings], page 5 (Azerbaijani text: mu\u011fam) page 39 (English text: \"According to the decision of UNESCO, Azeri mugham is now included in the list of masterpieces of verbal and intangible heritage ... In Azerbaijan, where mugham is considered an inseparable part of the system of fundamental culture values of the Azeri people, this decision was accepted as the public recognition of the merit of prominent mugham performers ...\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Qaraba\u011f xan\u00e4nd\u00e4l\u00e4ri = Khanandas of Garabagh, 2005:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 927 (from the chapter Music of Azerbaijan: \"The highest achievement of Azerbaijani musical culture is the mu\u011fam, a polysemous term signifying a mode but also a genre, a form, and a specific manner of musical development. In Turkic usage, the term (derived from the Arabic word maq\u0101m) may have acquired new semantic content as early as the late nineteenth century, when the art of mu\u011fam began to be distinguished from other genres. In Azerbaijan this art is characterized above all by presentation of each mu\u011fam in solo performance, with no rhythmic cycle (usul) or rhythmic formulas\"); author cites two Azerbaijani music theorists who distinguish mu\u011fam as one of three classes of folk music: \"without meter\"(b\u00e4hrsiz), involving improvisation; plural is mu\u011famlar)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Garland encyclopedia of world music. V.6, The Middle East:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pages 3-4 (mugham; \"the classical music of Azerbaijan\"; \"mugham is a musical genre that weds orally transmitted music with classical written poetry. A mugham composition is a succession of improvised recitations interspersed with songs and dance-like episodes. In addition, mugham is a performing tradition identified with specific settings and purposes\"; points out that mugham is also the term used to denote the modal system)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Naroditskaya, Inna. Song from the land of fire : continuity and change in Azerbaijanian mugham, 2002:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mughams"}}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mughams"}}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mu\u011famlar"}}, {"@id": "_:n34d72a6eaf2a4d17a78f9ff7cb64cbdfb13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Mu\u011famlar"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026101"}
