@value": "(Recorded in performance Dec. 21-22, 1999, Wiener Staatsoper)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Aschenbr\u00f6del [VR] : Ballett in drei Akten, c2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(excerpts of performances recorded at the Bolshoi Theatre, Moscow and Th\u00e9\u00e2tre Royal de la Monnaie, Brussels)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Maya Plisetskaya [VR] 2006", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Recorded in live performance Aug. 1996)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Voltaire's temple of glory [VR] : a baroque ballet, 1998", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Ballet; a style of theatrical dancing that developed in France during the 17th century; achieved 'classical' status in the 19th century; also describes a theatrical spectacle, in which use it has a history stretching back to the Middle Ages; since the late 18th century \"ballet\" as a genre has usually meant a spectacle accompanied by purely instrumental music; the 20th century saw a shift in emphasis away from story ballets set to newly composed scores towards more abstract works set to pre-existing music not necessarily composed for dancing)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, viewed January 30, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb15", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (Vaslav Nijinsky's 1915 dance score [of] L'apr\u00e8s-midi d'un faune... revealed, translated into Labanotation) title page verso (this volume presents Nijinsky's ballet as he himself recorded it in 1915, making this authentic version translated into Labanotation, available to dance students, teachers, scholars and researchers)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Nijinsky's Faune restored, 1991:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb16", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The four major styles of ballet: classical ballet, romantic ballet, neoclassical ballet, contemporary ballet)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://www.bostonballet.org/stories/the-four-major-styles-of-ballet/"}, "Boston Ballet website, viewed April 22, 2024"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb17", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Recorded Apr. 1993 at the Maryinsky Theatre, St. Petersburg)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Copp\u00e9lia [VR] : ballet in two acts and three scenes, c1994", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb18", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Performed at the Royal Opera House, Covent Garden on 1 & 5 Dec. 2005; originally broadcast in 2005)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Sylvia [VR] 2007", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb19", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Ballet; A form of Western academic theatre dance based on the danse d'\u00e9cole (classical school) and usually presented with elements of music and design to create a dramatic or lyric effect. The history of ballet began with Renaissance spectacles which combined all the artforms in a single entertainment, and quickly moved to France where the foundations of classical ballet as we know it today were laid at the royal court. Louis XIV was a keen performer himself in his younger days, and featured in ballets by Lully and Beauchamps. Following his retirement, the Acad\u00e9mie Royale de Musique was set up in 1671 and ballet became the province of professional performers instead of royal amateurs, while theatres replaced banquet halls as performance venues. By the early 19th century technique had been codified Romantic ballet flowered in France during the first half of the 19th century, with ballets such as Giselle, but developed a more virtuosic, athletic style in Italy and Russia during the latter half of the 19th century, the era which saw the creation of the three Tchaikovsky ballets (Swan Lake, The Sleeping Beauty, and The Nutcracker) that form the core of the classical repertory. In 1909 Diaghilev brought Russian dance to the West, sparking an international ballet boom that eventually led to the creation of schools and companies throughout Europe and America (the founding of the Royal Ballet by de Valois, for example). Strictly speaking, the term ballet should only be applied to works based on the danse d'\u00e9cole and subsequent permutations of the academic form, but with the enormous cross-fertilization of dance in the 20th century the term took on a much broader meaning and is now frequently used to describe a wide range of non-classically based theatrical dance.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford dictionary of dance (2 ed.), via Oxford reference, viewed January 28, 2023:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb20", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Recorded at the Bolshoi Theatre, 1989)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Raymonda [VR] 2005", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb21", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb22"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Romantic ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb22", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Romantic ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb24", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb25"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Televised ballets"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb25", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Televised ballets"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb27", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb28"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Contemporary ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb28", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Contemporary ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb30", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb31"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Neoclassical ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb31", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Neoclassical ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb33", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb34"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Classical ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb34", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Classical ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb36", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb37"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ballet (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb37", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ballet (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb39", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb40"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Filmed ballets"}}, {"@id": "_:nff34604a94d5477d98d0c7d7c1a22d6fb40", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Filmed ballets"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026112"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026113", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aTangos (Dance)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026113", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db1"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Tangos (Dance)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026091"}, "madsrdf:hasEarlierEstablishedForm": {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db5"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db8"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db9"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db10"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db12"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established May 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Heading changes from Filmed tangos to Tangos (Dance) in May 2024."}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-10-19T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-05-22T14:04:10"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Tangos (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db5", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db6"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Filmed tangos"}}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db6", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Filmed tangos"}}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Film of a ballet by Oscar Araiz comprised of sketches featuring aspects of the tango performed by the Ballet of the Grand Theatre of Geneva)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Tango, \u00a92003", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The tango is a sensual ballroom dance that originated in Buenos Aires, Argentina in the early twentieth century. The tango dance is usually performed by a man and a woman, expressing an element of romance in their synchronized movements. Originally, the tango was performed only by women, but once it spread beyond Buenos Aires, it developed into a dance for couples)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Bedinghaus, T. All about the tango, 2018, via ThoughtCo. website, viewed Mar. 7, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Hector Elizondo hosts this staged dance performance which tells the story of the tango with fourteen compositions that span the life of the tango and features music from composers Carlos Gardel, Astor Piazolla, and Pablo Ziegler)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Tango magic, \u00a91999", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Tango (Dance); BT: Dance; Here are entered works on the tango as a dance. Musical compositions are entered under the heading Tangos)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, viewed October 18, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Filmed tangos"}}, {"@id": "_:n7f34107968724f3e9bfc47770e48cc8db13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Filmed tangos"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026113"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026114", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aBallroom dances", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026114", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb1"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ballroom dances"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026091"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026060"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026061"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb5"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb6"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb7"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb8"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb9"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb11"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established May 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Dances for couples performing conventional step patterns and traditionally performed in social or competitive settings, and dances composed stylistically and/or ideologically within these traditions."}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-10-19T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-05-22T14:04:10"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ballroom dances"}}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ballroom dance entry (Ballroom dance: A broad category of Social dance in which two people dance as partners, one leading and one following, to popular music, independent of other couples on the dance floor. The term came into general use in the 20th century with two meanings: (1) social dances for partnered couples done for recreation and personal pleasure or (2) specific sets of couple dances performed in national and international competitions. Ballroom dances are also often performed in a theatrical context and are enjoyed as entertainment on stage, in films, and on television.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Grove Dictionary of American Music (2 ed.) via Oxford Reference, viewed February 3, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Ballroom dancing; UF Ball room dancing, Social dancing; BT Dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, viewed October 18, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 43 (Ballroom dancing; forms of social dance which arise from musical rhythms)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dance words, 1995:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Ballroom dance; type of social dancing, originally practiced in Europe and the United States, that is performed by couples and follows prescribed steps. The tradition was historically distinguished from folk or country dance by its association with the elite social classes and with invitational dance events. In the 21st century, however, ballroom dance is present in many parts of the world and has practitioners in virtually all segments of society. It is performed in various contexts, including invitational and public dance events, professional dance exhibitions, and formal competitions. Standard ballroom dances include the waltz and the polka from the 19th century and the fox-trot, the two-step, and the tango, among others, from the 20th century. Other popular dances--such as the Charleston, swing dancing, the mambo, the twist, and disco dancing--have also visited the ballroom repertoire at various points in the tradition's history. Owing to the social and stylistic breadth of the ballroom tradition, the term ballroom dance has often been loosely applied to all sorts of social and popular dancing)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Britannica online, viewed October 18, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "video file description (Three ballroom dance teams, founder-director Lee Theodore of The American Dance Machine, and a group of its students demonstrate dances derived from social dancing that have influenced Broadway dance styles)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: From Ballroom to Broadway [VR], 1980, 2007:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Ballroom dance: Social dance usually performed by couples in dance halls or at social gatherings. During the 20th century these dances came to be performed widely in competitions, which flourished in Britain and America following the First World War. Standard ballroom dances include the waltz, Viennese waltz, foxtrot, tango, lindy, charleston, and the quickstep. Latin American dances such as the rumba, samba, paso doble, and cha-cha-cha are also part of the ballroom repertoire.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford dictionary of dance (2nd. ed.) via Oxford reference, viewed February 29, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ball room dances"}}, {"@id": "_:n4b5586dd85e146458c24eac86845fdceb12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ball room dances"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026114"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026117", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aNotated movement", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026117", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b1"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Notated movement"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026089"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026118"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b5"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b6"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b7"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b8"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b9"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b10"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b12"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b15"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established January 2025.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-10-26T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2025-01-27T10:29:49"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Notated movement"}}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (somatic strategies, choreographic technologies, and notational abstractions through a Laban/Bartenieff Lens) page 22 (movement scores) page 23 (movement scores (notation)) page 248 (Labanotated score; While created primarily as a means of recording choreography, Labanotation has been used to record human movement in other contexts as well -- and even used to program robot motion) page 250 (an example of a Labanotation score) page 251 (notated score)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Making meaning with machines, 2023:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "glossary (notated movement: A content type consisting of content expressed through a form of notation for movement intended to be perceived visually. All forms of movement notation other than those intended to be perceived through touch are included.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "RDA Toolkit, viewed October 26, 2023:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 99 (motion scores can be used for mime, sign languages used by the deaf, martial arts, gymnastics, physical therapy, and ice skating)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://soctech.spbstu.ru/userfiles/files/articles/2022/2/81-126.pdf"}, "Technology and Language, 2022:"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page v (contains entries of both dance and non-dance materials, notated or reported since 1988) page 20 (Compulsory floor exercices for women in labanotation, 1964; version: XIII Olympiad, Tokyo, Japan, 1964; genres: Athletics: Gymnastics; notator: Diane Patterson) page 23 (La cr\u00e9ation du monde; creator: Marcel Marceau; genre: mime; version: Marcel Marceau, Espace Cardin, France, November 1993; notator: Etienne Bonduelle)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Laban notation scores: an international bibliography, 1995:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 45 ([transcription in labanotation of] movement sequences from corporeal mime) (OCoLC)83446801)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Movement sequences from corporeal mime, spring 1983:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pdf page 2 (a shorthand notation that would allow a student to rapidly and accurately capture the essentials of a new form after only a few viewing; a robust, fairly complete set of symbols and shorthand notation for stances used in the forms) page 3 (A notation system for martial arts should meet the following goals: 1. the notation should use a minimal number of symbols; 2. the symbols should be unambiguous; 3. the notation should be capable of describing basic techniques, forms, and step-sparring; 4. the notation should be extendable to weapons techniques; 5. the notation should include a shorthand to allow rapid transcription of techniques, ideally at the speed they are normally performed; 6. the notation should be intuitive and readable by students with a minimal amount of instruction; 7. a reader familiar with the notation should be able to learn a technique from the description of the technique in the notation.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "An as-yet Unnamed, preliminary written notation for martial arts techniques, 2013:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 12 (fight notation; there is no universal system of notating a fight scene; most fight directors have their own method of writing moves down according to the way in which they work; four systems are those of John Barton's, Arthur Wise's, William Hobbs [the author] and the Benesh Movement Notation)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Stage combat, 1980:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Movement scores"}}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Movement scores"}}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Motion scores"}}, {"@id": "_:neb1f1dbdad5f489b964ced9e78744554b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Motion scores"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026117"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026118", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aDance scores", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026118", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b1"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dance scores"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026091"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026117"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b5"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b6"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b7"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b8"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b9"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b10"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b11"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b12"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b13"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b14"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b15"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b18"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b21"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established January 2025.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2025-01-27T10:29:49"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-10-26T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dance scores"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page ii (Benesh movement notation scores; scores)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Benesh movement notation score catalogue, \u00a91986:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (theatrical dance scores) page v (dance score is analogous to a music score. It is the representation of an ephemeral art in a tangible format. It is usually created at the time the work is being staged. In addition to the details of the dance steps, a score contains floor patterns and information about the motivations and nuances as detailed by the choreographer or stager as the work is refined. It also details the relationship of the dance to the music. The dance score is laid out in measures that correspond to the music measures. There are exceptions to this, as with Doris Humphrey's Water study, which is performed to silence, or works such as Paul Taylor's Speaking in tongues, which is set to Matthew Patton's music for magnetic tape. Dance scores allow time for studying a particular movement without distorting it or losing the sense of the movement by slowing it down. They also allow study of the movement without the obstruction of lights, costume, props, etc. The scores generally contain additional information in an introductory portion of the document. This information can cover such topics as historical, stylistic, and production information, as well as bibliographies and cast lists) page vi (dance and movement notation scores; notated scores; Labanotation score)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "http://www.dancenotation.org/library/pdf/NTD_2009.pdf"}, "Notated theatrical dances, 2009:"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (the process of recording movement on paper) page viii (choreographic score) page xiv (Dance notation is the translation of four-dimensional movements (time being the fourth dimension) into signs written on two-dimensional paper. (Note: a fifth 'dimension' --dynamics -- should also be considered an integral part, though it is not.) Dance notation is (or should be) to dance what music notation is to music and the written word to drama) page 129 (score; dance score) page 131 (notated score)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dance notation, 1984:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (being a collection of figure dances, of several numbers, compos'd by the most eminent masters; describ'd in characters after the newest manner of Monsieur Feuillet)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "An essay for the further improvement of dancing, 1711:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (translated into Labanotation) title page verso (Nijinsky's score) page xiii, etc. (Labanotation score) page 55 (Labanotation score; score) page 71 (choreographic score)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Nijinsky's Faune restored, \u00a91991:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (a comparison of dance notation systems from the fifteenth century to the present) page xi (score) page 66 (notated score) page 170 (dance score)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Choreo-graphics, 1998:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(A dance score is a written or visual representation of a choreographer's vision for a dance piece. It serves as a blueprint for the dancers, providing them with the necessary information to execute the choreography accurately. The score typically includes details such as movement sequences, timing, spacing, dynamics, and any other specific instructions that the choreographer wants the dancers to follow. Dance scores can take various forms, including written descriptions, diagrams, notations, or even video recordings. They are essential tools for both the choreographer and the dancers, helping to ensure that the artistic vision is realized on stage.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "SF Conservatory of Dance website, viewed November 21, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dance score disambiguation page (Dance score may refer to: Dance notation that describes a dance; Sheet music for a dance; A performance score given by a judge at a dance competition) article Dance notation (A dance score is a recorded dance notation that describes a particular dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed November 21, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(partition chor\u00e9graphique; partition [cataloger's translation: choreographic score; score]; piece of paper that reflects much more than just steps, but also emotions, states, intentions; dance notation; symbols and codes to notate dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://master-danse.fr/partition-choregraphique/"}, "Master danse website, viewed November 17, 2023"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (a graphics editor to describe body position and movement using Benesh movement notation) page i (Benesh movement notation is an ideographic language, written on stave lines akin to those used in music, for recording human body positions and movement in three dimensions. Its primary use has been to record a wide repertoire of dances. The preparation and revision of dance scores is a lengthy and error-prone task; this document describes a computerised editor designed to assist in this notation process) page 39 (score)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "ChoreoScribe, 1985:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Scores, Dance"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Scores, Dance"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Choreographic scores"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Choreographic scores"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b21", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b22"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Notated scores (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf800895dec549788d9aa0534acf0556b22", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Notated scores (Dance)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026118"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026122", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aIsekai manga", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026122", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb1"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Isekai manga"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026331"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2022026036"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb5"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb6"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb7"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb8"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb9"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb10"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb11"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb12"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb13"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established January 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-11-03T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/npkmc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-01-17T15:46:25"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Isekai manga"}}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(term translates to \"different world,\" \"another world\" or \"otherworld\"; \"subgenre of portal fantasy\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Isekai, via Wikipedia, viewed Sep. 28, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 1 (\"Shungiku Okapi defines the modern isekai as a story in which an 'average Japanese person is teleported to another world, where they achieve all different kinds of success. They achieve a lofty status, fame, treasures, power, and a fulfilling life, in which everything is smooth sailing.' In addition to the otherworldly journey, a tale of sudden, extreme growth has become a hallmark of the genre, one that fans actively seek out\") (OCoLC)1192498574"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Muhamed, F. Rewriting your own narrative : Isekai as a contemporary coming of age tale, 2020:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"'Isekai' means (roughly) parallel world, and has come to denote the sub-genre of story in which a person from the real, mundane world finds him or herself in a radically different world; this parallel reality often has monsters and magic, and broadly resembles an epic computer or console RPG, only with unlimited processing power\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Why are there so many isekai/parallel world anime?, Jan. 31, 2017, via Anime News Network website, viewed Sep. 14, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 60 (\"isekai is ... a subcategory of speculative fiction stories in manga, anime, and light novels\") p. 69 (\"The concept of the 'cheats' in isekai stories is borrowed from the mechanics of the coding of computer games\") p. 72 (\"There is no grand narrative to isekai stories. Instead, the hundreds of individual stories follow a common set of plot points (e.g., life prior to entering the isekai, entering the isekai, the nature of the isekai, transformations of the protagonist, and motivations in the isekai) and only differ in the specifics of how the elements occur\") p. 82 (\"While computer games have a tremendous influence on isekai stories, the stories usually subvert traditional games' storylines rather than simply repeating them\") p. 83 (\"In addition to subverting plots, the stories subvert the basic assumptions of the standard game worlds\" like preferring achieving \"personal goals\" rather that being a \"hero\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Price, P. S. A survey of the story elements of Isekai manga, in Journal of anime and manga studies, v. 2 (2021):", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Series > By genre (Isekai / Another world)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Seven Seas Entertainment website, viewed Sep. 6, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Isekai is a subgenre of fantasy in which a character is suddenly transported from their world into a new or unfamiliar one\"; also called \"'trapped in another world manga.' That phrase hits on the core difference between isekai manga and other adventure stories. In isekai stories, the main protagonist must be either physically/mentally spirited away to another world, or reborn into this new world in a new form more powerful than their previous one.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Pagan, A. A beginner's guide to isekai, July 15, 2019, via the New York Public Library blog, viewed July 28, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(summary: \"The light-hearted isekai tale about a fantasy pharmacist that inspired the anime! When corporate drone Reiji wakes up in another world, he's disappointed to discover that he hasn't been granted any combat skills. Instead, his Medicine Making ability allows him to brew powerful potions - which quickly become popular enough for him to open his own drugstore in the countryside!\") (OCoLC)1199057247"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Kennoji. Drugstore in another world : the slow life of a cheat pharmacist, 2021", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"This fast and funny action isekai manga\") (OCoLC)1350637807"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Sumimori. S. Am I actually the strongest, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Parallel world manga"}}, {"@id": "_:nc8b14f977b2b40cb8e93f0a24a66aa8eb14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Parallel world manga"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026122"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026123", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aLitRPG (Fiction)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026123", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b1"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "LitRPG (Fiction)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026333"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026529"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b5"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b6"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b7"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b8"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b9"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b10"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b13"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b16"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established February 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Fantasy or science fiction incorporating the conventions of computer role playing games, including statistics and game mechanics."}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-11-03T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/miul"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-03-04T12:36:59"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "LitRPG (Fiction)"}}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "definitions of genre tags (\"LitRPG: Novels where linear progression, such as levels, are main themes of the story. Almost always shows stat boxes, EXP gain, and other notifications.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Royal Road website, viewed Sept. 6, 2023:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "back cover (In a new virtual reality game, Jason finds himself on the path to becoming the game's villain. However, he suspects that there is something unusual going on within the game world.) (OCoLC)986497338"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Bagwell, T. Awaken online. Book 1, Catharsis, 2016:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"LitRPG, short for literary role playing game, is a literary genre combining the conventions of computer RPGs with science-fiction and fantasy novels. The term was introduced in 2013. In LitRPG, games or game-like challenges form an essential part of the story, and visible RPG statistics (for example strength, intelligence, damage) are a significant part of the reading experience\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed Sep. 27, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "experience points webpage (Experience Points (or EXP) are a numerical way of tracking lessons learned and Character Development, the bread and butter of many RPGs. These are used to either track character progression for leveling up, or to allow a character to buy and upgrade abilities.) statistics webpage (Stats (short for Statistics) are numbers that represent and determine your character's abilities such as how many hits your character can take, how high they can jump and how much damage they can deal)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "TV Tropes website, viewed February 23, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "What is LitRPG (\"LitRPG are stories where the characters exist in a game world and where the RPG mechanics of the game are visible to the characters, who typically have to master the game to succeed\"; \"A defining characteristic of LitRPG is that you are reading about a character who is progressing in an online game or game-like world. And in that read, you can experience an RPG in a fashion that actual play thwarts: you can vicariously enjoy levelling up fast and engaging with the best high-end encounters that the game has to offer\"; term LitRPG originated in Russia in 2013; \"What is GameLit? Novels in which the main character is playing or inside a game, without the reader seeing much or any of the game mechanics\") What are the best GameLit books? (\"Broadly speaking, GameLit and LitRPG are the same. They are books where we see characters navigating a game. Their world itself might be the game, or they might be characters who have challenges to meet within a game, one that they enter in avatar form\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Level Up Publishing website, viewed Sep. 14, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Progression fantasy fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Progression fantasy fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Literary role playing games (Fiction)"}}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Literary role playing games (Fiction)"}}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "GameLit (Fiction)"}}, {"@id": "_:ne9acdd326de0412f820b1de0a2130b68b17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "GameLit (Fiction)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026123"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026124", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aIsekai anime (Television programs)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026124", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b1"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Isekai anime (Television programs)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026244"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026047"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b5"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b6"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b7"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b8"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b9"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b10"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b11"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b12"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b13"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b14"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established January 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-01-17T15:46:25"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-11-03T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ic"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Isekai anime (Television programs)"}}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 60 (\"isekai is ... a subcategory of speculative fiction stories in manga, anime, and light novels\") p. 69 (\"The concept of the 'cheats' in isekai stories is borrowed from the mechanics of the coding of computer games\") p. 72 (\"There is no grand narrative to isekai stories. Instead, the hundreds of individual stories follow a common set of plot points (e.g., life prior to entering the isekai, entering the isekai, the nature of the isekai, transformations of the protagonist, and motivations in the isekai) and only differ in the specifics of how the elements occur\") p. 82 (\"While computer games have a tremendous influence on isekai stories, the stories usually subvert traditional games' storylines rather than simply repeating them\") p. 83 (\"In addition to subverting plots, the stories subvert the basic assumptions of the standard game worlds\" like preferring achieving \"personal goals\" rather that being a \"hero\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Price, P. S. A survey of the story elements of Isekai manga, in Journal of anime and manga studies, v. 2 (2021):", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(summary: \"The legend begins when a stranger confronts a teenage girl, Yoko, and she is pulled into another world to face her destiny. Thus begins Yoko's perilous and mystic journey to the Kingdom of Kei on an epic road of espionage, terror, and betrayal\") (OCoLC)62141130"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: The twelve kingdoms, 2003", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(summary: \"Ordinary 37-year-old Satoru Mikami dies and is reincarnated as the most unremarkable creature imaginable, a slime. Initially, things are pretty grim. He's blind, deaf, and weak. But by combining his special abilities with some good old-fashioned charisma, the newly named Rimuru Tempest will use his blobby powers to create a nation, destroy the malicious, and convince the world that maybe monsters aren't so bad after all\") (OCoLC)1329319482"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "That time I got reincarnated as slime, 2019", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Anime > Isekai)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://myanimelist.net/anime/genre/62/Isekai"}, "MyAnimeList, viewed Sep. 28, 2023"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(term translates to \"different world,\" \"another world\" or \"otherworld\"; \"subgenre of portal fantasy\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Isekai, via Wikipedia, viewed Sep. 28, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 1 (\"Shungiku Okapi defines the modern isekai as a story in which an 'average Japanese person is teleported to another world, where they achieve all different kinds of success. They achieve a lofty status, fame, treasures, power, and a fulfilling life, in which everything is smooth sailing.' In addition to the otherworldly journey, a tale of sudden, extreme growth has become a hallmark of the genre, one that fans actively seek out\") (OCoLC)1192498574"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Muhamed, F. Rewriting your own narrative : Isekai as a contemporary coming of age tale, 2020:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"In Japanese media, this is known as 'Isekai' with such protagonists typically being their local demographic's flavor of hero, and usually involves said character gaining RPG-like powers on arrival (or at the very least, is set in a Role-Playing Game 'Verse)\"; \"the majority of isekai stories as we know it are derived from Web Serial Novels or old stories reworked into Light Novels, with their premises and writing style even being noted as a subgenre: Narou Isekai... In Literature, this is often referred to as a 'Portal Fantasy'\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "TV Tropes, viewed July 28, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"'Isekai' means (roughly) parallel world, and has come to denote the sub-genre of story in which a person from the real, mundane world finds him or herself in a radically different world; this parallel reality often has monsters and magic, and broadly resembles an epic computer or console RPG, only with unlimited processing power\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Why are there so many isekai/parallel world anime?, Jan. 31, 2017, via Anime News Network website, viewed Sep. 14, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Isekai is a subgenre of fantasy in which a character is suddenly transported from their world into a new or unfamiliar one\"; also called \"'trapped in another world manga.' That phrase hits on the core difference between isekai manga and other adventure stories. In isekai stories, the main protagonist must be either physically/mentally spirited away to another world, or reborn into this new world in a new form more powerful than their previous one.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Pagan, A. A beginner's guide to isekai, July 15, 2019, via the New York Public Library blog, viewed July 28, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Parallel world anime (Television programs)"}}, {"@id": "_:n96bf66c5def24c2f85291ba1a5a35518b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Parallel world anime (Television programs)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026124"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026126", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aIsekai anime (Motion pictures)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026126", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b1"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Isekai anime (Motion pictures)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026242"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026046"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b5"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b6"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b7"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b8"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b9"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b10"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b11"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b12"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b13"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b14"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established January 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-11-08T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/ien"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-01-17T15:46:25"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Isekai anime (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"In Japanese media, this is known as 'Isekai' with such protagonists typically being their local demographic's flavor of hero, and usually involves said character gaining RPG-like powers on arrival (or at the very least, is set in a Role-Playing Game 'Verse)\"; \"the majority of isekai stories as we know it are derived from Web Serial Novels or old stories reworked into Light Novels, with their premises and writing style even being noted as a subgenre: Narou Isekai... In Literature, this is often referred to as a 'Portal Fantasy'\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "TV Tropes, viewed July 28, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(summary: \"When high school student Yohko Asagiri discovers that a song she wrote opens a corridor to an other-dimensional world, it is up to her and Leda, a legendary warrior, to make sure that this corridor is not used to invade the Earth\") (OCoLC)39211128"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Leda. Genmu senki = The fantastic adventure of Yohko, 1997", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 1 (\"Shungiku Okapi defines the modern isekai as a story in which an 'average Japanese person is teleported to another world, where they achieve all different kinds of success. They achieve a lofty status, fame, treasures, power, and a fulfilling life, in which everything is smooth sailing.' In addition to the otherworldly journey, a tale of sudden, extreme growth has become a hallmark of the genre, one that fans actively seek out\") (OCoLC)1192498574"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Muhamed, F. Rewriting your own narrative : Isekai as a contemporary coming of age tale, 2020:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Isekai is a subgenre of fantasy in which a character is suddenly transported from their world into a new or unfamiliar one\"; also called \"'trapped in another world manga.' That phrase hits on the core difference between isekai manga and other adventure stories. In isekai stories, the main protagonist must be either physically/mentally spirited away to another world, or reborn into this new world in a new form more powerful than their previous one.\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Pagan, A. A beginner's guide to isekai, July 15, 2019, via the New York Public Library blog, viewed July 28, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Anime > Isekai)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://myanimelist.net/anime/genre/62/Isekai"}, "MyAnimeList, viewed Sep. 28, 2023"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 60 (\"isekai is ... a subcategory of speculative fiction stories in manga, anime, and light novels\") p. 69 (\"The concept of the 'cheats' in isekai stories is borrowed from the mechanics of the coding of computer games\") p. 72 (\"There is no grand narrative to isekai stories. Instead, the hundreds of individual stories follow a common set of plot points (e.g., life prior to entering the isekai, entering the isekai, the nature of the isekai, transformations of the protagonist, and motivations in the isekai) and only differ in the specifics of how the elements occur\") p. 82 (\"While computer games have a tremendous influence on isekai stories, the stories usually subvert traditional games' storylines rather than simply repeating them\") p. 83 (\"In addition to subverting plots, the stories subvert the basic assumptions of the standard game worlds\" like preferring achieving \"personal goals\" rather that being a \"hero\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Price, P. S. A survey of the story elements of Isekai manga, in Journal of anime and manga studies, v. 2 (2021):", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(summary: \"When Kyuta, a young orphan living on the streets of Shibuya, stumbles into a fantastic world of beasts, he's taken in by Kumatetsu, a gruff, rough-around-the-edges warrior beast who's been searching for the perfect apprentice. Despite their constant bickering, Kyuta and Kumatetsu begin training together and slowly form a bond as surrogate father and son. But the unlikely pair will be put to the ultimate test; a final showdown\") (OCoLC)947734192"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: The boy and the beast, 2016", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"'Isekai' means (roughly) parallel world, and has come to denote the sub-genre of story in which a person from the real, mundane world finds him or herself in a radically different world; this parallel reality often has monsters and magic, and broadly resembles an epic computer or console RPG, only with unlimited processing power\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Why are there so many isekai/parallel world anime?, Jan. 31, 2017, via Anime News Network website, viewed Sep. 14, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(term translates to \"different world,\" \"another world\" or \"otherworld\"; \"subgenre of portal fantasy\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Isekai, via Wikipedia, viewed Sep. 28, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Parallel world anime (Motion pictures)"}}, {"@id": "_:n37974c5cddcb4dc79b81722db6577211b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Parallel world anime (Motion pictures)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026126"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026127", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSacred dances", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026127", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b1"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sacred dances"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026091"}, "madsrdf:hasReciprocalAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026137"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b5"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b6"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b7"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b8"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b9"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b11"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b14"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b17"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established October 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Dance compositions expressing personal or communal religious or spiritual beliefs, for use in devotional settings and/or general performance."}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-11-16T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-18T07:01:47"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sacred dances"}}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 4 (Sacred dance; Dance can be divided into four categories: 1. Reflexive dance. Each dancer focuses on his/her own kinesthetic experience. 2. Participatory dance. The dancers' attention addresses their interaction with each other. 3. Sacred dance. The message is intended for supernatural beings. 4. Presentational dance. Performed for an external human audience.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dance Research Journal, Spring, 1995:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "article Liturgical Dance (The terms sacred dance, liturgical dance, and religious dance are often used interchangeably. However, liturgical dance suggests that which is performed only as part of the liturgy or worship service, whereas sacred or religious dance suggests a broader category of dance. The styles of sacred dance are diverse; modern dance is probably most common today, but folk, ballet, and jazz are also popular. Like dance in general, sacred dance is exploring the blending of art forms. Mime and drama are some of the increasingly common combinations. What sets liturgical or sacred dance apart is its intention to inspire a religious experience in the congregation or audience and the dancer. The creative source of the dance is considered to be God.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The International Encyclopedia of Dance via Oxford Reference, viewed November 16, 2023:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 11 (choreography of liturgical dances) pages 16-65 ([includes choreographies of 5 liturgical dances])"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Dancing for God, 1969:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Religious dance; UF: Liturgical dance; Religious dance, Modern [Former heading]; Ritual dance; BT Dance; NT: Buddhist dance; Christian dance; Shinto dance; Sacred dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, viewed November 16, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford dictionary of dance (2 ed.)", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sacred dance page (Sacred dance is the use of dance in religious ceremonies and rituals, present in most religions throughout history and prehistory. Its connection with the human body and fertility has caused it to be forbidden by some religions; for example, some branches of Christianity and Islam have prohibited dancing. Dance has formed a major element of worship in Hindu temples, with strictly formalized styles such as Bharatanatyam, which require skilled dancers and temple musicians. In the 20th century, sacred dance has been revived by choreographers such as Bernhard Wosien as a means of developing community spirit.) article Workship dance (Worship dance or liturgical dance take on several forms of sacred dance in Christianity and Messianic Judaism, and is usually incorporated into liturgies or worship services) Ceremonial dance page (Ceremonial dance; major category or classification of dance forms or dance styles, where the purpose is ceremonial or ritualistic. It is related to and overlaps with sacred dance and ecstatic dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia viewed December 12, 2023:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Worship dances"}}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Worship dances"}}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Liturgical dances"}}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Liturgical dances"}}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Religious dances"}}, {"@id": "_:nca0343e92ea84d8c90699171515dc337b18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Religious dances"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026127"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026131", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aConcert dances", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026131", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b1"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Concert dances"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026091"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026112"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026001"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b5"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b6"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b7"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b8"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b9"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b10"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b11"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b12"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b13"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b14"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b15"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b18"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b21"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b24"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b27"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b30"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established May 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Dances for staged performance, usually associated with music, whether created as an integral part of the work or to otherwise accompany or enhance it."}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-05-22T14:04:10"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-12-05T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Concert dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed February 29, 2024: Dance page (Theatrical dance; also called performance or concert dance; intended primarily as a spectacle, usually a performance upon a stage by virtuoso dancers) Concert dance page (also known as performance dance or theatre dance in the United Kingdom; dance performed for an audience; frequently performed in a theatre setting, though this is not a requirement; usually choreographed and performed to set music.)", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(DNB Notated Theatrical Dances Catalog)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dance Notation Bureau website, viewed February 29, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Stage dances", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (containing a great number of the best ball and stage dances; composed [i.e. choreographed] by Monsieur L'Abbe, dancing-master to Their Royal Highnesses; that have been performed both in Druy-Lane [sic] and Lincoln's-Inn-Fields by the best dancers; recollected, put in characters, and engraved by Monsieur Roussau, dancing-master)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: A new collection of dances, 1725:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(theatrical dance; dance is a form of art with two distinct forms: theatrical dance in which dancers perform for an audience, and participatory social dance where dancing in a group is encouraged by anyone)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dance Facts website, viewed February 29, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "The rationale of dance page (\"Presentational dance\" is a term used to indicate dances presented in designated spaces where an audience watches passively (\"performance dance,\" \"concert dance\" and \"theatrical dance\" are the substitutes))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Notes on the performing arts website, viewed February 29, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Theatrical dance; types include intermedi, ballet de cour, masque)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Encyclopedia.com, viewed February 29, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikidata, viewed February 29, 2024 (Concert dance; variants: performance dance; theatre dance; scenic dance)", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Theatrical dance; A broad category comprising all genres of dance created to be performed in a theater or a theatrical context. See Ballet; Jazz dance; Minstrelsy; Modern dance; Musical theater dance; and Tap dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Grove Dictionary of American Music (2 ed.) via Oxford reference, viewed February 29, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Concert dance is the most frequently used term; less commonly used terms: theatrical dance, stage dance, theatre dance, dramatic dance, performance dance, presentational dance, scenic dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Ngram, viewed April 23, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Presentational dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Presentational dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Theater dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Theater dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b21", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b22"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dramatic dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b22", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dramatic dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b24", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b25"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Theatrical dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b25", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Theatrical dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b27", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b28"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Performance dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b28", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Performance dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b30", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b31"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Scenic dances"}}, {"@id": "_:ncf4d886cecd645f89ef58a8a1ff81be6b31", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Scenic dances"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026131"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026132", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aImprovisations (Dance)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026132", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b1"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Improvisations (Dance)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026091"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b5"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b6"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b7"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b9"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b12"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b15"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established November 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Dances or transcriptions of dance performances that consist wholly or primarily of improvised dance content."}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-11-14T09:15:22"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-12-05T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Improvisations (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Improvisation in dance; UF Dance improvisations; Dancing improvisation; Improvisational dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, viewed April 8, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Improvisation: the creating of one's own dance while dancing; The dancer simultaneously originates and performs movement without pre-planning. It is thus creative movement of the moment)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dance words, 1995:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Improvisation; Dance improvisation can be defined as the spontaneous exploration of human movement possibilities. It is an important element in performance dance, as well as in ritual, social, educational, and therapeutic dance.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The international encyclopedia of dance, via Oxford Reference, viewed April 8, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "video file description (Students of the Barbara Mettler Studios are seen in three studies in group dance improvisation) (OCoLC)1323147288"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work Cat: Three excerpts from a group dance improvisation [VR], 1979:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dancing improvisations"}}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dancing improvisations"}}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dance improvisations"}}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Dance improvisations"}}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Improvisational dances"}}, {"@id": "_:n0715b76285e441ad8add0eb257de67f4b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Improvisational dances"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026132"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026134", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aExperimental dances", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026134", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b1"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Experimental dances"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026091"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b5"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b6"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b7"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b9"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established October 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Dances that are defined by sensibilities radically opposed to or pushing the boundaries of institutionalized aesthetic conventions in dance at the time they were choreographed."}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-12-05T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-18T07:01:46"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Experimental dances"}}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "container (early works 1966-1979; during the 1960, Brown created her own works which attempted to defy gravity, using equipment such as ropes and harnesses, to allow dancers to walk on or down walls or to experiment with the dynamics of stability (...) During the 1970s Brown worked with her company on developing and expanding her choreography most often in alternative spaces and venues; [videodisc contains filmed performances of 18 dances by Brown])"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Trisha Brown [VR], 2004", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 7 (What is experimental dance? No definition will do unless it covers the extreme possibilities at either end of the artistic range. Like, for instance, the man in New York inside a cube of plastic sheeting, wearing no clothes but jumping energetically in and out of pools of paint. Or the white-costumed group in The Hague dancing with no apparent emotion to calm, gentle music in a changing context of square structures below, above or around them. It must include pure-dance works in the classical technique by George Balanchine and muti-media presentations by producers who never had a dance lesson in their lives)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Experimental dance, 1971:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Experimental dance explores the images, sounds, concepts, revelations and complexities of our time. It does so by instigating transient structures derived through the manipulation of movement, media, technology and conceptual frameworks. Makers of experimental dance attempt to create new dances for the new moment. They believe that the forms of the past, though instructive, no longer speak to our dynamic and changing world. Experimental dance takes no one form or style. So much of the media with which we interact tells us how to respond to it, providing cues for our reactions. Experimental dance operates outside this system; it serves no one social purpose. Its form and content aren't laced with one rarified interpretation or meaning locked inside a terpsichorean puzzle. Rather, experimental choreographers create experiences a viewer can imagine \"in parallel to.\" In other words, there is nothing to \"get,\" but there is everything to experience.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Minnesota playlist website, viewed May 17, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "video abstract (choreography by Merce Cunningham; Split Sides (2003) was a work for the full company of fourteen dancers. The choreography, music and design elements for the dance were each created in two parts, which were varied for each performance. Chance procedures decided which of the two versions of each element would be paired together. Mathematically, there were thirty-two different possible versions of Split Sides. Cunningham choreographed the two 20-minute sections with the aid of the software DanceForms. There were a number of ensemble (often unison) passages, solos, duets and trios that feature much inventive partnering and complex movement patterns. The order of the choreographic sections, music compositions, d\u00e9cor, costumes and lighting cues were all determined onstage, prior to the opening of the dance by the rolling of dice.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Split Sides 45 [VR], 2006:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Avant-garde dances"}}, {"@id": "_:nce05df7a92174501bc9115e2575f1416b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Avant-garde dances"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026134"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026137", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCeremonial dances", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026137", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b1"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ceremonial dances"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026091"}, "madsrdf:hasReciprocalAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026127"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b5"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b6"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b7"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b8"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b9"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b10"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b13"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established October 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-18T07:01:46"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-12-12T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ceremonial dances"}}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title screen (the ceremonial dance of the !Kung Bushmen) opening frames (N/um Tchai which means \"medicine dance\" is a formalized curing ceremony)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: N/um Tchai [VR], 2009:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 37 (Ceremonial dances: Display dances which fulfil some of the criteria of purpose, place, performer, time of year associated with ceremonial forms, but not all.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dance words, 1995:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(ritualistic and ceremonial Dance; dance that is of a ritualistic or ceremonial nature: Native American war dance, expressing prayer for success and thanksgiving for victory; the dance of exorcism or healing, performed by shamans to drive out evil spirits; the dance of invocation, calling on the gods for help in farming, hunting, the fertility of human beings and animals, and other tribal concerns; initiation dances for secret societies; mimetic dances, illustrating events in tribal history, legend, or mythology; dances representing cosmic processes; and, more rarely, the dance of courtship, an invocation for success in love)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Infoplease, viewed June 18, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(no entries for ceremonial dance; term used in 46 entries)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The international encyclopedia of dance, via Oxford Reference, viewed December 12, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Ceremonial dance; major category or classification of dance forms or dance styles, where the purpose is ceremonial or ritualistic. It is related to and overlaps with sacred dance and ecstatic dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed December 12, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ritualistic dances"}}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ritualistic dances"}}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ritual dances"}}, {"@id": "_:n8627c8ad16d3433981d32482a59a7389b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ritual dances"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026137"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026142", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aMinuets (Dance)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026142", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b1"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Minuets (Dance)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026008"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b5"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b6"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b7"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b8"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b9"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b10"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established February 2025.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-12-14T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2025-02-04T11:18:26"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Minuets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Minuet; The name derives from the French pas menu, or small step. It was probably originally a peasant dance from the Poitou region of France before becoming fashionable as a court dance under Louis XIV. Its popularity among the aristocracy spread far outside France. A dance in triple time, it is both dignified and leisurely in execution, featuring many curtseys and deep bows.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford dictionary of dance (2 ed.), via Oxford reference, viewed December 14, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 41 (The slow minuitt composed by Mr. Thomas Caverley; written in carractters by Kennelm Tomlinson) page 78 (A minuet compos'd & writ in to Characters by Kellom Tomlinson; danc'd by Mrs. Scolding in the Island Princess May 10, 1716) page 95 (Mr. Caverley's Slow minuet by Mr. Pemberton) page 96 (Mr. Caverley's Slow minuet; a new dance for a girl; writt by M. Pemberton) (DLC)92002787"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: A work book by Kellom Tomlinson, \u00a91992:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Minuet; The term minuet (Fr., menuet) usually denotes a French court and theater dance in moderate or slow meter, but it can also refer to a musical composition or a symphonic movement. The menuet ordinaire superseded Louis XIV's favorite dance, the courante, and remained the most popular ballroom danse \u00e0 deux (couple dance) in aristocratic society until the years following the French Revolution in 1789)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The international encyclopedia of dance, via Oxford Reference, viewed December 14, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pages 139 (Menuet de la reine by M. Marcel) pages 145 (Menuet dauphin by M. Marcel) page 157 (Menuet d'Exaudet by M. Magny) (OCoLC)19858661"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Principes de chor\u00e9graphie, 1980:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 4 of cover (Menuet de la cour or Court minuet; the most popular social dance of all time; choreographed dance that lasted a 150-year) pages 31 to 137 (includes facsimiles of scores and textual descriptions of twenty choreographies of the Menuet de la cour)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Menuet de la cour, 2007:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Menuets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:n0b245e164e414674bd67e0e175145fb3b11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Menuets (Dance)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026142"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026147", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aStreet music", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2023026147", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b1"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Street music"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026821"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b5"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b6"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b7"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b8"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b9"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b10"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b11"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b14"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established August 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2023-12-20T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-08-22T14:19:21"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Street music"}}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 1 ( the literature shows that street music plays a significant social and cultural role at local and international levels)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://ueaeprints.uea.ac.uk/id/eprint/70955/1/Bennett_McKay_Street_Music_report_interactive.pdf"}, "Bennett, Elizabeth, and George McKay. From Brass Bands to Buskers: Street Music in the UK, 2019."], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(blog compiling street music performances from all over the world)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://worldstreetmusic.com/"}, "World Street Music WWW site, viewed Dec. 20, 2023"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Street music (\"For centuries, street musicians, or buskers, have been plying their trade the world over...The genres of music include street cries and brass bands, along with [...] solo instruments such as the harp, violin, voice, and accordion\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford bibliographies, searched Dec. 20, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove Music", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(street music; the musical life of urban people has been lived not in the concert hall, opera house, or church, but in the streets...Such music might have encompassed anything from the 'street cries' of a pedlar to choral music or symphonies. Like folk music, as a genuine popular culture, in danger of extinction - today, street music survives mainly as 'busking', performed by music students and unemployed musicians, and in street festivals)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford Companion to Music online, viewed July 10, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(compilation of archival recordings of the artist's street music)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Moondog on the streets of New York:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Busking (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Busking (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Street performance (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:na1d82f6392834a869da4dfe0bc919f57b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Street performance (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026147"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026001", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aChaconnes (Dance)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026001", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b1"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconnes (Dance)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026131"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b5"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b6"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b8"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b11"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b14"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established October 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-18T07:01:46"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-01-02T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconnes (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "jpeg page 39 (A chacone by Mr.Issac [notated in Beauchamp-Feuillet notation]) (OCoLC) 612750294"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: An essay for the further improvement of dancing, 1711:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Chaconne (Sp., chacona; It., ciaccona; Eng., chacoon); The Chaconne and Passacaille were two dances in triple meter that developed independently in the early seventeenth century but that both evolved into extended compositions based on continuous variation over a ground, or ostinato. As dances, both reached the height of their development in French operas of the late seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries. Of the two, the chaconne has a longer history as a dance, spanning two hundred years, from the late sixteenth to the late eighteenth century, whereas the passacaille developed later and declined in popularity sooner. As performed in France, both were primarily, if not exclusively, theatrical dances and were among the most difficult of their period because of the virtuosic technique required of the dancers and the stamina needed for these longest of Baroque dances)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The international encyclopedia of dance, via Oxford Reference, viewed January 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(chaconne; A dance in triple time, which probably originated in Spain (where it is known as the chacona). It began as a sensuous dance for a couple, but by the time it featured in the ballet de cour and the ballet operas of Lully and Rameau it had acquired a more modest refinement)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Oxford dictionary of dance (2 ed.), via Oxford reference, viewed January 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chacoons (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chacoons (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chacones (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chacones (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconas (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:na65d0076bb864d1d83bc470c4c21e050b15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chaconas (Dance)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026001"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026003", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aSocial tragedies (Drama)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026003", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab1"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Social tragedies (Drama)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026576"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab5"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab6"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab7"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab8"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab9"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established April 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-04-24T10:15:03"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-01-02T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Social tragedies (Drama)"}}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 2 (the middle class social drama of mid-nineteenth century Germany) p. 16 (An example of this might be a moral and cultural value such as innocence, having to do with honesty and simplicity. This value may be transmitted in a number of ways in a social tragedy, either directly through the dialogue or indirectly in the description of actual characters. It may also be transmitted in relation to its opposite: deceit and avarice) p. 25 (Statistics compiled by Helmut Schanze for the King's Theater in Berlin in the period ranging from 1850 to 1890 suggest... the productions which enjoyed the greatest success were middle class comedies, historical dramas, and social tragedies) p. 29 (the genre of middle class social tragedy) (OCoLC)1194816358"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Quanbeck, Randi Marie. The politics of sentiment : middle class social tragedy and regressive utopias, 1992:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Social tragedy. A form of tragedy which presents the fate of hero and heroine as determined by class background, social circumstance, parental attitude and childhood experience. The genre emerged in the later nineteenth century out of a deterministic social Darwinism. ... deal with characters whose aspirations to personal freedom and control are blocked from without by public attitudes, and from within by acquired or inherited tendencies)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hodgson, Terry. The drama dictionary, 1988", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(In the history of tragedy the rise of modern social tragedy proves extremely significant... Modern social tragedy always deals with some sort of complex critical problems - social anomalies and contradictions. It depicts the clash between individual passions and inclinations and social rigid conventions. In a social tragedy the individual is always treated against the mighty force of society, he suffers and perishes; The protagonist in a social tragedy is the helpless victim of tragic impact of different social force)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Literature analysis (Website), Jan. 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)2117221"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Sitwell, Osbert. All at sea : a social tragedy in three acts for first-class passengers only, 1927.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2ccabb611f294592800c1ab5a53c86eab9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)18315620"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Johnson, Vernon Elso. Dramatic influences in the development of Arthur Miller's concept of social tragedy, 1962.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026003"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026004", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aMartial arts comics", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026004", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b1"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Martial arts comics"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026266"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b5"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b6"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b7"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b8"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b9"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b10"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b12"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established April 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-04-24T10:15:03"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-01-05T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/npkmc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Martial arts comics"}}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 77 (director Andrew Lau Wai-Keung \"is also known for the two film adaptations he made from martial arts comics, The Storm Riders (1998) and A Man Called Hero (1999)\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "East Asian cinemas : exploring transnational connections on film, 2008:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Official genres list (genre \"martial arts\"; \"Works featuring stories centered on characters who use the fighting styles developed in East Asia and similar fighting styles\"; keywords: kung fu, karate, judo; examples: Master of Kung Fu, Street Fighter, Jimmy Chang)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grand Comics Database Wiki, viewed Jan. 5, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Genres > Martial arts)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Comikey website, viewed Jan. 5, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a martial arts comic; summary: \"Vol. 4: now with more kicks, punches, rock & roll, subspace, half-ninjas, experience points, samurai swords, girly action and laughable attempts to seek gainful employment!\") (OCoLC)921571881"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "O'Malley, B. L. Scott Pilgrim gets it together, 2007", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a martial arts comic; summary: \"By 1974, Kung Fu fever struck the United States. Each issue of Deadly Hands featured...the comic-art adventures of Marvel's greatest Kung fu heroes\") (OCoLC)945352711"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: The Deadly hands of Kung Fu omnibus. Volume 1, 2016", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Genres (\"Martial arts\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Comic Online Free website, viewed Jan. 5, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Category: Comics by genre (\"Martial arts comics\") Category: Martial arts comics (\"Collects comics that are written in or reflect the style of martial arts films\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed Jan. 5, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kung fu comics"}}, {"@id": "_:nc6403d8e000b40edab9d35f8d128b491b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kung fu comics"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026004"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026008", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCourt dances", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026008", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b1"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Court dances"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026091"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026101"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026142"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b5"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b6"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b7"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b8"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b9"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b10"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b11"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b12"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established February 2025.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2025-02-04T11:18:26"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-01-23T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Court dances"}}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Court dance: a grave and stately dance suitable for court functions; distinguished from folk dance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam-Webster Dictionary online, viewed January 24, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Court dance: any of various elaborate, stately dances originating in or popular at royal courts, esp. those of Europe in the 17th and 18th centuries, such as the minuet, courante, etc.; such dances collectively)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford English Dictionary online, viewed January 24, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (reconstructed by: Dr. Ingrid Brainard; notated by: Ray Cook) table of contents page (Bassadanze Chiamata, Casulle la novele; Verceppe; recorded in Labanotation) (OCoLC)3700162"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Three court dances of the early Renaissance, 1977:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Court dance (For court dance traditions in western Europe, see Ballet Technique, History of, article on French Court Dance; and Social Dance, article on Court and Social Dance before 1800. For similar traditions in Japan, see Bugaku; Gagaku; and Japan, overview article. See also Cambodia; China, overview article; Ghana; Indonesia, article on Javanese Dance Traditions; Korea, overview article; Malaysia; Okinawa; and Taiwan)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The international encyclopedia of dance, via Oxford Reference, viewed October 17, 2023:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(no entry for court dance; term court dance appears in 16 entries)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford dictionary of dance (2 ed.), via Oxford reference, viewed January 23, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (a treatise on courtly dance, together with the choreography and music of 49 dances)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Nobilt\u00e0 di dame, 1986:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 339 (Court dances: a term used in television, film, and theatre for dances to be performed by actors in period pieces, dances which cannot and do not attempt authenticity but which convey the flavour of the period and situation and enable the action of the play to continue through them)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dance words, 1995:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Courtly dances"}}, {"@id": "_:n4a89fd8ce8b54edb849bbeb4bd3ab915b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Courtly dances"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026008"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026009", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aIntroductory works", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026009", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b1"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Introductory works"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026113"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026114"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b5"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b6"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b7"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b8"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b9"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b10"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b11"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b12"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b13"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b14"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b15"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b16"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established May 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Works that provide basic elementary information about a subject."}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-05-22T14:04:10"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-01-23T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Introductory works"}}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(introduction 1 : something that introduces: such as a(1) : a part of a book or treatise preliminary to the main portion (2) : a preliminary treatise or course of study)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam-Webster dictionary online, Jan. 23, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(introduction: a book or course that provides basic knowledge about a subject: e.g., an introduction to psychology)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Cambridge dictionary online, Jan. 23, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)30669988"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Bandstra, Barry L. Reading the Old Testament : an introduction to the Hebrew Bible, \u00a91995.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(free-floating form subdivision --Introductions. SN Use as a form subdivision under uniform titles of sacred works.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Jan. 23, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(introduction: The part of a book in which the subject, purpose, and limits of the work are briefly stated, and the reader prepared for the treatment of the subject that follows in the text, usually written by the author or a recognized authority in the field. The introduction normally appears in the front matter, following the preface or foreword, but may sometimes take the form of the first chapter. In either case, it is considered an integral part of the work and is not necessarily changed in subsequent editions, as is the preface. Abbreviated introd. Compare with prologue. Also refers to a book written for persons unfamiliar with its subject to provide information at an elementary level, the title often beginning with the words Introduction to... or ending in the subtitle An Introduction.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Online dictionary for library and information science, Jan. 19, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)59756040"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Scripture : an ecumenical introduction to the Bible and its interpretation, 2005.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)866857562"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Harris, Stephen L. The New Testament : a student's introduction, 2015.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)646112356"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Springer, Joseph Tucker. An introduction to zoology, \u00a92013.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)1303568176"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Asim, Jabari. A child's introduction to jazz, 2022.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(introduction 4: an elementary treatise: e.g., an introduction to botany)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dictionary.com, Jan. 23, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b15", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)1289614991"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Morrison, Val. Introduction to health psychology, 2022.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Introductions (Introductory works)"}}, {"@id": "_:n7ac298b34cc043088efcf9aa1df1fa24b17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Introductions (Introductory works)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026009"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026010", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aCloset drama", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026010", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b1"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Closet drama"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026297"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b5"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b6"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b7"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b8"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b9"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b10"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b11"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b12"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b13"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b14"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b15"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b16"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b17"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b20"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established May 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Plays written solely or primarily for reading rather than for performance on stage."}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-05-22T14:04:10"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-01-30T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Closet drama"}}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a closet drama) (OCoLC)438102"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Shelley, Percy Bysshe. Prometheus unbound, 1968", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(closet drama. A play intended for reading rather than performance on stage. Seneca's dramas were probably written to be read aloud and include extravagant scenes that would be very difficult to stage. During the Romantic and Victorian era many established poets wrote closet dramas)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Methuen Drama dictionary of the theatre, 2011", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Closet Drama. A dramatic text that is meant to be read rather than performed, at least in its original conception. The plays are read only in a group or individually, which is meant to ensure a more sustained attention to the literary beauties of this \"dramatic poem.\" The first author of such a \"theatre for reading\" was Seneca, but the genre flourished mainly in the nineteenth century. ... The contemporary trend is to stage all kinds of texts, including those considered unstageable)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Pavis, Patrice. Dictionary of the theatre, \u00a91998", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)894819677"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Wallace, Albany. Elfrida : a drama in five acts (design'd for the closet, not the stage), 1850.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Shelley wrote the play to be a closet drama, which is a play not performed on stage but rather played out in the reader's mind)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Prometheus unbound by Percy Bysshe Shelley, via Study.com, Jan. 30, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Closet drama. SN Here are entered works on plays written to be read rather than performed on stage. UF Closet plays. BT Drama)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Jan. 30, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pp. i-iii (closet drama; closet play; a play deliberately intended to be read and not acted; plays more readable than actable--giving their true and best rewards in the closet and not in the theatre; this is what makes the closet dramas a class by themselves; undue emphasis on poetry, character, psychology, philosophy, subjectivity or reform, cultivated largely to the exclusion of other elements or not fully harmonised with the dramatic. In these plays 'literature' and 'drama' do not become organically one: often they merely 'co-exist') p. iv (the growth of the literary closet play, on the one hand, and of the unliterary theatrical drama, on the other; closet plays are a highly significant part of the non-dramatic literature of the age; these plays' lasting contribution also lies in the fact that they were, and are still, a challenge to the theatre and the merely theatrical evaluation of drama) p. 1 (What makes drama a distinct art form is the fact that, unlike other forms of literature, it is written primarily to be acted. ... When this fact is lost sight of, a freedom of treatment is attained, but the borders of drama become nebulous and mingle with those of the other realms of literature ... The result is 'closet drama' - a drama which, on account of an undue predominance of the 'literary' element, 'reads' much better than it acts (if it is at all intended to be produced), and communicates its full and characteristic pleasure in reading and not in a theatrical performance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Mathur, O.P. The closet drama of the Romantic Revival, 1978:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(closet drama. A play written for reading as opposed to actual performance. Works of this type include the plays of Seneca and much Victorian poetic drama)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The new Penguin dictionary of the theatre, 2001", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Closet drama. (a) Drama written to be read in the study like a novel; (b) drama meant for reading aloud, rather than performance; (c) drama more suitable for reading, whatever the original intention. The many dramatic attempts of the Romantic poets, of which Byron's Manfred (1817) and Shelley's The Cenic (1819) are best known, are often considered closet drama in the last sense of the term)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hodgson, Terry. The drama dictionary, \u00a91988", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(closet drama, plays that were written to be read rather than performed, or as \"mental theater\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Prometheus unbound and on stage, via The Romanticist Research Group of New York University blog, posted Nov. 21, 2013, viewed on Jan. 30, 2023", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b15", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Closet drama, a drama suited primarily for reading rather than production. Examples of the genre include John Milton's Samson Agonistes (1671) and Thomas Hardy's The Dynasts (three parts, 1903-08). Closet drama is not to be confused with readers' theatre, in which actors read or recite without decor before an audience.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Britannica online, Jan. 30, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b16", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(closet drama. A play written to be read, but not to be performed. Popular in the Romantic age among such poets as Percy Shelley (Prometheus Unbound), Johann Goethe (Faust), and Lord Byron (Manfred))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Mobley, J.P. NTC's dictionary of theatre and drama terms, \u00a91992", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Closet plays"}}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Closet plays"}}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b20", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b21"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Closet dramas"}}, {"@id": "_:n7498b9eda8fa43db92e2afe80ba0a6d1b21", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Closet dramas"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026010"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026013", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aRegimental histories", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026013", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b1"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Regimental histories"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026113"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b5"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b6"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b7"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b8"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b9"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b10"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b11"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b12"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b13"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b16"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b19"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b22"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b25"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b28"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established June 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-02-07T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-06-12T11:53:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Regimental histories"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Regimental histories are valuable research sources that offer a unique perspective for the study of the Civil War. They provide details on the organization and achievements of a particular unit, focusing on wartime activities. A regimental history can provide context and clarity to the personal history of an individual soldier for a researcher)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://guides.loc.gov/civil-war-regimental-histories"}, "Elsbury, Will. Civil War regimental histories : a guide to finding unit information, 2019, via Library of Congress research guides website, Feb. 7, 2024"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Regimental histories were--and still are--a popular subgenre of Civil War history. They catalog a regiment's campaigns by describing its organization, personnel, training, battle action, and casualties. Although each author's style differs, regimental histories usually follow a lengthy, sentimentalist narrative. Not every regiment created its own history, but thousands did so, and together, they forged the first major attempt by veterans to control the commemoration of the war; Although the last confirmed Civil War veteran died in 1956, unit histories continued to be published in the twentieth- and twenty-first centuries. By that point, they belonged to the realm of professional historians, and they remain so to this day. Modern regimental histories are more analytical than their nineteenth century counterparts and attempt to incorporate social history into their narratives)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Orr, Timothy J. Regimental histories, via Virginia Center for Civil War Studies at Virginia Tech Essential Civil War curriculum website, Feb. 7, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(regimental histories, written by Civil War soldiers or their relatives or friends to document and commemorate the war experience. A regimental history is only one source of information about the activities of any particular unit, but it does offer the unique perspective of one soldier or group of soldiers)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Many roads to freedom: regimental histories and official records, via Monroe County Library System website, Feb. 7, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 6 (the early emergence of unit histories as a coherent genre which dominated the written response to war in the first five years of peace; this distinctive genre; unit histories were highly variable in quality, but were intent on illuminating the war record of battalions, regiments, brigades and divisions at the end of a period of sustained military activity) p. 7 (the constantly changing lists of officers highlighted a further purpose of regimental histories. There were barely any similarities between the officer compositions of each battalion in 1918 as compared to 1915. Therefore the book served the purpose of demonstrating the continuity of service of a unit through a description of its movements which few officers could have appreciated at the time of their service) p. 11 (battalion histories)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grieves, Keith. Making sense of the Great War: regimental histories, 1918-23, in Journal of the Society for Army Historical Research, spring 1991:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)4525157"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Burns, John C., Lt. Tropic lightning : a history of the 25th Infantry Division, 1969?", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Home > World War collections > Regimental histories (our collection of World War II regimental histories)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Bangor Public Library website, Feb. 7, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Regimental histories. USE subdivision Regimental histories under individual wars, e.g. United States--History--Civil War, 1861-1865--Regimental histories; World War, 1939-1945--Regimental histories; and names of individual military units)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Feb. 7, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. v (Organizational history is the record of a military unit's entire past; commanders at all echelons are charged with the responsibility of establishing and maintaining organizational history programs) p. 8 (unit histories; many excellent histories have been prepared and made available to unit members, former members, and the public at large. As may be expected, unit histories will vary considerably for a number of reasons. A division- or regimental-sized organization, with its greater resources, may be able to prepare a historical work of greater quality and appeal than a smaller unit) p. 9 (The largest collection of Army unit histories, is maintained by the U.S. Army Military History Institute; another large collection of published unit histories is located in the New York Public Library) p. 10 (Unit histories are not official publications of the Department of the Army and are not subject to review or revision by the Center of Military History) p. 11 (the more interesting and successful unit histories generally have devoted their contents to the following matters, approximately to the extent indicated: 1. The general history of the unit, its connection with a state or geographic area of any sort, its age, and some of its more important assignments and stations 10% 2. The accomplishments of the unit, particularly in battle; its battle honors and decorations; citations for gallantry and for successfully completing missions 50% 3. Noteworthy former members including Medal of Honor winners and other individuals decorated, prominent former commanders, and veterans who achieved distinction in military or civilian life 10% 4. Symbols, both tangible and intangible, such as distinctive insignia, the designations and nicknames given the outfit, battle streamers, unit ceremonies and customs, unit marches and songs, and unique or unusual trophies and historical properties 15% 5. Current mission and importance of unit, stressing the part it will play in battle, the need for team play, hard work, and courage in carrying out its mission 10% 6. Advantages to the individual soldier in being a member, including fraternal aspects, career advancement, etc. (Treatment here will greatly depend upon the type of organization, Regular Army, National Guard, Army Reserve, service, or combat.) 5%)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Organizational history, 1999, via U.S. Army Center of Military History website, Feb. 7, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Battalion histories"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Battalion histories"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b16", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b17"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Histories, Military unit"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b17", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Histories, Military unit"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b19", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b20"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Military unit histories"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b20", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Military unit histories"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b22", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b23"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Unit histories, Military"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b23", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Unit histories, Military"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b25", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b26"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Histories, Regimental"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b26", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Histories, Regimental"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b28", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b29"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Histories, Battalion"}}, {"@id": "_:nd2fb8ec05bd14536b420da863bc32325b29", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Histories, Battalion"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026013"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026014", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aArtistamps", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026014", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb1"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artistamps"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017027218"}, "madsrdf:hasReciprocalAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017027242"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb5"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb6"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb7"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb8"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb9"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb10"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb13"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established June 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Artwork created to resemble postage stamps, but not intended to defraud postal authorities or stamp collectors"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-02-08T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/nnmm"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-06-12T11:53:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artistamps"}}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page [1] (\"A complete collection of the artist postage stamp sheets produced by Fluxus artist Robert Watts was displayed at The Stamp Art Gallery\") (OCoLC)922651221"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Robert Watts : artistamps, 1961-1986, 1996:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Artistamps; UF Artist stamps; Artists' stamps; BT Cinderella materials (Philately); Mail art; scope: Here are entered works on artwork created to resemble postage stamps, but not intended to defraud postal authorities or stamp collectors)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, February 8, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "RBMS Controlled vocabularies: Genre terms, February 8, 2024;Thesaurus for graphic materials, February 8, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "notfound"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(An artistamp (a portmanteau of the words \"artist\" and \"stamp\") or artist's stamp is a postage stamp-like art form used to depict or commemorate any subject its creator chooses. Artistamps are not valid for postage, but they differ from forgeries or bogus Illegal stamps in that typically the creator has no intent to defraud postal authorities or stamp collectors. Artistamp creators often include their work on legitimate mail, alongside valid postage stamps, in order to decorate the envelope with their art. When so combined (and sometimes, less strictly speaking, even when not so) the artistamp may be considered part of the mail art genre. Artistamps are created as one-off works of art, in limited editions, or as mass-produced product. Artistamps have been produced as multiples of one design per sheet; a multitude of designs per page; as miniature sheets with a decorative or inclusive border; in booklets; or any combination/size/shape the artist chooses)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, viewed on January 4, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(UF artistamp; artist stamps; artist's stamps; note: Stamps that share the appearance of conventional postage stamps, including size, perforated edges, and in some cases imagery, but that are created and used by artists engaging in the practice of mail art)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Art & architecture thesaurus online, February 8, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artists' stamps"}}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artists' stamps"}}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artist stamps"}}, {"@id": "_:nbaf97da3e50b49529ed99e7e946c090cb14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artist stamps"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026014"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026015", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aPrice lists", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026015", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb1"}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Price lists"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017027257"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb5"}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb6"}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb8"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established June 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Lists of the prices of materials, merchandise, stock, products, or other items offered for sale."}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-06-12T11:53:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-02-16T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/nnmm"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Price lists"}}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(OCoLC)874809520"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Price list : Eastman Dry Plate and Film Company, 1886", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Price lists; UF price list; pricelist; lists, price; prices current; note: Statements or lists of the prevailing prices of merchandise, stock, or other items for sale)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Art & architecture thesaurus online, February 16, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Price lists; note: Use for lists of the prices of materials or products offered for sale)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "RBMS Controlled Vocabulary online, February 16, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Pricelists"}}, {"@id": "_:n08e5e970270a4d8a8ed5ccd2e3a174ccb9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Pricelists"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026015"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026018", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aComputer-generated literature", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026018", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b1"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer-generated literature"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026415"}, "madsrdf:hasNarrowerAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026269"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b5"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b6"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b7"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b9"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b12"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b15"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b18"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b21"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established June 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Literature generated or manipulated by computer algorithm and arranged as a sequence of words, signs, or symbols."}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-11-19T09:09:09"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-03-11T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer-generated literature"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "CIP galley (This is an anthology of computer-generated literature; there is a focus on the actual output of computer programs and systems, computer-assisted projects are not represented here) (DLC)2024004621"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Output, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "about (aims to carry out curatorial work related to the bibliography of computer-generated literature; mission is to establish computer-generated literature as a new specialization in book collecting)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://www.aleator.press/"}, "Aleator Press, website viewed March 11, 2024:"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Using AI to write an article. Increasingly, computers are used to write original text, and when it comes to describing current and newsworthy events, it can sometimes be hard to tell the difference between the computer's output and human prose)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://www.pcmag.com/encyclopedia/term/computer-generated-writing"}, "\"Computer-generated writing\" via PCMag Encyclopedia, viewed March 11, 2024"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "summary provided by publisher (From Literature to Biterature is based on the premise that in the foreseeable future computers will become capable of creating works of literature; argues that acknowledging biterature as a species of literature will involve adopting the same range of attitudes to computer authors (computhors) as to human ones and that it will be necessary to approach them as agents with internal states and creative intentions)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": [{"@id": "https://www.mqup.ca/from-literature-to-biterature-products-9780773542952.php?page_id=73&"}, "From Literature to Biterature: Lem, Turing, Darwin, and Explorations in Computer Literature, Philosophy of Mind, and Cultural Evolution, 2013, viewed on publisher website, March 11, 2024:"], "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer literature"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer literature"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artificial Intelligence generated literature"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Artificial Intelligence generated literature"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "AI generated literature"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "AI generated literature"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Generative literature"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Generative literature"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b21", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b22"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer-generated writing"}}, {"@id": "_:ndf0469283e424d8f88d4d56a698510e0b22", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Computer-generated writing"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026018"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026020", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aHorse ballets (Dance)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026020", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb1"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Horse ballets (Dance)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026112"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb5"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb6"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb7"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb8"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb11"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb14"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb17"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established October 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-03-21T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-18T07:01:46"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Horse ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "video abstract (For the first time since 1686, Versailles' Castle is recreating its \"Royal Carrousel\", a phenomenal & majestic performance of cavaliers & horses. Shot in the Versailles Castle Gardens, this unique experience highlights sumptuous cavaliers on Lusitano horses with fabulous costumes and baroque music, bringing you total immersion in the court of the Sun King!) (OCoLC) 1176455121"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: The Horses of the Sun [VR], 2013:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Horse ballet; NT Ballet, Horse Carousel (Horse ballet). Carrousel (Horse ballet), Equestrian ballet; BT Horsemanship)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LSCH, viewed March 20, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Horse ballet; Part equestrian exercise, part dramatic spectacle, a horse ballet (Ger., Rossballett, Karussell, Carossel; Fr. ballet de chevaux, carrousel, carroussel) was a form of choreographed movement performed by knights on horseback that enjoyed special popularity during the seventeenth century. Claude-Fran\u00e7ois M\u00e9n\u00e9strier considered the horses' steps as \"true dance figures, in harmony with the sounds and tunes which guide the horses during the ballet\" (1682))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "International Encyclopedia of Dance via Oxford Reference, viewed March 21, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb8", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb9"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carrousels (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb9", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carrousels (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb11", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb12"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ballets, Horse (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb12", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ballets, Horse (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb14", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb15"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carousels (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb15", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Carousels (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb17", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb18"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Equestrian ballets (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:nb502a567dfa64568a16cf3deb6eb28cdb18", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Equestrian ballets (Dance)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026020"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026022", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aLittle magazines", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026022", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb1"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Little magazines"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026093"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026139"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb5"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb6"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb7"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb8"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb9"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb10"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb11"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb12"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb13"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb14"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb15"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established June 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Periodicals usually dedicated primarily to literature with small circulations."}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-03-25T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-06-12T11:53:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Little magazines"}}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(little magazine: a literary usually noncommercial magazine that features works especially of writers who are not well-known)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam-Webster dictionary online, Mar. 25, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Little magazine, any of various small periodicals devoted to serious literary writings, usually avant-garde and noncommercial. They were published from about 1880 through much of the 20th century and flourished in the United States and England, though French writers (especially the Symbolist poets and critics, 1880--c. 1900) often had access to a similar type of publication and German literature of the 1920s was also indebted to them. The name signifies most of all a noncommercial manner of editing, managing, and financing. A little magazine usually begins with the object of publishing literary work of some artistic merit that is unacceptable to commercial magazines for any one or all of three reasons--the writer is unknown and therefore not a good risk; the work itself is unconventional or experimental in form; or it violates one of several popular notions of moral, social, or aesthetic behaviour. Foremost in the ranks of such magazines were two American periodicals, Poetry: a Magazine of Verse (founded 1912), especially in its early years under the vigorous guidance of Harriet Monroe, and the more erratic and often more sensational Little Review (1914-29) of Margaret Anderson)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Britannica online, Mar. 25, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "About (Characterized by their non-commercial attitudes and their penchant for the avant-garde and experimental, little magazines have continuously rebelled against established literary expression and theory by demonstrating an aggressive receptivity to new authors, new ideas, and new styles)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Little Magazine Collection at UW-Madison Memorial Library (Blog), viewed Mar. 25, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Little Magazines. \"Little magazines\" is a term referring to a set of literary periodicals published between roughly 1912 and 1939 that are characterized by their small readership, financial fragility, and artistic innovation. Little magazines were the nursery of several literary movements but are most closely connected to the birth of American modernism. They provided a place where writers of new, unusual, and often iconoclastic work could get into print. Those who published little magazines were amateurs and often artists themselves. Their goals were more likely to be artistic than commercial, a distinction borne out by their overwhelming tendency to be short-lived. They were especially important in creating and developing new American poetry and in consolidating and establishing ties between literary communities all over the world, but especially those in the urban centers of Chicago, New York City, London, and Paris)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford research encyclopedias online, Mar. 25, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Little magazines share certain characteristics: they are primarily literary, often experimental, and typically unfettered. Virtually all have a small circulation, and they are usually published, edited and financed by one person or by a small group of persons who are amateurs--that is to say, people without a profit motive)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Literary magazines: History: Little magazines, via Hamline University Bush Memorial Library research guides, viewed Mar. 25, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(little magazine: A periodical of limited circulation devoted to experimental or avant-garde poetry, fiction, essays, humor, photography, and art. Little magazines flourished in the United States, Britain, and France during the 1920s, but most disappeared before the beginning of World War II. Desktop publishing has given new life to this form of publication. According to Magazines for Libraries, over 5,000 little magazines are currently published in the United States, with some available online. For libraries, indexing can be a problem because many fall outside the mainstream press)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Reitz, Joan M. ODLIS : online dictionary for library and information science, Mar. 25, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(little magazine: a magazine, usually small in format and of limited circulation, that publishes literary works)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Dictionary.com, Mar. 25, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb12", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Little magazines. UF Small magazines. BT Periodicals)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "LCSH, Mar. 25, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb13", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(reprint of a little magazine edited by Margaret C. Anderson and originally published 1914-1929) (OCoLC)220434695"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Little review (Chicago, Ill.). Little review, 1967", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb14", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Little Magazines. A little magazine is a periodical dedicated primarily to serious literature, usually featuring poetry, short stories, serialized novels, and sometimes dramatic installments, as well as essays, reviews, and reader correspondence. They are called \"little\" (sometimes also \"small magazines\") owing to their cultivation of coterie contributorships and readerships in opposition to large commercial magazines whose content is strongly influenced by markets. As such, little magazines often prize experimental content that contravenes the public taste, which is one reason they were the primary vehicles for the development of modernism, its many movements, and their niche audiences. In many cases, the lack of a robust commercial apparatus meant that little magazines tended to be irregularly published and short lived, but with outsized contributions to literature and culture, such as the simultaneous serialization of James Joyce's Ulysses in The Little Review (1914-29) and The Egoist (1914-19) and T. S. Eliot's The Waste Land in The Dial (1920-29) and The Criterion (1922-39))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Routledge encyclopedia of modernism online, Mar. 25, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Small magazines"}}, {"@id": "_:n0e56cf213b4b413c8526677f5cc4e7fcb16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Small magazines"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026022"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026027", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $a\u010co\u010deks (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026027", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b1"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u010co\u010deks (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b5"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b6"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b7"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b9"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b12"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b15"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b18"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b21"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b24"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established October 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-04-08T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-11T14:07:29"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u010co\u010deks (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "\u010co\u010dek (\u010do\u010dek; romanization of the Serbian and Macedonian \u0447\u043e\u0447\u0435\u043a; in Bulgarian, kyuchek, romanization of \u043a\u044e\u0447\u0435\u043a [LC-ALA romanization: ki\ufe20u\ufe21chek]; Albanian, qyqek; Romania, sistemul; musical genre and dance that emerged in the Balkans during the early 19th century; originated from Ottoman military bands scattered throughout Serbia, Bulgaria, Macedonia, and Romania; today, features prominently in the repertoire of many Romani brass bands and especially popular among the Muslim Roma and Albanian populations of Kosovo, South Serbia, and Macedonia; dance is typically in 9/8 meter divided 2-2-2-3 or 2-2-3-2)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, April 8, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "container (one of selections on the \"Anthology\" disc is entitled \u010co\u010dek, while many others incorporate the work \"\u010do\u010dek\" in the title; note this is the [double] album of a Serbian Romani brass band) (DLC)2024618765"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Fejat & Neboj\u0161a Sejdi\u0107 Orchestra. Blues ; Anthology, 2018:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "page 951 (Romani brass bands and newly-composed folk music groups throughout Serbia have popularized \u010do\u010dek (in Turkish, k\u00fccek, \"belly-dance hip movement\"), a favorite dance among southern Serbian and Macedonian Romani) page 983 (\u010do\u010dek, the most popular dance music among Romani in Macedonia, exists in 2/4 (3+3+2 [based on 8 16th notes to a measure]) and 9/8 (2+2+2+3) meters) page 282 (in Bulgaria, the most typical Romani instrumental genre, kju\u010dek, can be found in two metric patterns, in duple meter, and in 9/8)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Garland encyclopedia of world music. Vol. 8, Europe, 2000:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\u0447\u043e\u0447\u043a = Chochek)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "ALA-LC Romanization tables (2012), online, viewed September 17, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Qyqeks (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Qyqeks (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ki\ufe20u\ufe21cheks (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Ki\ufe20u\ufe21cheks (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b15", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b16"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "K\u00fccekler (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b16", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "K\u00fccekler (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b18", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b19"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chocheki (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b19", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Chocheki (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b21", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b22"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kyucheks (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b22", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kyucheks (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b24", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b25"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sistemuls (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nce3941bafca442f0be1a78c07888d747b25", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Sistemuls (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026027"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026031", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aJuvenilia", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026031", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b1"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Juvenilia"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026415"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026952"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2017027218"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b5"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b6"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b7"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b8"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b9"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b10"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b11"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established October 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}, "madsrdf:note": "Works created during a person's childhood or youth."}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-11T14:07:29"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-04-12T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Juvenilia"}}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(work composed at the age of 14) t.p. (series: Inediti rarit\u00e0 e juvenilia / Felix Mendelssohn-Bartholody) (OCoLC)213434795"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Mendelssohn-Bartholdy, Felix. 4 fughe per pianoforte, 2007", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Juvenilia. SN Works produced by artists in their childhood or youth. See Children's art for works produced by children in general. UF Artists' early works; Children's works. BT Pictures)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Thesaurus for graphic materials, Apr. 12, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The collective term for those works written during an author's youth. Use of the term commonly implies that the faults of such writings are to be excused as the products of immaturity or lack of experience.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford Dictionary of Literary terms, online, viewed September 16, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(The term \"juvenilia\" describes the works of writers and artists that were produced during their youth. These works are often unpublished and only become public after the writer has already achieved renown. Although writers' works of childhood and youth were once viewed as inferior or unimportant, scholars are increasingly looking to juvenilia as a subject worthy of study)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "When they were young : juvenilia in the modern literature collection, via Washington University in St. Louis University Libraries website, Apr. 12, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(written between the ages of 15-21) (OCoLC)28797782"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Auden, W. H. Juvenilia : poems, 1922-1928, \u00a91994", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Juvenilia. SN Use for works produced by children and young people.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "RBMS controlled vocabulary, via LC Linked Data Service, Apr. 12, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd50249c460d44dcc902c2f2e2f284207b11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "home page (dedicated to the discussion and promotion of literary works by young writers; the ISLJ promotes the research, teaching, and dissemination of literary juvenilia; the society also supports the publication of juvenilia-related research through its journal, the Journal of Juvenilia Studies)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "International Society of Literary Juvenilia website, Apr. 12, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026031"}
