:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kakawin"}}, {"@id": "_:n486b3df78a04405e853dd4582c1e8d71b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 8 (kakawin; Old Javanese poetry in Indian meters)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Zoetmulder, P. Kalangwan, 1974:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n486b3df78a04405e853dd4582c1e8d71b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 5, etc. (Old Javanese poetry (kakawin) is a form of court poetry, essentially epic in nature, with distinctive metrical conventions and a somewhat artificial style)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Suarka, I. Telaah sastra kakawin, 2009:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n486b3df78a04405e853dd4582c1e8d71b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 3 (kakawin is a form of Old Javanese [Kawi] poetry with distinctive writing conventions and a distinctive narrative structure)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Kakawin Sena, 2020:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n486b3df78a04405e853dd4582c1e8d71b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(kakawin poetry, the classical epic poetry produced in the Indic courts of Java and Bali between the ninth and twentieth centuries)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Creese, H. Images of women and embodiment in kakawin literature, in Intersections: gender, history and culture in the Asian context, issue 5 (May 2001), viewed online July 5, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n486b3df78a04405e853dd4582c1e8d71b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n486b3df78a04405e853dd4582c1e8d71b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kawi"}}, {"@id": "_:n486b3df78a04405e853dd4582c1e8d71b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Kawi"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026059"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026060", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aWaltzes (Dance)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026060", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb1"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Waltzes (Dance)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026114"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb5"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb6"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb7"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb8"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb9"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb10"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established November 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-07-02T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-11-14T09:15:22"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Waltzes (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Waltz; A dance performed to music in triple time by couples who, almost embracing each other, swing round and round in the same direction with smooth and even steps, moving on as they gyrate"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford English dictionary online, viewed July 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Waltz, [Ger. Walzer; Fr. valse]; A German-Austrian turning dance in 3/4 or 3/8 time whose origins are not clear, though it bears similarities to the volta, the weller, and the l\u00e4ndler. (...) The name waltz appeared in the late 18th century and the dance itself gained widespread popularity through the ballroom waltz music of Lanner and the Strausses. Some authorities tried to ban it on account of the daringly close embrace required between male and female dancers. Its first stage appearance was in Vicente Mart\u00edn y Soler's opera La cosa rara (Vienna, 1786) and its first ballet showing was in Gardel's La Dansomanie (Paris, 1800). It features prominently in Swan Lake, Sleeping Beauty, and The Nutcracker, while individual works have been choreographed in celebration of its music and its romantic associations, including Nijinska's La Valse (mus. Ravel, Monte Carlo, 1929, also several later versions including one by Ashton, 1958), Balanchine's Liebeslieder Walzer (mus. Brahms, New York, 1960), and his Vienna Waltzes (mus. J. Strauss II, Leh\u00e1r, and R. Strauss, New York, 1977); also Mark Morris's New Love Song Waltzes (mus. Brahms, New York, 1982).)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford dictionary of dance (2nd. ed.) via Oxford reference, viewed July 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Waltz; This European couple dance in triple measure reached its zenith in the nineteenth century, in the capitals of Europe and the Americas. It captivated all strata of society with its heady, romantic rotations, a contrast to the precise protocol of the earlier minuet and the confining geometry of the cotillon and the contradance.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The International Encyclopedia of Dance, via Oxford Reference, viewed July 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "volume 1, title page (The waltz) page 7-21 ([includes pictorial and verbal description to dance the waltz]) page 22 (Diagram of entire operation of [the] waltz) (OCoLC)4615938"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The latest method, home instruction by mail, dancing courses ..., 1922:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Waltz; a ballroom dance in \u00b3/\u2084 time with strong accent on the first beat and a basic pattern of step-step-close)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam Webster dictionary online, viewed July 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n70ffab6a51724cc39247d22aeecbd27bb10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "pdf cover (A combination of curved steps and movements, so as to represent or describe the letter C.) page 4 (Description of the steps of The waltz C.; lady's part) pages 5-6 (gentleman's part) (OCoLC)5147948"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: The waltz C., \u00a91876:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026060"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026061", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aFoxtrots (Dance)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026061", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b1"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Foxtrots (Dance)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2023026114"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b5"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b6"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b7"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b9"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established November 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-11-14T09:15:22"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-07-02T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/cty"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Foxtrots (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Foxtrot; A modern dance, of American origin, consisting chiefly of alternating measures of long and short steps; also, a piece of music suitable as an accompaniment for the foxtrot.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford English Dictionary online, viewed July 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "volume 2, title page (The fox trot) pages 5-23 (includes textual and pictorial description of the foxtrot) page 4 (diagram of entire operation of the foxtrot) (OCoLC) 4615938"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: The latest method, home instruction by mail, dancing courses ..., 1922:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Foxtrot: a ballroom dance in duple time with slow walking steps, quick running steps, and the step of the two-step.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Merriam Webster dictionary online, viewed July 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(foxtrot; Popular ballroom dance of American origin danced to a march-like ragtime (slow or fast). From 1913 it was widely danced around the world.)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Oxford dictionary of dance (2nd. ed.) via Oxford reference, viewed July 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Fox trots (Dance)"}}, {"@id": "_:n2148b444f70f4adf816b30f63b94ac66b10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Fox trots (Dance)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026061"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026062", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aHahiwang", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026062", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b1"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Hahiwang"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026343"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026400"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b5"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b6"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b7"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b8"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b9"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established October 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-07-10T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wau"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-10-18T07:01:46"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Hahiwang"}}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(hahiwang is a type of oral literature of the people of Pesisir Barat Regency; this literature contains stories of life's suffering and romantic failure; hahiwang is divided into religious hahiwang and traditional hahiwang; religious hahiwang is used as a tool for preaching Islam, while traditional hahiwang is spoken at weddings and at ceremonies giving traditional titles; hahiwang is spoken when someone is alone or when they are separated from their family)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Indonesian Wikipedia, July 10, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(the hawihang tradition developed among 16 clans in Pesisir Barat Regency, Lampung; hawihang has been employed as a means for Lampung Sabaitin women to lament their oppression as a result of patriarchal domination in their community; it has, however, also been used by patriarchs as a means to broadcast religion and attract sympathizers in regional elections)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Gufron, A. Tradisi lisan hawihang pada perempuan di Pesisir Barat Lampung, via neliti.com website, July 10, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(hahiwang is a form of oral literature of the people of Lampung, specifically the indigenous people of the 16 Krui coastal clans; hahiwang is a form of poetry relating sad events; it can be divided into two types, those relating the sufferings of an individual's life and those dealing with failed romance)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hahiwang, via Scribd website, July 10, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(hahiwang is a type of oral literature among the indigenous communities of 16 clans in Pesisir Barat Regency; hahiwang can be divided into two types, one relating stories of life's suffering and one lamenting romantic failure; both types are sung)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Hahiwang, tradisi lisan Kabupaten Pesisir Barat, via BPKIX website, July 10, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nb4d55983bb7b4ece85b0902060b66448b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a collection of hahiwang) p. 3 (hahiwang is a type of poetry that contains sad stories or narratives, whether experienced by one individual or many; these themes or stories of sadness can be likened to those in the works Romeo and Juliet and Majnun Laila)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Hahiwang, 2020", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026062"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026063", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aParanormal romance fiction", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026063", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db1"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Paranormal romance fiction"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026469"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026516"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db5"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db6"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db7"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db8"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db9"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db10"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db11"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established February 2025.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2026-01-25T15:32:09"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-07-15T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/csds"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Paranormal romance fiction"}}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(a novel) p. 4 of cover (paranormal romance) (OCoLC)960711696"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: Andrews, I. Wildfire, 2017", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 222 (\"Paranormal romances... typically include nonhuman characters as romantic leads. The paranormal elements in the novel augment the romantic relationship and help further and deepen the story line.\") (OCoLC)1050140720"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wyatt, N. The readers' advisory guide to genre fiction, 2019:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(\"Paranormal romance is where the alpha males really tend to come out and play. While many romances feature a strong, dominant hero who surrenders to his need for the heroine, this theme is writ large when the hero is a centuries-old magical creature or an actual alpha wolf. While most romances have a strong heroine (or at least a heroine who comes to find her strength during the story), this is especially true in paranormal romances, where the kick-butt heroine is a mainstay and the struggle becomes how to balance her supernatural nature or responsibilities with a desire for love\") (OCoLC)882463851"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "McArdle, M. M. The readers' advisory guide to genre blends, 2015", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(short fiction and novellas) (OCoLC)83601611"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Best new paranormal romance, \u00a92007", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(mission statement: \"to support and promote all authors of the paranormal romance genre and their works\")"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Paranormal Romance Guild website, viewed Sept. 2, 2024", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db10", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "p. 143 (\"In paranormal romance, each novel ends with the HEA of one or more couples, therefore the romantic elements consistently play a major role in the narrative; the point of view alternates between the hero and heroine, and occasionally other characters\") (OCoLC)1184234563"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "The Routledge research companion to popular romance fiction, 2021:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n03e820af5ab94b1aa1ec6cb5a9154f5db11", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Paranormal Romance. The Paranormal Romance genre is defined by the Romance Writers of America as \"Romance novels in which the future, a fantasy world, or paranormal happenings are an integral part of the plot\" (RWA, \"Romance Subgenres\"). This definition opens this subgenre up for any romance novel that has elements that are paranormal in nature. This includes but is not limited to vampires, faeries, werewolves, ghosts, time travel, locations on other planets, and so forth; has roots in early literature with works such as Bram Stoker's Dracula and other Gothic novels, but the Paranormal Romance usually makes the vampire or other paranormal entity the hero or heroine rather than the villain; the modern Paranormal Romance started in the 1970s with novels by Victoria Holt and Phyllis A. Whitney and continued with authors such as Jayne Castle, Jude Deveraux, and Johanna Lindsey)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Encyclopedia of romance fiction, 2018, via Gale eBooks, viewed Sept. 2, 2020", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026063"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026064", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aNaigryshi (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026064", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab1"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Naigryshi (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026753"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026809"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab5"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab6"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab7"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab8"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab9"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab10"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab13"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established November 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-11-14T09:15:22"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-07-18T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wimtmla"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Naigryshi (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Part II, Self and Other: How the Acorn took root (\"Kamarinskaya\" is one of a well-defined group of Russian folk naigrishi, or instrumental dance tunes. These typically consist of a short (often three-measure) phrase repeated ad infinitum as the basis and framework for an open-ended series of extemporized variations)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Taruskin, Richard, Defining Russia Musically, 1997:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (Ukrainskie narodnye naigryshi = Ukrainian folk naigryshi)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat.: V. Gut\ufe20s\ufe21al, Ukrainskie narodnye naigryshi, 1986:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Naigrysh; a folk melody performed on a musical instrument, sometimes in an ensemble with other instruments... Typically, the term Naigrysh is applied to folk dance and round dance melodies performed on wind instruments, string instruments and harmonicas)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Russian Wikipedia, December 29, 2022", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under N (Naigrysh - a folk instrumental melody, sometimes with accompaniment; commonly used in relation to music accompanying dances)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Great Russian Encyclopedia WWW site, viewed December 29, 2022:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Russian Federation (Russ. Rossiiskaya Federatsiya; Russia); Bibliography (L. Barag and others, eds.: Bashkirskoye narodnoye tvorchestvo: pesni i naigr\u00efshi [Bashkir folk tradition: songs and instrumental pieces] (Ufa, 1983))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, July 18, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Naigr\u00efshi (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Naigr\u00efshi (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab13", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab14"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Naigryshes (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nd0aba4be961040b2880158f7f0c11d9ab14", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Naigryshes (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026064"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026065", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aKoli\ufe20a\ufe21dy (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026065", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb1"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Koli\ufe20a\ufe21dy (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026695"}, {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026795"}], "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb5"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb6"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb7"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb8"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": [{"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb9"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb12"}], "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established November 2024.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-07-18T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wimtmla"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-11-14T09:15:22"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Koli\ufe20a\ufe21dy (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under Russian Federation. II, Traditional music. ii, Song. b, Calendar songs and 'calls' (They are divided into genres, according to the association of the songs with the calendar: winter\u202fkolyad\u00ef\u202f(carols), called variously\u202fkolyadki)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Grove music online, July 18, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Video @ Wesleyan; Winter Songs: Koliada and Music From the Carpathian Mountains (The Koliada is a winter ritual that coincides with Christmas, but pre-dates that holiday in some parts of Eastern Europe. High in the Carpathian Mountains, in the Hutsul villages where the oldest songs are preserved, Koliada is considered to be the most important event of the year. Groups of male singers visit each family and sing ancient songs that are believed to hold special powers. According to tradition, spring and the harvest will not come unless the songs are sung in every household)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wesleyan University WWW site, July 18, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "title page (Uglichskie narodnye pesni = Uglichsky folk songs [from the Uglichsky district of the Yaroslavl Oblast in Russia]) (DLC)unk85067768"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Zemt\ufe20s\ufe21ovski\u012d, I.I. Uglichskie narodnye pesni, c1974.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "(Koliadka; traditional songs usually sung in Eastern Slavic, Central European and Eastern European countries during the Christmas holiday season)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, October, 3, 2023:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb9", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb10"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Koli\ufe20a\ufe21das (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb10", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Koli\ufe20a\ufe21das (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb12", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb13"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Koli\ufe20a\ufe21dki (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:n8d1758d4eaeb448381d2ad032a8a42abb13", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Koli\ufe20a\ufe21dki (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026065"}
{"@context": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/context.json", "@graph": [{"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026066", "@type": ["madsrdf:Authority", "madsrdf:GenreForm"], "bflc:marcKey": "155  $aFuture funk (Music)", "identifiers:lccn": "gf2024026066", "madsrdf:adminMetadata": [{"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b1"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b2"}], "madsrdf:authoritativeLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Future funk (Music)"}, "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b3"}]}, "madsrdf:hasBroaderAuthority": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014027153"}, "madsrdf:hasSource": [{"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b5"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b6"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b7"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b8"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b9"}], "madsrdf:hasVariant": {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b10"}, "madsrdf:historyNote": "[Established February 2025.]", "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSCollection": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/collection_LCGFT_General"}, "madsrdf:isMemberOfMADSScheme": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms"}}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b1", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2025-02-04T11:18:26"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/dlc"}, "ri:recordStatus": "revised"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b2", "@type": "ri:RecordInfo", "ri:languageOfCataloging": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/iso639-2/eng"}, "ri:recordChangeDate": {"@type": "xsd:dateTime", "@value": "2024-07-19T00:00:00"}, "ri:recordContentSource": {"@id": "http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/organizations/wimtmla"}, "ri:recordStatus": "new"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b3", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Future funk (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b5", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "insert (Future funk)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Work cat: Neoncity delights!, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b6", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Future Funk (Once seen as Vaporwave's happy-go-lucky sibling, Future Funk (also known as Vaporboogie); Future Funk visual aesthetics are usually just a looped GIF of a classic anime set to the track; but it's also not unusual to find [visual] aesthetic cues from Vaporwave, Synthwave, and Lo-Fi either; Future Funk takes musical cues from Vaporwave, French House (which gives it some very subtle connections to the poppier entries into the Synthwave genre), Nu-Disco, and Synth Funk to create fun, happy tracks to dance to, taking samples of old funk tracks from the 70's and 80's and repurposing them in a manner similar to Vaporwave (to the point where Future Funk will often just be lumped in with the genre despite the very clear differences in musical philosophy between Future Funk and Vaporwave))"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Aesthetics wiki WWW site, October 4, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b7", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Vaporwave; Offshoots and subgenres (Future funk; a French house-inspired offshoot that expands upon the disco and house elements of vaporwave. It involves much of the same visual imagery drawn from 1980s anime; Musically, future funk is produced in the same sample-based manner as vaporwave, albeit with a more upbeat approach. Most of the music samples are drawn from Japanese city pop records from the 1980s, and the genre has led to an increased exposure of city pop music to western audiences; Some of the most popular future funk artists include Macross 82-99, who pioneered the genre with his Sailorwave album series in 2013; other artists [...] include Skylar Spence (aka Saint Pepsi), Tsundere Valley, Ducat, Yung Bae, and Night Tempo)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Wikipedia, July 19, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b8", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationSource": "Macross 82-99. A million miles away, 2024.", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b9", "@type": "madsrdf:Source", "madsrdf:citationNote": {"@language": "en", "@value": "under, Sommet, Moritz. Intermediality and the discursive construction of popular music genres: the case of \"Japanese City Pop,\" 2017, p. 17 (The emergence of Future Funk in 2015, a more upbeat Vaporwave offshoot, marked a shift towards an unironic nostalgic appreciation of Japanese music. Future Funk too makes extensive use of City Pop samples, but aims to adapt these samples to the sound of modern house and lounge music instead of adding distortion effects)"}, "madsrdf:citationSource": "Swiss open access repository WWW site, July 19, 2024:", "madsrdf:citationStatus": "found"}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b10", "@type": ["madsrdf:GenreForm", "madsrdf:Variant"], "madsrdf:elementList": {"@list": [{"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b11"}]}, "madsrdf:variantLabel": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Vaporboogie (Music)"}}, {"@id": "_:nfc68431aa0c64c188312aeaff30614c5b11", "@type": "madsrdf:GenreFormElement", "madsrdf:elementValue": {"@language": "en", "@value": "Vaporboogie (Music)"}}], "@id": "/authorities/genreForms/gf2024026066"}
